      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS. 1</p>
        <p>r PrterrEcrrvx eXRMISSION.—" Does your wife open your letters?" "No ; I told her she might." "On yes, I oft en have to sit on the jury," said the Supreme Court Judge, as he administered a reprimand. " The air in Ireland is very excellent and healthy." " Madam " said Swift, "don't say so in England, for if you do they will certainly tax it." LEARN modesty from the burglar. Does it ever bother him who may get the credit for his work 7 Toy, who is taken to the seaside, and sees a steamer for the first time, exclaims—" Look, ma I there's a railway engine having a bathe." VICTOR HUCO wrote to a "fraternal banquet" of I working men that he could not be present, but sent them "a shake of his hand from the battom of his heart." Two broomsellers were rivals. One said to the</p>
        <p>other—" How can you undersell me for I steal the stuff, and make my brooms myself ?'' "Why," replied the other, "I steal them ready made." "Mamma," said Harry, "what's the difference aetween goose and geese ?" "Why, don't you know ?" interposed four-year-old Annie. " One geese is goose, and a whole lot of gooses is geese." FARMER. GREEN (to boy caught stealing his apples) : "Welt, sir, what have you to say for yourself ?" Boy (with pockets full) : "Please sir may I have some apples ?" "SMALL thanks to you," said a plaintiff to one af his witnesses, "for what you said in this case." " Ah, sir" replied the latter, "but just think of what I didn't say." A YANKEE pedlar in his cart overtaking another, asked What he was carrying. "Drugs," was the reply. "Go ahead," said the former; "I carry tombstones." _</p>
        <p>.. . MANAGIKG A 11179BAND.—lle : "There isn't snongh on this breksizfa.c.-table to feed a canary bird." She : "I know it, my dear ; but there are 3everal things I want you to order from the shops, and I know you'll forget/all about it unless you Leave the house hungry." A SOCIAL CATECIIISSI "And what do you mean by a wise man ?" "One who can do without the svcrld." "And by a fool?" "One who fancies that the world cannot be without him." NissrisEss : "I never saw such a fearful-looking kitchen. How do you manage to accumulate so many dirty dishes?" Cook: "Sure, mum, the young ladies was just after showing me howithey roasts a potato at their cooking schools." "JksiNix," said he, "1 snail go to your father and ask his consent at once." Wait, George. Don't be impatient, said dennie ; " wait until after _ . . . _</p>
        <p>the first, wlien my 'clressmaker's.bill comes in. He will be more wing to part with me then."</p>
        <p>Mss. TO3IPKYNS : "George, mother is very ill.* rompkyns : "Is she? Well, 1 daresay she'll die." Mrs. Tompkyna ; "What makes you think so?" Tompkyns : "Oh, I don't know. Because I'm an optimist by nature, I suppose." TEACHER (to youngster who had been mis- behaving) : "Now, Bobby, don't you know where bad boys go?" "Yes, ma'am; over their mother's knee." "What can be the matter with Arthur dear? He mnpes so, he must be ill." "I don't think it is as serious as that, mamma. It's his whiskers." "His whiskers? Why the boy hasn't got any!" That is just it, mamma, He wishes he had them, don't you know." "THEY say poor Briggs, the teetotaler, died of hard drink," said Dodson. " You astonish me! How did he acquire the habit?" "It was very sudden. A cake of ice fell on him." " Youit honour and gentlemen of the jury, I acknowledge the reference of counsel of the other side to my grey hair. .2.1 y hair is grey, and it will continue to be grey as long as I live. The hair of that gentleman in black, and will continue to be black as long as he dyes." "Does our talk disturb you?" said one of a com- pany of talkative ladies to an old gentleman sitting in a railway Carriage the other afternoon. "No, ma'am," was the naive reply, "I've been married nigh on to forty years." TOMMY—" Mr. Wilkinson, ain't you shaped just like other men ?" Mr. Wilkinson—" I suppose so, Tommy, Why?" Tommy—" Papa says you ain't exactly square, and Belle Bays you seem to be always round.'" THRVIC boys were comparing notes as to how far they were on in the catechism. The first said, "I am in an estate of sin and misery;" the second said, " I'm at eflectual calling," but the third said, "I am-far farther than that, for Tm past redemp- tion !" - "ETHEL," said the teacher, "who do the students say supported the world on his shoulders?' " Atlas." " You're quite right," said the teacher, "Atlas stipported the world. Now, who supported Atlas 2" "I suppose," said Ethel softly—"l an pose be married a rich wife." Tue young father stood over the cradle, mitt.' cally 'inspecting his first-born. "Of course it's a healthy, well-formed baby, Lucy," he said to his wife, "and will be a credit to the family when it grows older, but its awful homely just now." "Dear little thing !" cooed a neighbour, who came In a few momenta later, "It's the very mage of its papa." A TEACHER was exorcising his scholars upon words having the same sound, but diff -rent mean- ing, as heir, hair, hare. After explaining the different terms and naming the last one as an animal, and spelling the word for distinction, hare, asked " What is a hare!' The clever boy of the class immediately stretched out his hand and said, " Please, sir, it's a rabbit."</p>
        <p>THIS mo.n was unconscious, breathing heavily. and his half-closed eyes had a glassy stare. " Yes, ' said the physician who had been hastily called in "he must have blown out the gas." The man on the coach opene4 his eyes and looked with stern a reproach at the doctor. "No I" he said, feebly, "I wish it to be clearly understood that I did not blow out the gas. I blew out the flame !" "I THINK it is too bad," said Mrs. Velox to her spouse, "that I have to slave all day Sunday jest the same as any other day in the week, while you loaf about the house and do nothing but smoke and read." "You forget, my dear, responded Velox, "what the Scripture says, The Sabbath was made for man 1' " ONn night Jones came home very late, and found his wife evidently prepared to administer a Caudle lecture. Instead of going to bed, he took a seat, and resting his elbows on his knees, seemed absorbed in grief, sighing heavily, and uttering such exclamations as, "Poor Smith, poor fellow.' Mrs. Jones, moved by curiosity, eaid sharply, " What's the matter with Smith?" " Ah," said Jones, "his wife is giving him fits just now." Mrs. Jones let her husband oil that time. "You see, mum," said the tramp, "I do oat make my request for viands from any selfish desire to appease my own sordid hunger. But I wished</p>
        <p>to demonstrate, mum, that that woman next door</p>
        <p>was in error. I tohl her that I knew she mis- judged you when she said you wuz h tattling old hen, that your eyes crossed each other at right angles, and that you were so stingy you charged your b9.by for its board." Then the tramp eat down at the dining room table while his hostess . .</p>
        <p>_ ran out to the rear of the house and made faces over the back fenqe.</p>
        <p>A LAWYER, trying to save his client by throwing suspicion on a witnoss in the case, in the course of cross-examination said : "You have admitted that you were at the prisoner's house every night during th's time?' " Yes, air," replied the witness. "Were you and he interested in any business together?" "Yes," answered .the man, ...</p>
        <p>unhesitatioily; "yes." " 1 Now will you be good enough to tell us how and to what extent, and what the nature of this bustne.s was in which you and he were interested ?" "Well, I have no objection to telling," was the reply. "I was courting his daughter." Sue came out of a butcher's shop. Her head was erect and her step queenly. In her delicate white hands she held a package; it was brown paper; flimsy and rotten, and as the snowflakes rested upon it after their long and weary flight, they melted under her warm gaze and saturated the paper. The sausage being reetleea, crawled out lick after link and spread themselves Over the ground, but never offered to run away. This high-stepping lady stooped to conquer those sausage, and as she shoved them into her pocket a crimson blush suffused her cheek, and three links huLg out to tell the tale. The high moral of this story iB—Always go to market with a basket and nen the frisky saueaze</p>
