      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>"CAPITAL" CHAT.</head>
        <p>"CAPITAL" CHAT.</p>
        <p>The papers were wrong in describing Captain Gammen as one of the few surviving officers of die Peninsular war. He was really the last survivior, having been left alone by the death of the cen- tenarian Lieut.'Bayly at Bath two years ago. The veterans have died off rapidly during the last few veers. Three years ago General Whicbcote, the Earl ot Albemarle, Colonel Hewett and Colonel Jackson all could say that they had fought at Waterloo. They all expired within a very few months of each other.</p>
        <p>M. Zola's good-natured courtesy when speaking of all things English has been very noticeable, but it is nothing put on for the occasion, for the stern realist in private life is as gallant as a courtier.—On one ,occition, now some) ears ago, a certain English novelist whom I will call Brown, because it is not his name, renewed his acquaintanceship with the sage of Medan, and a friend at his elbow prompted him by saying: "Since you met, Brown has become known as the English Zola" M.. Zola smiled as he shook his old friend's hand and said : " Not only that, but they have come to call me the French Brown;. so you see we are brothers in art."</p>
        <p>A familiar figure will disappear from the Stock Exchange with Mr. Charles. Cleve, who has an- nounced his intention to retire from business at the end pf this month. The white-aired old man,whose sharp features gain an additional touch of keenness from the fact that he is clean-shaven, is as well- known in the City as the Bank of England itself. Mr. Cleve is a character." He is a Dutchman, and quite the reverse of the popular notion of the native of Holland. for !leis as alert as any man in the American market, it which his catch-praise, " Tat you vant to do ? " has passed into a current locution.</p>
        <p>It is so rarely that the speeches of Chancery barristers are of sufficient interest for ordinary newspaper readers, that the peroration of Sir H. Davey, the new Lord Justice, has escaped reporting. The suit was one in which the famous inventor of a new burner sought to injunct, for ,infringement, another equally eminent inventor who, in turn, alleged that his idea had been interfered with. "My lord," said the eminent Q.C., "1 cannot sit down without saying this, that I have heard a very well-known saying translated into truly Johnsonian language in these words: Reflections from that most woeful and domestic article of the kitchen come with an ill grace from its more sable com- panion of the acullery. In other words, the pot is calling the kettle black.'" -</p>
        <p>Admiral Sir Algernon Heneage, who is in com- mand at the Nore, whither the Lords of the Admir- alty have just gone on their tour of inspection, is a naval officer of very aristocratic connections in- deed. with some idiosyncrasies which have shut out a naturally kindly man from the good opinion of his subordinates. No admiral has been respon- sible for a greater number of eccentric orders on small points of dress and etiquette, both in the wardroom and on the lower deck. At one time, in- deed, the fact of his holding a command was con- sidered quite a sufficient explanation of the excep- tional number of desertions which were sure to fol- low. Admiral Heneage has a craza for keeping men perpetually at sail drill, while leaving rifle practice pretty well neglected. His chief achievements in this direction were during his recent period as com- mander in chief on the Pacific Station.</p>
        <p>Tuesday was Thomas Sydney Cooper's nine- tieth birthday. He is a splendid specimen of that old workman who has retained undimmed all the enthusiasm for his art and has lost very little of his dexterity. He lives at Vernon Holme, a charming louse not many miles from Canterbury, where he still spends much time in his studio and occasion- ally talks of what he means to do in the way of pictures. The veteran cattle painter has not been much in London since forty years ago, when visi- tors on Show Sunday at his son's house in Bays- water were astonished and delighted to be re- ceived by the veteran Academician. Sydney Cooper laid the foundation of his fame with his "Cattle Reposing" nearly fifty years ago. He has never painted a better picture than the bull called "King of the Meadows," so mysteriously stolen a few years ago. In stockyard, pen, and pasture he reigns supreme.</p>
        <p>Mr. James Laing, the Sunderland shiobuilde:. has just been celebrating the jubilee of his connection with the famous Wearside firm. When he took over the management of the great shipbuililing yard the era of iron ships was only dawning. The older builders declined to prepare thejr yards for the new manufacture. Mr. James Laing was wiser, and his firm not only kept their own but led the way in what is now the staple industry of the port. Mr. Laing is a squire as well as a shipbuilder. Eight years ago he purchased the extensive property of Etal Manor in the Cheviots, where he had the late Lady Waterford for a neighbour. At Etal he has good fishing.in the Till. Se is popular with his workpeople at Sunderland, is a fresh-looking strongly-built man of seventy, was once an active Liberal, but has of late years ranked himself as a Liberal Unionist. Mr. Laing has now practically re- tired from the business of the firm.</p>
        <p>Signor Paolo Tosti, who, as readers of the "Court Circular" will have perceived, has been lately invited to Balmoral to ,sing and play to the Queen, has for years been a favourite with our Royal Family ;it was through the influence of the late Duchess of Cambridge, whom he used to visit every night to play over the old operas, that Signor Tosti was appointed teacher of singing to the Queen and her children. To the outside world the clever Italian is best known as the composer of "Forever and for ever," and "Bid Me Good-bye," but with several typical Italian ballads he is as much a favourite in his native country as in that of his adoption. He is now settled down over here for good in the artistic neighbourhood of St. John's Wood. He has a charming wife, who is herself a musician. He is an inveterate smoker, and a skil- ful amateur carpenter. While he receives nowa- days something like £4OO or £5OO fora song, he can look back on the time, not so very far distant,when a couple of sovereigns was all that he could com- mand.</p>
        <p>John Sylvanns Tanner, who has just met his fate at the Central Criminal Court—where he was justly sentenced for many fraudulent acts—figured in recent years in many a r6le. He was well known in the stage, musical, and Fleet-street world ; he has been in his day a newspaper proprietor, and actor, has "circussed" clowns and equestrians at provincial shows, and "bossed" many a theatrical and °pored?. troupe. Though not in the higher sense of the term what may be called an educated man, lie was smart with his pen, and in later days he was chiefly engaged in puffing in local journals (under the name of " Silvestris ") the land sales of an enterprising firm of London auctioneers. In his varied and chequered career he has frequently known both riches and poverty. When in possession of the former he was kind and hospitable to a degree, for his nature was a good one, and his reduction to extremities has driven him to the commission of what has resulted in his receiving 18 months's bard.</p>
        <p>Mr. Thomas Hawksley, F.R.S., one of the most eminent and best-known civil engineers of the present day, died on Saturday. He was born at Nottingham in 1807, and at about 1830 was appointed to construct waterworks in his native town. In 1852 be removed to London:where he had stood for a long period at the head of that branch .of the profession having to do with water and gas supvly, and with drainage and hydraulic works generally. His practice was enormous. He himself was accustomed to say that he had constructed above 150 waterworks, many of the largest charac- ter; and that there were no important towns in Great Britain, and, indeed, very few great cities in the civilised word, in regard to which he had not been professionally consulted in some way or other.</p>
        <p>Mr. Hawksley had the merit of being the first to suggest and to carry into practice the system of "constant service "in water supply. This system has been one of the greatest benefits ever conferred on urban populations, as it has combined the most free and ample supply with the almost perfect repression of waste, and with greatly improved sanitary conditions. In this country he was the first to adopt, for practical urea, certain new hydraulic principles discovered by French physi- cists, the results of which, as applied by bim to a proposed colossal culvert for a Midland town, somewhat astonished his professional brethren.</p>
        <p>CADBURY'S COCOA has. in a remarkable degree, those natural elements of sustenance which give the system endur• ance and hardihood, with a steady action that renders it a m at setntable and rell:ble lx.vtrrge."—Lholh.</p>
        <p>Sister Gertrude, a nun in the Loretto convent at Dub- lin, has written a drama entitled " Nemesius." It is dedicated to the memory of the martyrs of the cata- combs, and has been performed at Loretto Abbey. Throughout the East of Europe and in Roumania there has lately been organised a system of lady commercial travellers, whose mission it is to supply wedding trous- seaux, layettes, mourning outfits, and other goods. These ladies hail from Paris, and carry with them speci- mens and samples from the first French houses. Among the various trades ait I professious nowadays followed by English women, money-lending does not figure. It has been left to Italy to lead the way in this business, At Milan a woman, by name Signora Colum- ba, has been arrested for habitual usury, having appar- ently done a brisk trade with necessitous young men. Sara Bernhardt is terribly extravagant, and never out of debt. She was born at Havre in 1844. Her mother was a Dutch Jewess, and her father a French barrister. Besides being an actress the Divine Sara is a painter, a sculptor, and a journalist, and yet those who call themselves her friends, declare she will die a pauper._ . .</p>
        <p>Sara J. Lippincott—better known by her peu-name "Grace Greenwood "—is now 70 years old and lives in Washington. She was the first woman newspaper cor- respondent in that city. She began her journalistic labours there as long ago as 1850, and although she has published many columns of poems, travels and stories her literary reputation rests chiefly on her journalistic work.</p>
        <p>Miss Empsie Bowman, the famous little actress, is eleven years of age, and she is an actress in every sense of the word—not a mere reciter. She made a start between the age of four and five, when she danced a hornpipe at the Stratlord Theatre. She has a re- markable memory, and masters her part in a very short time.</p>
        <p>Tea Drinking. We drink more tea than our parents ; we take it oftener, stronger, and of coarser quality. The results are less obvious than those of alcoholic intoxication, but not less serious; and in truth the time may not be far distant, when the earnest disciples of the new temper- ance will plead with us with tears in their eyes : "Give up the accursed tea, and take 5....c0c0a, or even to beer." —" The Hospital." Laundry "Notions."</p>
        <p>Among the novelties- to be shown at the Laundry Exhibition, at the Agricultural Hall, Islington, are an electrical ironin,, apparatus, a steam starching machine, an American arrangement. which, it is claimed, removes used soapsuds from the tubs, and returns the water cleansed and the soap extracted, so that both may be employed over again, and a " torrent" washer which pours a continuous , volume of water upon the clothes while being washed. All the operations of the industry are to be illustrated. A working laundry enables the visitor to follow every process from st4rt to finish, with one exception—the important operation by which shirts are dcprived of their buttons,</p>
        <p>Scotch Brose.</p>
        <p>A Scotch Brose is A-iftil-,;-" &amp;;si-C-u-red dish. It calls for three cupfuls of the l!quor in which a leg of mutton or a piece of beef has been boiled, or the same amount of stock; a cupful of oitmeal, and an even teaspoonful of salt in case the stock has not been salted. Let the oat- meal cook steadily in an earthen pipkin in the broth for one hour' and Berns. Oatmeal, when served in this way for breakfast, may bq cooked the night before and steamed in the morning. Almost any grain, such as rice, hominy, or barley is excellent cooked in stock in this manner, especially when it is served with a dish of meat, and not as a breakfast porridge with milk.</p>
        <p>AtioNCH 4 THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 30, 1F93. LETTER.</p>
        <p>PARTED BY FATE;' OR</p>
