      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>ACTOR AND THE RUNCORN FRIED FISH SHOP.</head>
        <p>THE ACTOR AND THE RUNCORN FRIED FISH SHOP.</p>
        <p>On Monday at Runcorn Petty Sessions, before Major Hazlehurst and Mr. James Handley, Colin Harford, a well dressed young man, was charged, in custody, with assaulting Mary Shallcross, wife of W. Shallcross, refreshment housekeeper,of Duke- at. Be was further charged with damaging crockery to the extent of 10s. Mrs. Shallcross said the defendant was an actor, and last week was performing in the " Red Barn "at the Theatre Royal. About five minutes past 11 on Saturday night, the prisoner entered the shop and ordered " three fishes and three potatoes." • The daughter served him with one fish short, stating they had no more, and the prisoner said if he were not served with the fish he would clear the shoo in five minutes. Without more ado, he swept the counter with his walk- ing stick, and a large number of saucers and bottles were smashed. He then attempted to go into the kitchen but was prevented and put in the street. He returned minus his coat and with his sleeves rolled up, knocked Mrs. Shallcross against the boiler, and whirling his stick round took possession of the place until P.C. Gregory arrived and secured him. He was like a mad man. Shallcross corrobnrated his wife's statement. P.C. Gregory deposed that when he arrived at the shop, the prisoner was behind the counter. He attempted to strike him (witness) but he threw him on his back and hand- cuffed him. A crowd of 150 p•rsons had assembled. The prisoner was drunk. The p isoner, in his defence, said it was a very degrading position fer him to be ' placed in, and stated that the original grievance had been standing over for four years. When this woman made an accusation against his wife-. The Magistrates' Clerk : You cannot go into four years ago. The Prisoner: But it has a bearing upon the case. He denied that he had assaulted the woman. On Saturday night he had offered through his friends to pay for the damage. Edward Allen, another member of the theatri- cal company, gave evidence that there was no assault, and that the breaking of the articles was,to some extent, accidental. The bench ordered the prisoner to pay 305., including costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>~'PORTING "PROPHECIES" ANALYSED,</head>
        <p>~'PORTING "PROPHECIES" ANALYSED,</p>
        <p>Many of the sporting newspapers prlds themselves on the cleverness with which their prophet or prophets can predict the winners of forthcoming horse races, and it is surprising how many people can be gulled in placing their coins, on the strength of the predictions. Some- thing in the shape of an eye-opener, as to the worth of them, has just been given by the Rev. J. W. Horsley, for many years chaplain of Clerkenwell Gaol, who has been studying seven leading sporting papers and their prophecies. The result in eight races is that out of seventy-nine prophecies five were right and seventy-four wrong. The" Sportsman" named thirteen horses and was right once ; the " Sporting Life" named eight and was right once ; the " Field " named twelve, but never hit the mark ; the "County Gentleman " had ten choices, all ineffectual ; "Land and Water " was right twice out of nine attempts ; the " Sporting Times " be- lieved in twelve animals (not including the prophet),but was basely deceived by all ; and the " Licensed Vic- tualers' Gazette " could not be contented with less than fifteen winners for the eight races, but only once was right. Mr. Horsley adds : " I trust ludicrously blind faith placed by tens of thousands in such prophecies may receive a shock—to the advantage of their intel- lect, their pocket, and their morals."</p>
        <p>Dr. I. N. Love, of St. Louis, Missouri, U.S., recently read a paper on a mw remedy for obesity,which is called phytoline. It is prepared from the berries of the phytolacca decandra after they have been touched by the early frost.</p>
      </div>
