      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>rnE MYSTERY OF BLACK-TOR LIGHTHOUSE'</head>
        <p>rnE MYSTERY OF BLACK-TOR LIGHTHOUSE'</p>
        <p>Br LAURA JEAN LIEBEY, Author of "lone, the Pride of the Mill," &amp;c., &amp;o.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER I. TUE DEAITTIFUL, MYSTERIOUS STRANGER. - The long sea-beach lies barren and white, (Oh, tae wind and the salt, salt spray 6 The light-house glares from the stony height Where cliff and sky are grey. The lone- sea-beach lies lonely and bare, (Oh, the wind and the salt, salt spray !, The simple fisher-folk do not dare Come down at close of day. Yet, ever, they say, the dreary coast (Oh, the wind and the salt, salt spray 0 Is haunted by some unresting ghost • That mourns some crime away. SHIRLEY WYNNi</p>
        <p>"I wonder if it is true that coming events cast their dark, grim shadows on before? I have had such a strange foreboding of impending evil all day long, and as night draws nearer, ary heart grows heavier and heavier."</p>
        <p>A alight, girlish figure springs up from the white sands as she utters the words, and turns away from the sea sea with a shudder, walking hurriedly up the steep, narrow, irregular path that leads to the arched entrance of Black-Tor Light- House.</p>
        <p>The warm September afternoon is drawing to a close. The lurid rays of the setting sun glint with a touch of gold the picturesque Florida cost, the motionless sea, and the light-house that rises like a towering monument in grey stone out of the curl- ing waves which surround it, and the narrow strip of fertile island, dotted with dense thickets of palm and wind-blasted orange trees, on which it stands.</p>
        <p>The heat, for this season of the year, has been almost intolerable, and night is coming on in sullen sultriness. For hours no breath of air has stirred the leaves of the trees, or broken the calm of the purple sea. It is the dead calm which, in tropical lands, pre- cedes the hurricane of the September tempest. The slim figure, flitting up the path, sinks down on the worn door-sill of the light-house, anxiously watching the dark, warning clouds that creep over the sky in advance of the on-coming night. "The storm will break before Mark returns from Rocky Point," she murmurs. " What can detain him? He should have been here by noon." Even as she spoke, a few rain-drops splashed upon her upturned face. "It's corning," she cried, in affright, straining her eyes toward the distant line of shore. "The terrible storm I God watch over Mark, and speed his boat safe home I"</p>
        <p>. As she gazed, the gloaming deepened into inky darkness, and the storm began. The slender palms bent to and fro like reeds before the wind. Lightning flashed luridly, and terrific crashes of thunder mingled with the tor- rent of rain, lashing the angry sea into turbulent waves that rose mountain high, beating with wild, mad fury and mighty roar against the impregnable rocks of the old grey light•house. Mark Skefton, the keeper of the light, had gone to Rocky Point early that morning, and all the way the parting words of his pretty young wife haunted hirn, try as hard as he would to forget them. " Don't go, Mark," she had pleaded, earnestly. "I feel sure something will happen before you re- turn. I have a presentiment— ' "Nonsense," he had put in, with a hearty cheery laugh. "Don't be foolish and whimsical, Nella. Nothing is going to happen. Nothing could happen. I shall be back before the storm sets in, and in time to light up." Darkness had crept on, the storm had burst, but where was Mark ?</p>
        <p>Suddenly Nella remembered the light. She had quite forgotten it. Great Heaven ! what if any ship was out in that gulf of blackness without that beacon light to guide her? With winged feet Nella sped up the Spiral stair- way that led to the summit of the great tower. A moment more and a broad glare of light shot out from the great lamp, sending a ruddy glow over the vortex of seething waters that lashed the walls of the old light-house till it trembled and rocked on It foundation.</p>
        <p>"Heaven grant that no vessel is out breasting the fearful storm—to have need of the signal in the tower to warn her off the dangerous reefs." Hark! what was it that sounded over the fierce roar of the maddened elements, and the shrill, piercing cries of the storm driven sea gulls? Nella Sefton strained her ears to listen. The sound was repeated in quick succession. Suddenly the horrible truth burst upon her. The lamp had been lighted too late. A ship is in distress. In the inky darkness. she has drifted on to the fatal rocks, and is signaling for help. Boom! boom! boom! peal the minute guns. Nella Sefton clinging to the rails of the spiral stairway, watches the horrible scene from the tower, as the blinding flashes of lightning reveal it to her gaze, in terror too great for words. In the meteoric light she sees the great ship strike the fatal rock, and dark forms casting them- selves into the seething whirlpool. Then darkness reigns. " Mark," she moans, "God save you from being on the sea to•night " Another flash, and she sees a dark object one instant high on the mad waves, then hurled be- neath thtan. It is a plaak with a dark figure clinging to it. Ms wh te face was turned to her for one awful instant; and the wildest cry that ever fell from human lips burst from Nella Set ton's. She had seen and recognised—Mark..</p>
        <p>But in this appalling' moment her daring oourage did not forsake her. In that glance she had realised his peril, and she knew the only means of saving him would be to fling him the life•rope. If he were to miss it, that meant death—death in the horrible sea she had always dreaded so much but, au I she must not think of that now.</p>
        <p>fjown the spiral stairway Nella flew like a storm-driven swallow, out of the light-house, and out on the sands. The flying spray dashed in her face, and the tierce tempest almost hurled her from her feet, but she scarcely heeded the huricane. A moment more, that seemed the length of eternity, and she had unfurled the great coil of rope which was made fast at one end to the iron ring embedded in tbe solid rock. And over the wild fury of the storm, the man clinging to the Vank heard her voice.</p>
        <p>rope, MALL* Catch the rope I" Once, twice it eluded his frantic grasp. Again it was hurled out over the mountain waves. Oh, God ! would he reach it, or miss it? Nona could only fall on her knees and pray. A breathless moment of horrible suspense. God had answered Nella's prayer. Mark had seized the life-rope in his strong right hand. Terror lent strength to Nella's slender arms. Bracing herself firmly against the projecting rook, she succeeded in drawing Mark to the shore. As his feet touched the sand and he clambered up on it with her aid, she discovered it was not a plank to which he clung, but the body of a woman. A moment more and he had staggered, panting and exhausted, into the living room, followed by Nella, and had lain his dripping burden down upon the old chintz covered settee.</p>
        <p>"Never tnind me. Do what you can for them, Neils.," he said. "There's two of 'ern. I picked them tw in the water. iv.4l_ Wore nay boat atrucit</p>
        <p>the rocks, and threw us into the waves." As he spoke, he threw back the dark cloak . which enveloped the unconscious figure, and Nella saw a tiny infant, which the woman head clutched tightly in her arms. But it was not, the sight of the child, pitiful as the scene was, that brought that startled cry to Nellie Sef ton's lips. It was the woman's face, whom the husband had saved at the perilous risk of losing his own. It was a face as gloriously beautiful as a poet's dream, or artist's ideal; pallid as marble, framed in a mass of dark curling hair, lying in short clusters over her brow, from which the water dripped in tiny pools; eye-brows dark and per- fectly arched ; and lashes long and silken, curling like fringes over the perfect rounded cheeks—a dimpled chin, and lips that seemed too delicately curved and beautious to have ever been invested with the breath of life. A strange, subtle, fierce, jealous pang shot through Nella's heart as she gazed. The rapt ad- iteration in Mark's eyes pained her—she could not tell why. But there was no time to waste precious moments, standing there idly, gazing at the help- -leBB creatures. Heedlfiss that she herself was drenched, Nella sent Mark trout the room, and set about restoring the waifs. In a trice she had divested them of their cloth- ing, wrapped them in soft woolen blaneets, and With .Mark's help, bore them to her own chamber, where her own niumbering, fair-haired baby—little And there, a few moments later, under the inlluence of the powerful stimulante which had been adadnis,ered, —the beautiful etrangsr opened her great, dark, velvet eyes ; and they stared wonder- ingly hot into Nella's, but up into Mark's face. " ‘Vhere ani 1, and where is bauy ?" she mur- mured faintly. "Here is toe little one; you are both safe and sound, and in good hands," responded Mark, cheerily, as he pls.eed the little one in her arms. At that instant niermay surged back again to her overwrought brain. 1 " Oa, .1 remeniber—l remember all," she cried, ,! with a Nvailing shriek. ' "Thu voyage on shipboard, the storm, the fatal rocks, the struggling in the wild sea with baby. Why did you save me," she cried out, sharply, "when I wanted so much to die ?—only to die and end it all. Heaven knows it would have been best for baby, too, if we had died together in the waves." One small hand caressed the baby face, while passionate sobs shook her frame. A beautiful, white, shapely hand it was, on which a wedding. ring shone. "Do not talk like that, dear lady," cried Nella, aghast. "Thank God that you and your baby were rescued, and are safe." "The world narrowed down to a grave, baby," she moaned, paying no heed to Nella's words. " Life is too haru and cruel. I should not begrudge you your poor, hapless existence; but, oh, it would have been better for both you and me if we had died." , "She is delirious, poor thing," said Mark ; " we must get the doctor from Rocky Point to- morrow." The beautiful stranger turned her face to the wall, and during all the long hours of that terrible night, while the hurricane raged outside, she never spoke—never moved—the great, dark, burning eyes never left the small picture of the "Saviour on the cross" hanging there. "Would the baby live through the terrible ordeal?" Nella wondered, taking the little mite, pityingly, from the mother's unresisting arms. Ah, it seemed not; its little life hung on such a slender thread. It was chilled and half drowned from the terrible exposure. But a glad morn was born of this darksome night. The gale had subsided. The waves lay smiling, dimpled, calm, under the golden rays of the rising sun, without a sign to mark the spot where, such a few short hours before, an many souls had been hurled ruthlessks into eternity. As morning broke the beautiful stranger grew rapidly worse, and Mark Salton set out hurriedly to Rocky l'oint for a doctor. "It will be useless ! useless !" she murmured, clinging to Nella's hand, and turning those strangely burning, luminous eyes upon her face. "I shall die within the hour. Something in my heart tells me so." A terrible convulsion shook her frame as she spoke which was rapidly followed by a second and a third. And looking at the white, stark face, Nella , saw that the shadow of death was indeed creeping over it. ' Oh, if the flickering flame of life might but last until the doctor's arrival ! But it was not to be. The stranger grew worse so rapidly that Nella's alarm increased with each passing moment. "11y life is ebbing out!" she gasped, speaking with difficulty. "But oh I cannot die with the dark, horrible story I have to tell, untold. I conjure you, for the love of Heaven, to listen closely to what I have to say. It is the strangest, most pitiful confession that was ever forced from human lips." Nella started back with a low cry, a wild, tan- gible thought shaping itself in her mind. Was , this beautiful young creature a fugitive from justice? Ah, no I It could not be that this lovely, girlish face could ever have been darkened by the shadow of a crime. Then instinctively Nella's eyes wandered from the baby's face to the wedding-ring the stranger wore. r "It is not that," murmured the stricken lad ' her dark, burning eyes following the other's gal' e and interpreting her thoughts aright. " What I have to tell you my husband, who is dead, never knew. Would that my baby might ~ never know !" she wailed out sharply. "It may be kept from her for long years; but on her eigh- teenth birthday—hark you I—on that fatal day it will burst forth upon the shocked and startled world like a skeleton stalking forth from the charnel house. And then—oh, God help my little baby with such a horrible sword hanging over her innocent, unconscious head—l dare not think what will happen then. "And yet I could not rest in my grave—l dare not—with this secret weighing down my soul. I dere not! Yet it is more bitter than death to reveal it. "But promise me first," she went on, huskily, "that you will take my little babe and care for it as your own. Then I will tell you all. "Promise me!" she wailed out, in an agony of entreaty. "I cannot leave baby all alone in the hitter world. I am dying! My moments are fleeting! For the love of Heaven speak quickly It was all 80 sudden Nella could scarcely realize what was transpiring. HOW could she refuse so vital a request, with those entertaining, burning eyes searching her very soul? It seemed to Nella, when she looked back to that weird scene in the long, bitter after years which followed, that she had been in a strange dream. "I promise, you, lady," she answered, slowly; and no warning came to her of all that would come from this fatal promise, "If you die I will keep your little child, ana rear it with my own and care for it as my own. And as I deal by her, so may Heaven deal by me," she added, solemnly. "God bless you! You have made death easier to hear. Never forget your promise. Remember the listening angels have heard it, and recorded it up there Be kind to her, she is so little, so feeble, so helpless ! Tell her about me when she grows up, and how young I was to die, but that death would have been welcome to rhe if it had not been for her, because I dreaded leaving her here. Tell her how I loved her and held her close in my arms, until my heart grew cold and her face faded from my dying eyes. You will net forget?" "No,' said Nella, breaking down completely, though she had struggled hard to keep back her tears through this pitifully pathetic scene ; "I shall not forget." The hapless young mother kissed the tiny, rose- bud face, all the passion and anguish of love shin- ing in her dying eyes. "Let me hold my baby close in my arms while I tell you all," she whisperea, faintly. "Lock the door, and come closer. It is the most pitiful story that ever darkened a human life, and made me wonder if there is peace to be found on the wide earth, and justice in Heaven. You will see by this confession that this babe must never love, for she must never marry." As this startling sentence fell from hor lips a violent spasm shook her frame again, and her features grew rigid. She started up from the couch with a look of frenzy on her ghastly face, as her eyes vainly and mutely sought Nella's. How she trterl to beat hack the wave of death for one brief moment. How her fluttering soul clung to its tenement of clay, to do the bidding of her will, one terrible instaet ; but, alas! the words froze on her stiffening lips in a bitter wail; the dark wave of death closed over her. The vital Secret which was to bring a world of woo to so many lives la as ilestiraoct to runaiu ur-rovt.aled until tllofa Ly</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>LADIES'</head>
        <p>LADIES'</p>
        <p>DEAREST NELLIE,</p>
        <p>The mornings already begin to feel crisp, and the evening shadows .close round us all too soon, telling us very plainly that the summer is over, and with it the reign of the cambric skirt and the dainty chiffons we have loved so well. We are promised a warm September and a brilliant October. But for all that we must provide our- selves with warm dresses and warmer wraps; ftir there is an "edge" on the autumn wind which cannot be overlooked. One of the most useful wraps is of homespun, rough and hairy natural. coloured. These tweeds, besides being exquisitely warm, retain their natural capacity for resisting rain, in consequence of the oil not being extracted from the wool before it is woven.</p>
        <p>Small hats, it appears, will be worn this winter, a the shape popularly known as the toque, and if Fashion has her own way we shall, it seems, half conceal these beneath very long veils. In windy weather these are apt to be troublesome, otherwise they are much more comfortable to weal than those which reach just below the chin, tied cannot be raised without disarranging them.</p>
        <p>A sleeveless vest adds greatly to the dressiness of a coat costume, and it is always advisable to have one vest made of the same material, or par- tially of it. A charming model gives the collar and lower part, arranged in corselet fashion, of the aerge or cloth, and a very small quantity—in fact, three-quarters of a yard—of fancy silk, let in as a full top, completes a very smart garment. Only the ordinary front of linisg is needed, and on this the silk is laid full at the neck, and again gathered about halfway down, and the serge or cloth is laid over it, the top cut in points on either side, and edged trimming, while the bosom darts are sewn in with the lining. The vest fastens invisibly down the front with hooks and eyes, alternately reversed, and is extremely effective if a good con- trast in colour is selec,ed.</p>
        <p>In trimming a circular cape with braid always run a fine, strong silk thread through the upper edge of the braid and draw it in just enough to make it fit smoothly to the shape of the cape, before basting it on. The cape between the rows may be a quarter of an inch, or the width of the braid. Graduated braid is frequently used for trimming caps, and the same rules apply.- Circular or slightly pointed collars are used with standing collars or rolled-over collars. If the cape is to be lined, it should be done after all the trimming is on, and the lining, preferably of silk, should We carefully basted in and hemmed down tail&amp; fashion.</p>
        <p>Some of the season's new coats are made consider. ably longer than was the case during the season proper. For autumn and winter wear the long onces will be coming in, and a very taking cloth jacket is made, with full basque at the back; high collar and shoulder cape edged with seal.</p>
        <p>One of the greatest drawbacks to country life is popularly supposed to be the lack of shopping facilities. The country maid is supposed to depend on an exceedingly limited supply in the country shop, for which she pays an exorbitant price, and looks with envy on the " bargain " advertisements of the city shops. There is a great deal more talk than truth in all this matter. The dowdily- dressed country girl is fast becoming the thing of the past. The facility with which country cus- tomer' reach the great emporiums of trade through the post-office leaves very little to be desired. It is possible that the shopper may see more if she goes personally to the great city, but she is likely to be confused by the very wealth of her opportu- nities, and make no better choice. The great advance in fashion newspapers, as well as the excel- ledt cut patterns which are daily improving, have made it possible for the country girl to be as neatly, and at the same time as stylishly dressed as her city counin.</p>
        <p>A frock for a girl of seven can be made of salmon faced cloth, trimmed with myrtle green, zonave jacket, edged with satin ribbon, and similar ribbon frills encircle the skirt; green collar bordered with satin.</p>
        <p>Pretty economical frocks for everyday school wear can be made by having the bodice and skirt of any old material, with a yoke and sleeves of Turkey red, or navy blue. Make sleeveless aprons to go over them, these being as long as the dresses. They are cut out in a most simple way by folding the material in half to the exact length required, and cutting the neck out large enough to admit the head to go through, making a small opening or kind ot plaque hole at the lsa‘k ; the side seams are sewn up, and a hand of the material worn round tole waist. The mother who has four or Ave little ones to sew for has a fine chance to ecoonomise, and not half a yard of cloth ever need go to waste. If the mother is careful, half a yard of blue material will make pretty sleeves for a brown checked or a dark Davy blue dress. A yard of oleth will make yoke and sleeves. Yours, with love, JENNIE.</p>
        <p>Amongst the ladies who own solid gold dinner ser- vices are Mrs. Astor, Mrs. Judge Andrews, and Mrs. George William Childs. The Dowager Duchess of Londonderry holds a position which is somewhat odd for a woman. She has been elected churchwarden of Ma chynlleth. A pathetic legacy has been left to the Emperor of Austria by an old woman of Mosti, Ursula Krezler, viz., five florins—all her worldly savings. Francis Joseph has accepted the legacy, and given it to a charity. The first coloured woman in the United States to fit herself for practising dentistry is Miss Martha Jordan of Dallas, Tex. She is studying her profession at Denver University. Miss Helen Gould, eldest daughter of the late financier, is planning the erection of a hospital for women. Miss Gould is probably the richest heiress in America, her fortune being estimated at from 10,000,000 dols. to 15,000,000 dols.</p>
        <p>iri-i} .!/ii'(iiiiiii'ol iv\ ..</p>
        <p>\ i I .i. i f , ' 1 1 V ... 1 1 I . 1</p>
