      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <p>ehe had foretold.</p>
        <p>She had fallen back upon her pillow—dead— with the poor, frail babe clatiped close to her pulse- less heart.</p>
        <p>With a sobbing cry Neils, took the child from those stiffening arms, placing it in a little crib in an adjoining room, close to her own s'ePt weeping such tears over it as only tender-hearted women can weep. "Poor, desolate little baby !" she sobbed. "You sleep all unconscious of the bitter bereave- ment that has fallen over you. You have lost the best friend the wide world held for you—your fair young mother!" As him laid the two babes side by side—the one fair, the other dark—they opened their eyes wide with wonder, and gazed at each other. God help poor Nella I flow little she dreamed how bitterly the paths of these two were to cross in the after years. If she could have read the future, her own heart would have broken then and there.</p>
        <p>(To N continued.)</p>
        <p>THE EDUCATIONAL DISPUTE AT BURTON WOOD.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Burtonwood School Board' St. Helens, on Tuesday night, Mr. J. Warburton pre siding, the Clerk (Mr. William Webster) submitted correspondence which had taken place between himself and the Charity Commissioners since the in- quiry into the rightful possessors of a school and schoolhouse, which were left to the township under an ancient deed of gift. On the 30th August the secre- tary to the Commissioners wrote :—" The Commissioners have had under consideration Mr. Durnforciis report with reference to this charity and his recent visit to Burton- wood, and I am now to state that it appears to the COM- missioners on the facts now before them that property conveyed by a deed of 1805 is that upon which the pre- sent church stands, and that the school has been erected upon a plot of land which has only been legally settled on trust. The Commissioners, however, consider that in view of the fact that the trustees appointed under the deed of 1605 have always apparently acted in the ad- ministration of the charity, those trustees must be con- sidered, if not legally trustees of the school and its endowments, in right the administrators thereof, and as such the proper persons to make the application •to the board for an order appointing trustees and establishing a scheme for the charity. lam accordingly to enclose a form in which application may be made for the above- mentioned purpose, and I am to explain that the Com- missioners will not be disposed to interfere by means of any scheme which they may frame, with the occupation of the school buildings on reasonable terms by the School Board." The charity included a schoolhouse, the rent for which is £lO a year, and in reply to Mr. Webster, the Charity Commissioners have since stated that this is in- cluded in their term " school-buildings." It is proposed to establish a scholarship at a local middle school with the £lO a year, subject to the sanction of the Commis- sioners.</p>
        <p>THE ITALIAN BIBLE IN HALFPENNY NUMBERS. REMARKABLE SUCCESS OF THE " SECOLO'S" ENTERPRISE. Mr. Alex. Robertson gives in the "Times" the latest details concerning the success of a reitarkat le enterprise of the " Secolo " of Milan in circulating an illustrated Roman Catholic version of the Bible in Italian in num- bers at a halfpenny each, soaking 10f, for the c•mpleted book. The " Secolo " now announces that the first edi- tion of the Bible is completely exhausted,and that as the demand for it still continues, a second edition has been prepared.and is being issued. The first edition consisted of 50,000 copies. Thus, as each copy cost 101., 500,000 f. or £20,000, have been voluntarily spent by the Italian people in providing themselves with family Bibles. And the demand for this Bible seems to have been special to no class or district, for it has been sold not only in large cities, but also in towns and villages ; nut only amongst the laity, but even amongst the clergy. Mr. Robeitson adds that it is within his knowledge that in the arsenal of Venice during the midday rest the "Secolo" Bible is often read with the daily newspaper with which it is sold. One workman reads whilst the others sit round and listen. When the text and notes do not seem to agree they are quick to notice it, and one instantly cries out, " All, there the text and flees are at fisticuffs," and form their own opinion on the passage. "Again," adds Mr. Robertson, "it might be thought, and indeed this was freely said in 1888, that the sale of the Martini ver- sion would hurt the sale of that of Diodati. But what is the case? Ever since 1888 the sales of the British and Foreign Bible Society's books in Italy have shown s steady increase. Several causes may have brought this about, but certainly one of these has been the issue of the " Secolo" Bible. That enterprise has popularised the Bible by brt aking down the bias against it to which I have_referred. It ha 4 likewise enabled the colporteurs to say, ' The •Secolo7 Bible is cheap for its get up, but still it costs you ten francs. I offer you the whole Bible for one franc, and the difference in the text is in favour of the greater correctness of mine.' The circulation of these Bibles thus runs in parallel lines, cod the flow of the one helps that of the other. One other statement I make. When we unite together the two -Bible streams. then the volume of their circulation surpasses that of any other book in the land. The Bible heads the list of books sold in Italy. It holds its rightful place in the literature of the country as, what Signor Sonzogno calls it, il libro per eccellen:a, and il libro dei libri."</p>
        <p>EXCITING SCENE IN A CIRCUS.</p>
        <p>DEADLY FIGHT BETWEEN CARNIYORA. A LION TAMER IN DEADLY PERIL. CHICAGO, TUESDAY.—One of the most exciting spec- tacles ever seen in Chicago was witnessed here last night at Hagenheck's circus, in the middle of the Plaisance Fair Grounds. Just alter the performing animals, including two bears, three leopards, and a lien, were taken into the arena, which is protected and separated from the auditorium by a high wall, the animals became enraged and attacked each cther with tremendous fury. One of the bears was accidentally struck on the head by a board, and, with a savage growl, he sprang upon one of the leopards. A terrific strugrzle followed. The lion was attacked by the two ether leopards, and one he socn laid hors de combat. He seized him by the spine and literally shook and gnawed him to death. The other leopard also quickly succumbed. the lion and one 9f the bears had a tremendous tussle. The audience panin stricken, fled from the building. In the general stam- pede for the doors several women and children were trampled down and seriously injured. Eventually the lion-tamer, Fritz Maas, rushed into the arena, and, lash. - ing right and left with his heavy whip, succeeded in separating the lion and the bear. Several other anima'g sprang upon the tamer and dragged him to the ground- But for the timely arrival of tha other keepers. armed with iron bars, he would have been torn limb Iron% limb by the infuriated beasts. One keeper thrust a red- hot iron rod into the open mouth of the lion, which then retreated with a ft artul howl. Theother animals were clubbed into submission, and they were then removed. The tamer has sustained severe injuries. • A COMPOSER'S EXPIINSIVE LUXURY-</p>
        <p>M. raderewslii has now delivered the schre of his nal° " Polish Fantasia " which will be produced at the wich Festival next week. The work, it is said, has er ready been performed, though in absolute privacy. Tile great pianist, it seems, desired to judge the effect of the music on his own account before it was given tot world ; arid accordingly he organised in Paris a specia!, rehearsal, engaging and paying a full orchestra, an himself playing the pianc-forte part. Save as to the el" ecutants, nct a solitary person WAR present at tiiis Pert- formance. The idea is, we should imagine, alinos. unique, but it is an extremely sensible one, tor MuSi- cians who listen to their own music for the first time invariably perceive details which call for altpratin Few besides wealthy composers could, however, aff°l., themselves the luxury, which in Loi.dcn would Pr"- bably cost nearly £lOO.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>WIFE'S DEVOTION.</head>
        <p>A WIFE'S DEVOTION.</p>
        <p>A touching story was told to the deputy coroner oif Liverpool on Wednesday. It related to the death °, Mary Banks. She waq but 33 years of age, was marrie. to an artisan, and has left five children. While:l/It husband, who of late has been unemployed, was in Ja', for neglecting to pay towards the maintenance of twof' the children in an industrial school, the poor Wel"n ,e took him food every day for over three weeks, snd to belief is that she caught, in these heroic journeyings and iro, a cold which led to her death from syncope Stt' ceeding pleurisy. Nothing is needed to add to the trag° significance of this pathetic episode.</p>
        <p>41is Mr. John Bryn Roberts is one of the grominent Welsh- men in the House of Commons. A keen lover of sport 10 all its phases, he is a great hunting man and devoted tcol his horses. He plays chess with unusual excelience, Si enjoys the reputation of being the best billiard player 10 the House of Commons. el , 71 ,i . . rjr-A •41 'MI4I y 17101404 Gr. GIVE EA9E &amp; COMFORT DURING TEETHIN Pleas.mt, Cooling Aperient for Children of all Ages. Try them and prove their valuP for yourselves. .....A Sold everywhere in packets, Is. lad. and 2s. 9d., or post '- 14 or 54 stamps. J. PRITCHARD. CIIBMIST. 0 filiADLli. MANWIESTIIIR °EMU horlton Waal.</p>
