      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>THRILLING EXPERIENCE.</head>
        <p>THRILLING EXPERIENCE.</p>
        <p>-- The following storc—which is attracting wide atten- tion front the press—is 80 remarkable that we cannot excuse ourselves it we do not place it before our readers, "Pecially as it authenticated and verified in such an ir- refutable manner. Mrs. E. Bacon, 48, Bank-street,: Rillbead, Glasgow, March 25th, 1893, wrier' :—" About abC years ago I had a bad attack of dropsv. host all appetite ; had it severe pain in my back, with shooting ing tains all through me ; giddiness in the head ; and my -,s very much oppressed. My stomach and :reath NY egg were very uch swollen ; then it extended to my 'are. A f mriend advised me to try Warner's Safe Cure !tad Pills. I did so, and after taking the second bottle I felt much relieved. By the time the fourth bottle was !nished, I felt quite well again. I continued, however, O rake it for seine time longer. I keep Warner's Safe tyis in the house always ; they are such a good medi- / Ile' MY recovery is the more remarkable as I may say d!nherit the disease; both my mother and grandmother ld of dropsy. I recommend this remedy to all my friends, as it has doni, me so much good. My health is t_ •trter now than it was ten years ago."</p>
        <p>COLLIERY OWNERS' PROFITS. From Wolverhampton we learn says the "Daily News," that a striking example of enormous profits derived by those colliery concerns which, are at work in the present crisis was reported on Wednesday. It was , announced that the great Wyrley Colliery Company entered into a contract for coal as ss. 3d. per ton before the strike, has just contracted with the local poor-la w to supply rough slack at 103 3d. Thus the colliery owners are now getting rough slack double what the rail- way company paid for large coal. CONFERENCE AT BOLTON. STERN RESOLUTION. At a special meeting on Thursday at Bolton of the Ashton, Haydock. Boltoa, kc., Miners' Union, Mr. Greenall presiding, Messrs. Tattersall and Turner were deputed to the Chektertield conference, and a resolution was passed condemning the action of the men st Prod- sham pits, St. Helens, for resuming work without the Federations' permission. These were to be excluded from the membership roll unless they cease work till such permission is granted. A COALOWNERS MANIFESTO. The Chairman et the Cealowners' Federation, Mr. A. M. Chambers, has issued a statement containing the owners' views on the present stoppnee in the coal trade. He says that if the owners in the Midland districts have to continue to pay the full 40 per cent, on the 1888 prices the result will inevitably be that the pits will work only one or two days per week, which would mean an enormous loss to the owners, and eventually would cause the permanent stoppage of a large number of the collieries. Be complains that although the owners allowed a disposition to meet, and did meet, the demands of the men in good times, the men refused to in any way meet the demands of the owners in bad times. The owners having offered arbitration, which was refused, had now authorised a committee of their body to meet the representatives of the Mirers' Federation to see if any means of settlement could be arrived at.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS MINEIN AND THE OLD RATE. About 2,000 miners assembled in the People's Palace, St. Helens, on Tuesday, to consider the movement for allowing those miners to go to work who could do so at the old rate of wages. Mr. John Jackson, a miner, was voted to the chair. Mr. George Wilkinson, another miner, moved tht following resolution :—" That this meeting of St. Helens miners is of opinion that the best waf to gain our ob- jects in the present despute is to allow those miners to return to work who can do so at the old rate ot wages ; and that we call upon those who return to work to pay a substantial levy to those who are locked out ; we also instruct our representatives to go for the same at the meeting of the Lancashire Federation to-morrow and at the meeting of the National Federation on Friday." Mr. James Hart seconded. Mr. Glover (miner's agent), then proceeded to review the action of tile Federation, and explained that on the voting of the men the Notts meeting could pass no other resolution than that no one should go to work until there was a general settlement.. Since that conference, not only North Stafford, but Pelsall and Bristol had gone to work at the old rate of wages, and in the Forest of Dean he was sorry to say they had gone in at 20 per cent, reduction and violated the resolution of the con- ference. He was sorry to say that nearer home at, a small colliery the men had returned to work, and had not stood true and faithful to the Federation ot Great Britain. They had gone to work at the old rate of wages but without authority (a Voice : "Stop them "). He (Mr. Gloves) loOked upon men who had gone to work as knobsticks," and it would be for the Federation to say what should be done with them. There was a strong ' feeling on the part of the employers not to ' surrender •any of the 25 per cent. So tar as St. Helens district was concerned there had been rumours that certain employers were willing to open their pits. He was not going to say that was so. He bad not had any official communication of that sort, and therefore he took it there was no employer in St. Helens desirous to open at the old rate of wages. He heard that the Pheenix Colliery Company were willing, put on Monday he met Mr. B. B. Glover, one )f the partners, and asked him if that was so. Mr. Glover re- plied that it was not true, and that before he allowed the men to go to work without the reduction he would see the pits filled up (a Voice: "They'll be filled up then," cheers). If that was his decision he (Mr. Glover) coal ' say they would be filled up before the men accepted any of the 25 per cent. reduction. (Cheers.) But he quite understood that those employers would not show their ' hand until there was good reason for doing so. Mr. Glover then urged the meeting to decide after the strike was over to form one central fund for the union and checkweighmen, and save the expenses of" roomery," ' which meant driak, by the system of separate lodges. A miner in the audience asked if when the men re- turned to work at the old rates they would be allowed to supply contracts. Mr. Glover said certainly not. So far as it was pos- sible they should prevent them. The resolution to go M work at 'the old rate of wages was carried unanimously, and with loud cheering. A resolution to abolish lodges and form a central fund was also carried unanimously. Votes of thanks concluded a meeting which had been orderly throughout, the men manifesting an earnest and enthusiastic spirit. TRADES DEMONSTRATION AT ST. HELENS. A CHANGE OF TACTICS ADVISED. On Saturday, a demonstration in connection with the St. Helens Frades Council was held in that town. A pro- cession was formed, and started from the vicinity of the Town Hall at half-past three o'clock. The miners to the number of 800 or 700, came first, their banner bearing the inscription " St.fielens District Federation of Miners. Established 1880. Justice and Truth." The Bottle- makers' Trade Protection Association sent their banner, but not many representatives, while the Coopers' Trade Society, coming next, had a new banner inscribed " United to obtain the reward of our labour." A special feature was the banner of the Amalgamated Society of Railway Servants, which had been sent down from Lon- don for the occasion. It was elaborately illustrated with two views of signal boxes on the line. Over one box were the words "Thirteen hours; over-work and danger," with a signalman asleep, unconscious of an approaching train. Over the other box," Eight hours ; short hours and safety," with a signalman in full exer- cise ot his duty. The Amalgamated Union of Bakers and Confectioners' the Sheet-glass Flattener? Society, and the National Association of Plasterers had also the emblems of their amalgamations. Several bands took part in the demonstration, which consisted of 800 or 1.000 persons. After parading the principal streets of the town, and collecting subscriptions for the miners en merle, a meeting was held in the Circus, about 1,600 per- sons being present. Mr. -Tames Hart, president of the St. Helens Trades Council, expressed regret that Mr. Samuel Woods,M.P., was confined to his room by illness, and unable to fulfil the engagement he had made to be present and address that gathering. Apologies were also made for the ab- sence of Mr. All. Greenwood and Mr. Hamblett, of the Glass 13(atle Makers' Association, who were in Glasgow. The Chairman then expressed his belief that up to the present time the miners had had a fair share of the sympathy of the public at large, and that ought to make them more determined to keep within the bounds of the law, and to give the authorities as little trouble as pos- sible. (Cheers.) Mr. R. Billington (insurance agent), proposed the first resolution, as follows:—'' That this meeting strongly condemns the action of the coalowners in torcing the present struggle not only on the miners in connection with the Federation of Great Britain, but also on most trades and industries throughout the country, and pledges itself to do all in its power, morally and finan- cially, to support the victims of the unjust demand of the coalowners ; and this meeting is further of opinion that the nationalisstion of mines and minerals is urgently necessary in the interests of the coal miners and the coal consumers; and therefore urges upon the Government the advisability of giving an early day fqr the consideration of a resolution or bill dealing with the subject." Mr. George Dixon, check weighman, in seconding the resolution, said arbitration meant reduction. (A Voice : "Yes, that's what the masters mean.") They did not know what had taken place at the coalowners' meeting, but he believed there was something behind the scenes, and that shortly there would be a complete victory for the men. (Great cheering.) Mr. Thomas Glover, J.P. (miners' agent), said it was clear to him that there was a great divisiun in the em- ployers' camp,because on all hands employers were ask- inc. men to go`to work at the old rate of wages. He had to admit that in Stifelens a number of men had gone to work,but not on a reduction. He had that morning been to a federation meeting in Manchester, and it was there felt that they must change their tactics and policy. He also thought this should be done for the purpose of bringing down the employers. If the conference decided to allow the men to go back whenever the old rate of wages was given, lie believed it would be the means of brimming them down all along the line. (Cheers.) Mr. Matkin, of the Joiners' and Carpenters Society, Liverpool, congratulated the miners of St. Helens on the worthy manner in which they had conducted them- selves during the movement. The resolution was then put and cartied unani- mously. Mr. Craig (Typographical Association) then proposed the second resolution as follows:—" That this meeting. realising, the urgent necessity of thorough organisa- tion, calls upon all workingmen not already trades unionists to join at once their respective unions where such exist, and where they do not exist to lose no time in having such fermed,as it is only by combination that the workers of Great Britain will ever be able to obtain their rights ; and, further, that in the interests of the workers it is only fair that we support home industries to the fullest extent in our power." Mr. P. Finch (Bakers' Society) seconded the resolu- tion. • Mr. J. Sexton (dock labourers) contended that non- unionists had placed the miners in their present position. Be pointed out that in Great Britain there were 700,000 I miners, and as only 300,000 were on strike 400,000 must be working. If those 400,000 had also been out the strike would not have lasted a day. The resolution was carried, and votes of thanks ' brought the meeting to a close.</p>
        <p>• .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>IRE COAL TRADE CRISIS.</head>
        <p>IRE COAL TRADE CRISIS.</p>
        <p>AID FROM THE JOINERS. I,.The Amalgamated Society of Csrpenters and Joiners granted £5O to nhe Miners' Federation, in aid of tbe —miners during their present dispute. THE CONTINENTAL MINERS. ii.l4". 6. Wanskenar.—There is a notable decrease to- 'y in the number of miners on strike. A speedy ter- reination of the strike is anticipated. MASTERS TERMS REFUSED. The ballot of Warwickshire misers taken on Wednes- n6Y resulted in a practically unanimous refusal to accept ;:ue masters terms of 5 per cent. reduction now and a lurther reduction on the Ist Janaary. Disturbances are teared unless a settlement is speedily effected. MILITARY RIOT AT CHESTERFIELD. DISGRACEFUL SCENE. On Ch.. "eadaY, disgracetul scenes were enacted at nit -4eTrfield. In a field near the railway station are eci- s ;tuned some three hundred men of the Dublin Fusilliers, id Northamptonshire, and York Dragoons. Tuesday ll:iss,Pa:7 day, and some of the soldiers drank heavily. n -ar g Jeered by a number of strikers who had assembled _e the -ncampment, the Dublin Fusilliers scaled the '" V.,fall surro ~unding the camp and attacked the miners. 1.,,th sides suffered severely before the regimental ser- Ze;.,"ts could separate the combatants, and by this time tn—e four thousand persons had assemoled. Four of I, 6 Military ringleaders were arrested, and will be "rneght before a military court. ,RELIEVING THE DISTRESS AT ST. HELENS. I. `41.. Thomas Bcechan, the celebrated pill mann- , .acturer, St. Helens, has been personally interesting riself in the prevailing distress in that town, and so kisrnalis we can ascertain has given over £5O to soup towneus at schools, food for the waifs and strays of t . he and monetary assistance for the poverty-stricken sy",,,iteal labourers, who do the " task " at Winston , ne°sC`muse. The latter mode of distributing •charity is a...: aPS the most remarkable, inasmuch as the donor, on kisa-ue!toining that these men receive the relief only in aptiZ...; concluded that they must require coal and other osses, and having secured the services of the relieving Moira% has, through them, added 6d in cash to each Irsd ue received the parish order for goods from esteen. The THE POSITION AT HAYDOCK. sneh Position of affairs at Haydock remains practically anged. Everyone appears to be getting heartily Tile ~cf the strike and is anxiously awaiting the end. the Ml6iless in the district is very acute osaccount of eonn//ni'le of the residents being directly or indirectly tth. eat teheted with the coal trade. Shopkeepers are ending ,eY Will not be able to subscribe much longer to shog"ef funds, and that their " trust" business must haeo cease. It is felt that when the colliers do get atsttt)tirot to a certain portion of them will never o Istesor twel:: off their debts. One shopkeeper has now ten u worth of bad debts contracted during a local strike years ago. The general opinion is that ta:Ara• Richard Evans and Co:, the proprietors of the ao,:'-mck Collieries, will remain firm and not submit to nc ico Collieries, whatever. A MINERS CONFERENCE AT MANCHESTER. an..cow - erence cf delegates belonging to the Lancashire Cheshire Miners' Federation was held at the tiessf;A• offices, Peter-street, Manchestester, on Wed- casf;,..l' tø consider what action &amp;mild be taken at the staiisAtenrce, at Chestsrfisld on Friday. Mr. C. Aspin- cus „,'L'gart) occupied the chair, and there was a flumes- a lo,7"eritig. The following resolution was passed by ene "e majority : " That our delegates to the confer- thee at Chesterfield move that all men who are offered thatold rate of wages be allowed to resume work, and in they be asked to pay a substantial levy tinsuPPort of those still locked out." The amount men- Was sy 5 as ss. The • , „umotion was carried by 52votes to 27. ).,..‘!t_ setliors were aEked ' why a joint meeting had not • 7,handd between coalowners and miners' representa- silawas carsiiaezi cout.c.oTmh ea to f u. thesee give r following delegates raets °tilt: e- Chesterfield Conference power to move or vote for a re- solution favourable to a meeting with the employers on . the open question." The three delegates appointed to attend the Chester- field Conference wer.. Messrs. R. W. Langford. Isherwood, T. Crreenall, ' and , The Conference also arranged for a public meeting to , ntheld at Preston on Saturday week, when Messrs. Sam Woods, T. Aspinwall, and T. Glover will speak. „n _ CURIOUS APPLICATION IN COURT. issu Tuesday a curious point arose before Mr. Justice nuedy sitting as vacation judge in connection with —is mai now, ..war in Lancashire. Counsel, in making the an,y33 ,e6oe. stated that he appeared for the Committee stee of the tenant for life of the Wigan Brewery, in"l"inutiastruoted him to apply for leave to subscribe the !liftoff P-30 per week to funds raised in the Wigan ens- . or relief of distress caused by the coal strike. The ' i7trtit for life was a lunatic. The trustee regarded the thecised contribution as an act of good management of in allstate, , Ibecause if it were not made the miners would , sine,Prnbability boycott this brewery's beer.—Mr. Jus- to tn,wertnedy o ; ads is an act of charity that you expect i 1ric0,.,.. lin this world.—(Laughter.) What is the ion'"e of the estate ?—Counsel 1 We cannot spend the "lel/A e'--Mr. Justice Kennedy : 4 shall require further p N.,trliee to show that the funds to which you wish to t nfog'ete will not be used for / controversial purposes tlopowtinateiF the strike is still continuing, and I have &amp;nun rto allow contributions to strike funds. At the altnpi une, on being' satisfied that the money will go to ,to the relief of distress, I will do all in my power Van `Lr.ler the wishes oi the trustee.—The application aellourned.</p>
        <p>eQns• ALARM AT HUYTON. Noe t Idcrable alarm was occasioned on Monday after- large ,‘`) the residents and shopkeepers of Hayton.when a Put itt's°d9 of police, under Superintendent Hasen dailre,. rotatidinu appearance. Some were drafted from he s torte, g districts,whilst a number came by rail.t A ru- ' itiey, hli9on 9 spread that a raid was expected on the iett7t h which had some foundation in e light Ip,,at:warnings had been given during the past fort- ' %I; erlnciPally on account of the employment of two .0e nands that were employed at the Whiston Col- the lock out.and also by an alleged breach of the'"lne h publithat no coal would be supplied to dealers but to ~tc • 4We be --Lrect ; and it is said that fifteen wagon loads of tz.zi en sent to Messrs Pilkington,St.Helens. A gang .expected from the neighbourhood of St. .4* their "'gar', but the police nityle every preparation Zoo, reception, having, in addition to a body in re- -4 into the ts the men failed to put in an appearance. The Colliery is but a new one, employing onlyabout 'lhaq'tda. who are principally engaged in sinking the gh thee have an average output of thirty tons '2sa Cr day. the owners did not join in the proposal troWsPer cent. roduction.but have been apprehensive of they" 2 from the beginning of the struggla ; but so far Ldhr %."akvo been allowed to continue, though the men Liveg about watch with suspicion the loads carted 401 and elsewhere. At &amp;k VIOLENT COLLIER AT ST. HELENS. 4nd en". Helens on Saturday, before Alderman Harrison 441• bearllor.r. R. Leach, a determined-lookingcollier !• Buil ,11liam Jackson, of Barnsley, was charged h 31. oreaking eleven panes of glass and a sash, value outtel ale window of Henry Whalley, a clogger, at otti;:, Prisoner has been in St. Helens about three ~uody '2 and a few weeks since he was at the head of the k.ea Gnr i strikers who visited Bold, Ashton's Green, and w:erl Collieries, and demanded that the few men t"'n Ft,' re keeping the pits in order should cease work. -nniint of this he acquired the nickname of e merit o asp, a or Leader." Chief Constable Wood stated the hair. liwatltton, Prison tid called evidence, from which it appeared that ,dsaie,,ereWent into the shop of Mr. Georgeßeid, provision On Friday morning, and demanded a loaf. a paid Lola him he could not give him one, upon which trriezatiro icedhethawtould take one. Mr. Held's sister then if he (Jackson) had asked in a proper kr. wir, Rhe would have given him something to eat. J, VleY at this juncture came into the shop, and son he auvht to he ashamed to ask anyone for .°114• 44 der he been drinking from Monday to pp% Mr: Whalley then returned to his own shop, erth 37 Prisoner, who said Mr. Whalley was not *eat sPatronising. The latter replied that he did net II" Patronage as Jackson could give, and be went carry. At half- ast one o'clock' however, he returned sal r,'"1,41 a stoneP ' which weir,,h,ql 57Ibs. on his shoulder. UP to the window he deliberately burled it BM completely completely destroying the glass and frame. ;Itne 1:(1 ran across and seized prisoner (who by fthat h.lad thrown a second stone at the window). Ind halo IT while Ali-. Whalley went for Constable Filling- risoner had nothing to say in reply to the loue'e'•"--Chier Constable Wood read n long list of prey- other nnvictiona at 'Barnsley for assaulting the police ird Prt,o4Person ,s for theft, &amp;c., under which he had been in ..,tnoath„for.,terms varying from fourteen days to two •11tho sae was committed to gaol for two months ut the optima Of a Aug. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>111 E F,XA INFR, SATURDY. SEPTEMBER O. 1893. TO LONDON IS A bTEAMBOAT.</head>
        <p>111 E F,XA INFR, SATURDY. SEPTEMBER O. 1893. TO LONDON IS A bTEAMBOAT.</p>
        <p>THE FALLING OFF IN TRAFFIC RECEIPTS.</p>
        <p>The Midland Railway Company's traffic receipts for last week show a decrease of £84,789 compared with the corresponding week last year. The aggregate decrease for the last twelve weeks compared with the same period in 1892, is no less than £422,994. Other companies are proportionately affected by the coal strik, The North Staffordshire receipts for last week dropped £2,347, and the Hull and Barosley showed a decrease of £1,539. Other cl creases are Manchester, Sheffield, and Lincolnchire, £18,231 ; Glasgow and South-Western, £906 ; London and North-Western, £29,844 ; Great Nor- thern, £18,797. DERBYSHIRE COALOWNERS GIVING WAY. In view of the decision of the Executive of the Miners' Federation several D:ltyshire coalowners have given way. An application was made by the Derbyshire Miners' Union for the men on strike at Salterwood Col- liery, Denby, West Hallam, Ilkeston, and Ford's Col- liery, Ripley, to recommence work at the old rates pend- ing a settlement. It is stated that Colonel Seeley, M.P., owner of several collieries in Notts and Derbyshire, and employing several thousand hands, has given notice to the owners Federation to resign, se that his pits can commence to be worked at the old rates. Notwith- standing this change in the situation, a member of the Federation executive informed the Press Association correspondent that there is not the slightest sign of a general settlement. THOUGHTFUL GENEROSITY AT ST. HELENS. The head masters and mistresses of the schools in St. Helens, on Thursday received the following interesting communication :—" Memorandum from Thos. Beecham, proprietor of Beecham's pills and Beecham's tooth paste, St. Helens, Lincashire,September 27th, 1893. Dear Sir, —I shauld like to provide all the barefooted school chil- dren in St. Helens with Clogs and stockings,and shall be much obliged if you will make a list of the children with bare feet attending your school on the morning of Thurs- day, the 28th inst. I ask your prompt attention to my request, and on receipt of your reply on the enclosed form I will send you a sufficient number of orders on drapers and cloggers to supply the want.—Yours faith- fully, Taos. BZECHAX." Mr. Beecham has already con- tributed liberally to the various soup kitchens in vogue for the benefit of the colliers' children, and this latest generous Impulse will be much appreciated in the borough, where Ike weather has already given an un- pleasant foretaste of a severe winter. MEETING OF THE MINERS' FEDERATION BOARD. A Chesterfield correspondent telegraphs that the Miners' Federation Board met on Thursday afternoon at the Miners' Offices, Chesterfield, which were opened six months since and cost £3,500. Mr. Pickard, M.P., occupied the chair, and there were present Mr. Ashton, secretary, Manchester; Mr. Edwards,treasurer, Burslem ; Mr. S. Woods, M.P., vice-president Messrs. Cowey and Parrott, Yorkshire; Mr. IsherwoOd, Lancashire; Mr. Harvey, Derbyshire; Mr. Balle'y, Notts; Mr. Chambers, Leicestershire: Mr. O'Connor, of the Coal Porters; Mr. B. Dean, Walsall; Mr. Brace, Monmouthshire; Mr. S. Wititehouse, Somersetshire ; and Mr. Whitfield, Bristol. The meeting was held in private. The executive sat until five o'clock._ when Mr. Ashton, the secretary, informed the representatives of the Press that it had been decided not to supply any official statement until Friday's full board meeting. The executive have prepared a series of resolutions for Monday's conference, and a warm debate took place on the owners' reply to Mr. Ashton. The executive do not recommend a committee being appointed to meet the owners as they do not recognise the necessity of a rdduction.</p>
        <p>which are equalled only in the splendour of their scenery, by the legends and stories which cluster about them. There are tales of the daring smug- glers with whom the coast used to abound ; of inci- dents of attempted invasions, and above all the weird mythology which has weaved around those ancient ports, and those never changing headlands and caves, a legendic charm which can only be appreciated by a sight of the coast. A' holiday is too common a phrase for the trip ; it was a pil- grimage where the health is administered to by the balmy ozone, and the imagination .quickened, and the eyes gladdened by almost everything that is seen.</p>
        <p>From Plymouth to Southampton and Portsmouth took another day, and this day abounded more with sights than any other. Indeed it would take to long to recount what is to be seen of the charming Isle of Wight, of the glimpses of Cowes and Ryde. It would take too long to attempt to delineate the scenic beauties which surround, in Southampton, the crumbling walls and the time corroded towers of the City of the Past,which stand, the grim silent antiquities of dead historical ages, amidst the bustle and life ot the Modern Port. Suffice it to say that when London is reached on the Wednesday every- one is pleased beyond utterance with the trip, pleased with the healthfulness of it, with the charms of its sights, and pleased above all with the consideration with which they were treated on board, and with the reasonable bum which covers the voyage. A. L. 40 - HARVEST FESTIVALS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>REMARKABLE SPEECH BY A COALOWNER.</head>
        <p>REMARKABLE SPEECH BY A COALOWNER.</p>
        <p>A very large meeting of miners was held on Thursday in Chesterfield Market-place, and men from all the sur- rounding districts attended. Mr. Thomas Sayler, M.P. who is a colliery proprietor in Derbyshire and Notting- hamchire, addressed the men, and advised them to stick to the federation and their leaders. He said he was in the position of being able on fair and reasonable terms to allow all his men to return to work at the old rate of wages, and he regretted that the Derbyshire owners could not do likewise. The masters, in demanding 25 per cent, reduction were in a false position, and that was why he withdrew from the Csalowners' Fsderation. He advocated a board of conciliation in each county. Pique alone prevented many masters from withdrawing the lockout, and many others were wicked enough to assert that by starving the men they would smash the Miners Federation. . .</p>
        <p>_ Mr. Hamlam, Derbyshire, secretary of the Federation' congratulated Mr. Bayley on the way he had acted towards his men. It was, he said, the cut-throat policy of excessive competition amongst noalowners that had brought them to this extremity, and be therefore wel- comed the idea of a gigantic coal trust. The meeting unanimously resolved to loyally carry nut the policy of the Miner's Federation, and declared its belief that the proposed reduction of wages was altogether unnecessary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>BLAIROOWRIE SERMON.</head>
        <p>THE BLAIROOWRIE SERMON.</p>
        <p>A REMARKABLE EPISTLE,</p>
        <p>Mr. Labouchere writes in " Truth :"—I have received from the Rev. F. W. Davies rector of Blairgowrie, the following letter, accompanied by the sermon mentioned in it. I gather that the sermon is the one in which Mr. Davies was alleged to have preached "at " Mr. Glad- stone. My other engagements unfortunately prevent my reading the sermon, but, from a hasty glance, I i should say t is of a most commonplace and inoffensive nature, and I hope that Mr. Davies will find my opinion " tally with his." The only remarkable thing about it is that this good man, who is so exercised about the revenues and endowments of the Welsh Church, and who can write such a foolish letter as the tollowing, should Tet be able to preach with a certain amount of sense on the duty of serving God and despising Mammon. If I might offer him a word of advice, it would be that he should henceforth devote himself to religion, and des- pise politics : The Rectory, 13Ia irgowrie, N. 8., September 21, 1893. Dear Veracity,—l send for your perusal, in the hope that I may see your opinion of it in the pages of that purgat-Tial print of yours, facetiously denominated "Truth," a sermon of mine, preached on the morning of Sunday, the 10th inst., in the presence of a gentleman who has promised (foe a consideration already granted) some twenty-five or more degenerate Welshmen, whose conduct I repudiate, and who have forsaken "the old paths" wherein their good old forefathers walked, to confiscate the ievenues of the old Church of their coun- try, which is more venerable in its antiquity than either. the English Church or the English Parliament, and to put them to profane purposes. Of course if your opinion regarding it tally with mine, 161)411 think you a man of much discernment and clear penetration, and that you are not quite so innocent of wisdom-teeth as divers your maligners dare to insinuate ; bamptllolesdtt., dr;:toobtlamh ebislnoyh.et,:haelolrot coagrunitd , should your verdicc be at all op- vgaelilnultyt tsehtraahlinlnekela e toi thatmamn etnahsueedl yiqb quotation, aeoroz,tlisi oe dins " Mens sana in corpore sano" does not apply to you. Reminding you, sir, that your reputation is at stake, and that, therefore, you were best to elevate great care to the review 01 mv sermon, lam, A WELSH TORY AND NO ROBBER OF CHURCHES.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>ADVENTURES OF A CRIMINAL.</head>
        <p>ADVENTURES OF A CRIMINAL.</p>
        <p>If the French Government are really desirous of rid- ding them,elves of their criminal population, it seems as thouh they will have to find a more suitable place of transportation than Cayenne. The number of escapes from this penal settlement has attained alarming pro- portions. So confident are the old hands of their ability to matte good their escape that some of them openly say so, and defy the police when they are recaptured for some fresh offence. A waiter named Capy, who has just teen arrested for robbing his master, the proprietor of the Café de l' Ecole Centrele, in the Rue de Turbigo, 'in Paris, bodily avowed on being arrested that he was an escaped goal bird (" cheval de re- tour evadee."( "You may send me back to Cayenne," lie d-clared, " but I shall soon be with you again, now I know my way about." From a narrative of his experi- ences which this engaging person also volunteered, it would seem, however, that there is some risk in runnii g away from Cayenne, although so many succeed in Li— attemrt. He had, he said, to traverse two forests, each of which was a fortnight's march in length, and during a whole month he risked being devoured by wild beasts or either killed or returned to the French police by sav- ages. Reaching the coast, Copy embarked for Hayti on an English vessel, and from Hayti he returned to France This be was enabled to do with the savings he had made Lout of his earnings as a convict. The last time he was I sent out it was for robbery and attempted murder.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>IBSEN.</head>
        <p>IBSEN.</p>
        <p>Ibsen, the Norwegian dramatist, is one of the most methodical of men. The pap(rs on the table are all most neatly tied up into little bundles, the manuscripts fastened with elastic bands—everything is in its own place. He is faddy, almost to the point of old-maidism and cannot bear disorder. Everything he does is done slowly and neatly, and he is always punctual to a second. He writes a clear round hand, the very essence of copy-book work. He talks very quietly and deliber- ately, and walks and moves slowly.</p>
        <p>According to Mr. Cuninghame Graham, the Feather- stone miners are so hard-leaturea and angular that one wonders how their women folk endure a kiss from them. It must, he says, .be like an embrace from a coping-stlne, or feel like falling on a bit of granite pavement in Aberdeen.</p>
        <p>PUBLIC NOTICB.—Not one tmt HUNDRBBS'of people ol both sexes with wrecked and debilitated constitutmns are yearly restored to sound and robust health again by Messrs. Wilkinson. 3 and 4, Fitzallan Square, Sheffield. Write to them for advicefree when suffering from weakness, loss of flesh. appetite, disturbed rest, pimples, blotches. indiges- tion, pallid skin, nervousness, and general debLity. Posi- tive cure for all Blood and Skin Diseases, acquired or consi tutiunal. Sixty years' experience. One address (as above) for over 30 years. The" A 1 Mixture," 4s. 9d. the case. The Magic Mirror in envelope. 'four' scarnps. Sufferers &amp;sap pointed with Elsetric Belts should write to us at onee.—Adti</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>DESIRABLE VOYAGE.</head>
        <p>A DESIRABLE VOYAGE.</p>
        <p>- The problem vz. one which perplexad me for • several weeks. How I was to spend the brief re- spite from business, which for years had inveigled • myself into terming, with some little irony," my annual vacation," was a question which I struggled zealously to answer, as many another has dsne. I tried very bard to do it. I collected all printed lists of tours that came before me; but everything seemed to give promise of my efforts being crowned with unmistakable and unmitigated fail- ure. Time after time did I eagerly select soma far off spot, worthy of a visit, and then laboriously set about arranging the details of the programme, which I invariably did with credit, until I came to the one item, "expenses," and, then lo I my dreams were shattered, for the exchequer I found would not carry me through. Sometimes I would have a change of fortune in the selectisns and fell upon the names of less imposing resorts. But the inev- itable impossibility of making the finances fit the scheme thrust its unwelcome self into consideration again, for in these cases I found I had too mush to spend—my finances were greater than my cal- culated expenses! And thus this harrassment con- tinued until a friend suggested that I should take a trip to London in a steamboat. This I did. I lost no time in visiting Messrs. S. Hough Bros., of Water-street, who are the proprietors of the only passenger steamship Company,plying between Livierpool and the capital, and arranging for my berth on one of their splendid boats " The Mary Hough." The trip takes on the average between three and four days, and the "Mary Hough," runs alternately with a sister vessel. Although the first attention throughout both boats has been bestowed in the direction of providing for the comfort of the passengers, they carry cargo. This, however, only lends itself as a facility to making the voyage even more beneficial to the holiday passenger than it could otherwise be at the same fare tor the cargo service carried on requires the vessels to put in at Falmouth, Plymouth, Portsmouth, and Southamp- ton. At each of these places the passenger is al- ways able to get a few hours ashore, and is thus lucky in securing a glimpse of four of the chief and most interesting ports on the south coast. We sailed for the Company's Wharf in Trafalgar dock on the Saturday afternoon, and as we steamed down the stream to the bar, I was able to obtain an idea of the accommodation that was provided us. To start with there are no steerage berths, and the saloon, and the berths attached thereto are aft, as well as a number of snug little state cabins. The saloon, I observed, was a spacious compartment, easily accommodating 100 persons, whilst there was more solid comfort with the character of its fittings than any fastidious person could have expected, especially in the sleeping berths. This also applies equally to the accommodation for lady passengers, who are freed from any inconveniences in their new and temporary home by the attentive services of a stewardess. Towards five o'clock we had got ' well in sight of one of the most picturesque and rugged pieces of coast scenery, namely, the two Ormes Heads. This first sight of any note had the effect ot drawing the passengers (just enough to make up a comfortable picnic party) together, anu by the mutual exchange of sea glasses and com- ments on the scenery acquaintances were made, and a kind of harmonious family feeling began to testify itself. Then we all adjourned for tea, the first meal on board. So far Neptune had not asked fora single tribute ; for which cordial but silent thanks were no doubt accorded him by that party, for the sea air had already given robust strength to their appetites and shown them wherein lay the joy of a hearty repast. And mach an one was provided. This, indeed, reminds me of a fact that unanimity of opinion was expressed upon, namely, that the meals provided throughout were at a less price than a table d' hots on shore, whilst their quality sur- passed that of many tables d' hote ,that I know of, which insinuate on their cards a claim for atten- tion because of the " high quality." of the food. Then at this meal the acquaintance is made of one who is responsible for the comfort of the pas- sengers, and he is the genial chief steward, Mr. Jackson, who besides having a wealth of experience to hear upon hisduties,which render him a valuable officer, has a reputation for kindly befriending those who are unhappy in the non-possession of sealegs, and the possession of an inclination to mal de leer, when the sea is rough. 1 After tea we again went on deck. The sun had set and the shadows of night were beginning to en- compass us, and to those whose first night it was at sea, the experience was striking and novel. The great silence of the sea always becomes more impres- sive at nightfall. It seems almost lonesome at first. Asvay over in the west was a great American liner, which had gone by us with prodigious snortings of its funnels; with its decks crowded with passen- gers. Now no sound came from it; no figure was discernable on its decks; only the night lights at the mast heads, and the lights from the port holes en- abled us to keep it in view as it disappeared gradu- ally, silently, over the horizon into the night. Occasionally a gull would dart out of th + darkness and pass over us with a weird cry, and just over the rail the eye could see the big dark waters falling and rising with a gentle, but full regularity, re- minding one somehow of the regular pulsations of the heart of a sleeping giant: a queer simile, but true. By-and-by the stars became clearer, and finally we had a clear moon shining upon us. At ten o'clock we turned into our berths. It was a happy, bright, summer morning that dawned upon us the next day; the sky was blue and cloudless, the sea calm and the breeze soft and re- freshing. Everyone was early astir, the loslies in- cluded, and the apprehensions of a call from Nep- tune which had made many faces look a trifle thoughtful the previous day,had vanished. Invigora- tion and happiness sat upon every countenance, and remained there during that calm pleasant day which was one of the most refreshing and enjoyable I had ever spent. This was getting the sea, and its beauties, in a way to be thankful for. It was differ- ent from the tainted sham to be had at the cheap watering place. The breeze was sweet and unladen with the hundreds of culinary stenches from sea- side restaurants, and the weary mind seeking rest had nothing to fear from those nauseous character- istics of the promenade; there were no alms-begging cripples about; no 4speptic hurdy-gurdies ; and no crowds of howling, screeching itinorant cork- minstrels, with their stale jokes and hateful mimi- cries. This was not taking a holiday by the sea in a way calculated to break down a cast iron consti- tution—and create an undesirable proclivity for saying things which have virtue in force, but none in politeness, which I have known men come from the sea-side equipped with ! During the morning a religious service was con- ducted by the Captain, at which most of the passengers attended. The name of the officer men- tioned is captain Rees, who has a reputation for being one of the most careful and conscientious commanders on the coast service. Towards noon we passed St. David's Head, and were soon plough- ing through the mouth of the Bristol Channel, and about four in the afternoon we had made the coast line of Land's End. _</p>
        <p>The first thing we caught eight of WAR the Long- ships lighthouse, which is built on rocks two miles from Lands End. So rough is the sea at times here that when a gale is blowing sometimes six or eight weeks elapse before the watchers in that solitary lighthouse are able to communicate with the main land. Gradually nearing the famous cape we came within Sight of the wild rugged barren coast of rocks, looking picturesque enough now in this calm weather. But they are not always so, sometimes they present in gales a scene whose wildness is dreadful to observe from the laud, but surely fatal to crafts that approach too near it from the sea. Later on we reached Land's End proper. In all I directions were headlands, crowned by what appeared to be ruinect castles and towers: rocks I were scattered about and heaped up in most fantas- tic shapes: whilst far off in the west could be seen the outline of the beautiful Scilley Isles. As Kings- ton said "Imagination might picture them av some fairy land likely at any moment to vanish." The Land's End itself consista of a massof granite which extends in a lofty ridge far into the sea,surmounted by rocks that look like the ruins of an ancient castle. It was dark when we passed the Lizard, and all We could see of it were the brilliantly illuminated towers and lighthouses. It was ten o'clock when we entered Falmouth harbour, and as this was the first port touched at, almosc the whole of the pas- sengers went ashore and "did" the" beautiful port " " by moonlight," but the glimpse of the Place obtained the next morning before breakfast alone showed us from the north the wealth of beauty and picturesqueness which this historical place possesses. We sailed for Plymouth after breakfast, and had a pleasant run for five hours, during which the pas- sengers were able to enjoy the coast scenery and Indulge in quoits, or cricket on the main deck. Before taking this trip I would advise anyone to read Kingston's " Yacht Voyage Round England," and they will then enjoy the betterthe many sights right along the south coast, too many to enumerate</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>WOOLSTON.</head>
        <p>WOOLSTON.</p>
        <p>The thanksgiving was held at Woolston on Sunday aftqrnoon. The school had been decorated for the occa- sion by the Misses Booth and Parkinson and the scholars. The pulpit was occupied by the Rev. J. J. Langham, M.A., (vicar), who took for his text, " Wherefore do ye spend money for that which is not bread."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>HALTON.</head>
        <p>HALTON.</p>
        <p>The harvest festival in connection with St. • Mary's Church, Halton, wts held on Thursday evening, when the Rev. T. Pym Williamson, vicar of Thelwall, preached The services were continued on Sunday, when sermons were preached by the Vicar to large congregations. In the afternoon a children's service was held.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>ST. JAMES', LATCEIFORD.</head>
        <p>ST. JAMES', LATCEIFORD.</p>
        <p>On Sunday the harvest festival services were con- tinued at St. James' Church, Latchford. There was a fair congregation at the morning service, when the pul- pit was occupied by the Rev. R. M. Stapylton, M.A. A children's service was conducted during the afternoon. The Vicar preached in the evening, when there was a large congregation. The offertories 'were in aid of the church expenses. ST. PETER'S. Large congregations attended the harvest festival con- tinued at St. Peter's Church on Sunday, The morning preacher was the Vicar. In the afternoon a children's service took place, when an address given by the Rev. J. Davies, curate-in-charge of St. Ann's. The Rev. J. C. Pigot, M.A., vicar of St. Thomas's, St Heleps, preached at the evening service. The offertories at each of the ser- vices will be devoted to the Church Boundary Wall Fund. _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>EMMANUEL CHURCH.</head>
        <p>EMMANUEL CHURCH.</p>
        <p>- Harvest thanksgiving services were conducted at the Emmanuel Church Bold-street on Sunday. The Rev. E. T. Reed preached both morning and evening. In the evening he took for his text the words "Bread corn is bruised." The rev, gentleman's remarks which related to the present distress, will be found incorporated in the article on that subject. The collections, which amounted to £4 ss. 4d., were handed over to the general distress fund.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>S. PAUL'S</head>
        <p>S. PAUL'S</p>
        <p>The Thanksgiving services for the safe ingathering of the harvest were commenced at S. Paul's Church, War- rington, on Sunday. The preacher at the morning ser- vice was the Vicar—the Rev. H. V. Pigot. The choir sang the anthem "Ye shall dwell in the land" (Stainer). The Rev. E. S. Jackson (vicar of Great Sankey) Preached in the evening. The anthem "Honour the Lord" (Stainer) was well sung by, the choir. There were large c•mgregations at each service and the collections amounted to £l3</p>
        <p>The services were continued on Thursday evening when the preacher was the Rev. Canon Greenall, rector of Grappenhall. The lessons were read by the Revs. T. M. R. Young Hughes and J. Forbes, and the prayers by the Vicar. The anthem " Honour the Lord " (Stainer) was well sung by the choir, Nir.J.J.Lyth,goe, accompany- ing on the organ. The solo parts were taken by Mr. Sherlock, bass, and Mr. Parkinson, tenor. The decora- tions were much admired, and had been carried out un- der the superintendance of Miss Westwell. FARNWORTH PARISH CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The harvest thanksgiving services were continued at St. Luke's Church, Farnworth, on Sunday, when there was a celebration of the Holy Communion at 8.15 a.m. At the 10.30 a.m. service there was a large congregation, and the Rev. W. Hodgson, M. A., vicar of Widnes, preached. In the afternoon an address was &amp;livered to the Sunday school scholars and frierids by the Vicar, the Rev. J. Wright Williams. The pulpit in the evening was occupied by the Hey. T. Gibson Smith, M.A., rector of Halewood. The collections amounted to about £27.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>ROLY TRINITY CHURCET.</head>
        <p>ROLY TRINITY CHURCET.</p>
        <p>The Harvest Thanksgiving services at Holy Trinity Church, Sankey-st., were continued on Sunday. The preacher at the morning service was the Rev. H. L. 'Mil- lard, assisted by the Rev L. B. Radford. A childien's service was held in the church in the afternoon when the Rev. E. C. E. Carleton conducted. The preacher at the evening service was the Rev. L. J. T. Darwall, 8.D., of Walton, assisted by the Rev. E. C. E. Carleton (vicar). The anthem " All thy works praise thee 0 God" was well sung by the choir under the conductorship of Mr. Evans. There were large congregations at the services and the collections_ were in aid of the Day School.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>-CAIRO-STREET CHAPEL:.</head>
        <p>-CAIRO-STREET CHAPEL:.</p>
        <p>Harvest thanksgiving services were held on Sunday at the Unitarian Chapel, Cairo-street. The chapel had been tastefully decorated with flowers and fruit by the ladies of the congregation. In the evening the pastor, the Rev. W. H. Drummond, preached to an overflowing congregation from the words " Then Jesus beholding him loved him," Mark x. 21, euforcing the lesson oi the need of sensitiveness to all that is good and beautiful in the world and human life. The anthem. "Sing a song of praise" (Stainer), was very effectively rendered by the obeli.. MOORE WESLEYAN CHAPEL.</p>
        <p>On Sunday the annual harvest thanksgiving services were held in the Wesleyan Chapel, when large congrega- tions were present b.th morning and evening. The sermon at the morning service was preached by the Rev. J. Kirtlan, of %Warrington, and in the evening by Mr. F. Wright. The chapel presented a neat appearance, hav- ing been tastefully decorated with corn, fruit and flowers by Miss Dutton, Mrs. J. Fairclough, and others. Suit- able hymns were rendered by the choir, and during the evening service the anthem, " Praise the Lord, 0 my soul" (Simper), was sung. Mr. J. Banner was at the harmonium.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>CROFT. •</head>
        <p>CROFT. •</p>
        <p>The harvest thanksgiving services were continued in Christ Church, Croft, on Sunday, when there were good congregations, especially in the afternoon. The Rev. JAseph Hare, of Poolstock, Wigan, intoned the prayers at the morning service, and the Rev. J. D. Llewellyn, of St. John's, Pemberton, read the special lessons. The Rector of the parish (the Rev. H. I. Wadeson) intoned the litany. The preacher was the Rev. Mr. Llewellyn. At the afternoon service the preacher in the morning intoned the first portion of the prayers, and the Rector the latter portion. The special lessons were read by Captain Withington. of Culcheth Hall. The collections for the three services, which were in aid of church expenses, amounted to £lO 17s. 7d. BOLD-ST. WESLEYAN CHAPEL. . . .</p>
        <p>The annual harvest festival in ccnnection with the above named Wesleyan Chapel was held on Sunday morning and evening. The chapel was tastefully deco- rated by lady members of the congregation, including Miss Collins, Miss Watkins, Miss Roff, Miss Weaver, Miss Piga. Miss Perkins, etc., and the work reflected great credit upon them. The chief part of the decorations was round the pulpit and in the communion rail. This portion of the chapel was completely filled with fruit, flowers, vegetables, and also a small haystack, the whole having a fine ap- pearance. Belcw the gallery also there were lines of leaves and flowers round the body of the chapel. The preacher in the morning was the Rev. • Owen Watkins. who took for his text St. John 12.24, "Verily, verily, I say unto you, except a grain of wheat fall into the ground and die, it abideth alone, but if it die, it bringeth forth much fruit." He said that when Christ was cruci- fied everything seemed lost. Death seemed to imply weakness. incapacity, and failure, and even His disciples shared this idea. But this was only the beginning of His work. Looked at superficially, nature seemed to say that death was ruin, but looked at closer, death Was not a failure, death was not the end, it was the beginning of endless succesions of life. If the grains of corn were kept out of the ground this year, there would be a mighty famine in the land next year. In the Same man: nor, many a man exerted far more influence by his death than he had ever done by his life, and Christ by His death and resurrection bad yielded much fruit, and was still doing so almost 1,900 years after that event.—The congregation during the morning service was very good, and during the course of the service, the choir rendered an anthem "Thou crowne.t the year" in an excellent style. The evening preacher was the Rev. John Kirtlan who also preached a sermon suitable so the occasion. The congregation was very large, and the choir sang the anthem "Praise the Lord, 0 Jerusalem" the solo parts being taken by Mr. L. Richardson and Miss Rawlin. They also repeated the anthem they had sung in the morning. After service, a great part of the congrega- tion remained behind to view the decorations more closely. The fruit and vegetables were sold on Monday night and the proceeds were given to the central relief committee for the distress in the town.</p>
        <p>RUPTURE CURED.—S. J. 8 HUMAN, Hernia Specialist, I 64, Chancery-lane. London. sends Ids hook on rupture post free. Branch Chambers, 26, King-st., Illanchester.—AdvS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>REVISION COURTS.</head>
        <p>REVISION COURTS.</p>
        <p>PENKETEL Mr. J. T. Foard. revising barrister, attended at the Crown Inn, Penketh, to hear the claims and objections for Penkedi and the surrounding districts. The Liberals were represented by Mr. Shores of Whines, and the Con. servatives by Mr. Holland Owen, of Rainhill, and Mr. Mr. Maudsley. The process of signing and correcting the lists for the different districts occupied a length period. Nothing, however, transpired of public interest.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON—NEWTON DIVISION. On Saturday evening, Mr. James T. Foard, revising barrister, attended the parade-room at the police-station to revise the lists of voters (Newton Parliamentary Divi- sion) for Warrington, Poulton-with-Ftarnhead,Martins- croft, and Rixton-with-Glazebrook. The Conservative, were represented by Mr. Holland Owen, of Rainhill,and the Liberals by Mr. Shores, of Widnes. The assistant overseers present were Mr. James DAinteth,of Woolston; Mr. George Brimelow, Houghton, Mr. Joseph Lythgoe, Rixton ; and Mr. Joseph L.Tunstall,Warrington. The pro- ceedings were of a very formal character, and occupied about half an hour only.</p>
        <p>BILLINGE. In the afternoon the voters lists for Billinge higher end, BiHinge Chapel end and Winstanley were revised. There were no cases of interest, and this completed the revision. Mr. Owen at the close tendered to the revising barrister on behalf of himself and Mr. Shores their best thanks for the courtesy, consideration and patience with which his honour had conducted the revision of the lists and compiled the register, and after referring to certain legal points that he raised in the interests nf the party he had the honour to represent, because they were good substantial points, he heartily thanked him on behalf of his party.—Mr. Shores endorsed what had been said and dwelt upon the despatch.and courtesy shown by his honour. The Revising Barrister replied thanking the representatives and thought the way in which the revision had been conducted, had been in every way satisfactory, and the points raised were well worth consideration.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>GOLBORNE.</head>
        <p>GOLBORNE.</p>
        <p>The revision of the voters lists for the Newton parlia- mentary division was continued at Golborne on Wednes- day by Mr. J. C. Foard. Mr. W. Holland Owen, Mr. W. Mawdsley, registration agent and Mr. T. Gregory, district secretary, represented the Conservatives and Mr. H. B. Shores the Liberals.</p>
        <p>A "FAKE."</p>
        <p>William Sandham claimed an occupation vote in respect of 18, School Terrace. The claim was objected to by the Conservatives, on the ground, that notwith- standing a memorandum from the Golborne Mill Co., and the imoduction of the rent book, the claimant had not occupied the house the qualifying period. It was pointed out that no date was given as to the commence- ment of tenancy, and the revising barrister despatched a messenger to the mill for this information. On the messenger returning it was found that the date was September sth, 1892. A rent book was then produced showing that the applicant had lived at another address at the date given and the vote was disallowed, the barrister remarking that it was evidently a " fake."— The other business was purely routine. NEWTON-LE-WILLOWS. ..• • •</p>
        <p>. _ Mr. J. T. Foard, revising barrister, held a court at the Town Hall, Newton-le-Willows, on Friday, for the pur- pose of revising the voters lists for the townships of Newton, Earlestown, Winwick with Hulme' Southworth with Croft, Middleton and Arbury, and Burtonwood. Mr. W. Holland Owen, of Rainhill, with Mr. W. Mawd- slev, registration agent, represented the Conservatives, and Mr. H. B. Shores,Liberal agent for Widnes,appearea for thy Liberals. The following assistant overseers were also present, Messrs. H. Houghton, Brimelow, E. C Cooper and J. Forster. The business was mostly of a routine character, owing doubtless to the fact that the Liberals had made no preparation whatever, for the revision and not a single written claim or objection had been made on their behalf. James Gabriel of Mercer's Cottages, Burtonwood, was objected to by the Conservatives, on the ground of insufficient occupation. A certificate was put in by the applicant from Mr. Southern, secretary to :he Collins Green, Colliery Company, showing that Gabriel had lived in the house since July 18, 1891. The claim wa, allowed, and on the applicant ap?lying for expenses,the revising barrister made an order for the payment of 3s. 6d. Phillip Edwards also of Mercer's Cottages, Burtonwood was objected to on the same grounds, but on a certifi- cate being produced showing that he had occupied the house since February 27th, 1892, the vote was allowed. On a claim for expenses, the revisiag barrister made an order for 35. 6d. An ownership claim on behalf of the Rev. J. Carson, rector of Newton, was put in, in respect of the rectoral tithes, but the Liberal agent objected to the claim as a bad one, on the ground that the particulars in the fourth column were wrongly stated. The source of the tithes being given as from "Lord Newton and others," instead of the " street, lane, or other place" and the objection was allowed to stand</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>80IITHWORTH_WITH OF.</head>
        <p>80IITHWORTH_WITH OF.</p>
        <p>An ownership claim on behalf of the Rev. ff. 1. Wade- son, rectgr of Croft,in respect to rectoral tithes was allowed.</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN.-OWNEUSIIIP CLAIMS.</p>
        <p>The Conservatives brought forward several ownership claims,to which the Liberal agent took objection, on the ground that he had not seen the claims, and many of them might be pre-dated. Ho understood some of them were dated the 10th and 11th July, and a recent decision was to the effect that the claim must nct be signed prior to the qualifying pericd. He asked to see the &amp;aims before proceeding.—Mr. Owen said he thought it was perfectly clear that it was necessary the claim should not be taken before the 15th July,bet a a rule of law was that any time on or before the 20th July, and lie read a section of the Act in support of his contention.—The Revising Barrister said that only described that it must not be later than the 20th July, but supposing a man in January said he was the owner of certain r roperty, knowing that he was going to sell it the next day, how could he say that lie was going to be the owner on the 15th July? Be was not in a position in June, April, or March to say that he would be the occupier on the 15th July. A thousand things might happen.— Mr. Owen : Then it is left to my friend to raise an objection to the property having been sold ? The Barrister : You must show that your claim is gocd, or it cannot be good on the face of it.—Mr. Owen said that if he had received notice of objection the owners would have been there to subst vitiate their claims. If his Honour was against him on the point,he would show that he had no power to expunge from any list the name of any person not objected to.—The Barrister : That is the list.—Mr. Owen : The overseers publish the list, and after the Revision Court comes the register.—The Barris- ter : That does not refer to a list of new claims.—Mr. Owen asked for a case.—The Barrister : Certainly. The claims were then gone through and it was found that many of them were dated before the 15th July, and they were in every case disallowed. Objection was also taken to several occupiers' claims, by the Liberal agent, on the ground that the claimants were not present to substantiate their claims, and the objections were upheld. A court was held at six o'clock in the evening for the convenience of working men. An occupier's claim on behalf of Richard Digweed, Superintendent of the Reformatory was objected to by the Conservatives, on the ground that he occupied the same tenement as his father, both living at the Reformatory, and that he -should have claimed as a lodger.—The objection was upheld. This was all the business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>TERRIBLE DEATH AT ST.</head>
        <p>THE TERRIBLE DEATH AT ST.</p>
        <p>B ELEN S.</p>
        <p>"STARVING AMID WEALTH AND FOOD." Brighoustp, county coroner, held an inquest at St Helens Town Hall, on Saturday, on the body of Thomas Etheridge, widower, aged thirty-six years, a glees flattener, whose dead body was found on Friday morning on a burning cinder heal) at Messrs. Baxter's Copper Works, Parr. Mary Alice Etheridge, daughter, said her father had been out of work, and had left the children with their grandfather. She had not see him for a fortnight. Thomas Houghton. 9, Gilbert street, stated that deceased lodged with him four nights, hut left more than a week ago. Witness met him on Wednesday night, and he said he had had one cup of coffee that day, and had no money. During the four days that Etheridge lodged with him he had no money and was without food. He tried to get some meat outside, but witness did not know whether he got any. The Coroner said that was all the evidence, and he did not think they would have much difficulty in arriving at a verdict. The poor ' fellow had been out of work and had been in such straits as could scarcely he imagined whilst surrounded by wealth and food. He had had no mon^y for three or four days, and was practically without food, On Wed- nesday night he told his friends that he bad only had a cup of coffee. A Juryman asked whether deceased had any relatives—father or mother. Inspector Steele said his father was in court. The Juryman raid he would like to know why he had not assisted him. The father, William Etheridge, was then called, and said he saw his son on Wednesday morning. He knew that he was out of work, but did not know that he was in such great poverty and distress. Witness did not speak to his son on Wednesday. His son had never applied to him for relief; but he had kept him for live months at Cie com- mencement of 1892. He then got work, but was thrown out again on account of drinking, and witness kept him for a further seven months. He should certainly have assisted his son on Wednesday if he had known lie was in want. (Witness here broke down and had to leave the box.) A Juryman. There is no doubt that if the son had applied for relief, the father would have given it to him. The Coroner: Even if he bad faults ? The jury retired, and in a few minutes returned, the Fore- man annoucing that they were all,agreed on a verdict of " Accidentally suffocated."'</p>
        <p>The Medical Annual advisee Practitioners to remember that when recommending COCOAS as food and beverage tor Invalids, the name CADBURY on any packet of Coco.' is it guArantee of purity.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
