      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>WOMEN NUISANCE INSPECTORS.</head>
        <p>WOMEN NUISANCE INSPECTORS.</p>
        <p>ACTION OF THE ST. HELENS CORPORATION. At the fortnightly meeting of the St. Helens Corpora- tion Health Committee, on Wednesday. Councillor John Ferster presiding, Dr. Robertson submitted a report which, among other things, dcalt with the appointment of women nuisance inspectors in such towns as St. Helens, which had large working-class populations. He Qaid he was quite satisfied that one woman inspector in St. Helens would be of considerable use, confining her attention specially to the very poor districts, where people required very careful supervision.—The Chairman said they had had women inspectors on special occasions such as epidemics. There were nurses belonging to the churches in the town, and they were doing a very valu- able work. He felt certain that it would be money well spent, and the inspector would be atle to give good dv ice to people who would be glad to receive it.—The Mayor understood that she would be appointed more to look to cleanliness than as a sick nurse.—The Chairman said that was so. He would move that a female inspec- tor be appointed, and that the medical officer be instructed to advertise for one and report at a future meeting.—The Mayor seconded, and this was agreed to</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD SCHOOL CONTINUA-</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD SCHOOL CONTINUA-</p>
        <p>TION CLASSES. DISTRIBUTION OF PRIZES.</p>
        <p>The fourth session of these Classes was inaugu- rated on Tuesday evening, when the prizes won by the students last winter were distributed by the Rev. Canon Wood, vicar. Mr. James Pritchard, chairman of the School Board, presided and besides Is large number of youths and their parents, there were present the Rev. Dr. Gesteldi, Messrs. E. W. Flumphreye, G. Moss (clerk), J. Moulton, head teacher, and the assistant teachers. The Chairman said he was pleased to meet the studnts and to express, on behalf of the members of the Board, *etisfaction at the result of the classes last session as evidenced by the large number of prizes for distribution. When these classes were first established some doubt was expressed as to their success, but he thought they had now passed the stage ot experiment and were a proved success. If the youths of Runcorn came forward and ap- preciated the advantages laid before them in that con- tinuation school, there was no doubt that the school would be of a permanent character. The students had considerable freedom in the choice of subjects and he hoped they would choose such subjects a; would give them the widest possible education. The Chairman pointed out that there would be no examination on the close of the session, but the inspector would make periodical visits to the school and witness the quality of the work done; consequently it was very important that they should attend well and work diligently during the coming session. Mr. Moss then read the report, of H.M. Inspector for the last session, which has been previously published. Tee Inspector remarked that the school was taught with zeal and ability and the results of the examination in both elementary and class subjects were highly satis- factory. Mr. Moulton explained the principle upon which the prizes had been awarded and alluding to the coming ses- sion, said the work would be made as attractive and use- ful as possible. The Chairman said it was probable that prizes would be awarded at the end of the session, but not upon the same basis as at present, namely the number of passes. The prizes woilld probably be for punctuality and regu- lar attendance. To those who were looking forward to joining the science and art classes he would impress upon them the necessity of acquiring arithmetic and algebra, because these subjects were indispensable for making calculations in scientific subjects. The Rev. Canon Wood said it cave him great pleasure to come and give the prizes. It was pleasing to give away things wherr one had not paid for them. Perhaps if he had paid for them be should not have been so anxious to distribute them. But as they came out of the pockets of the ratepayers there was no doubt a great amount of satisfaction in distributing them. But he believed that every ratepayer who knew what he ought to know and svflo thought as lie ought to think, would be delighted that a fee. of the pennies were going to he used in such an admirable way as this in encourag- ing the continuation of education. The continuation of educatinn that was going on with it after about 13 or 14 years of age, was a very important matter and many people felt it to be a pity that our boys and girls, just when education was opening its door that they were beginning to understand something, they should find the door shut in their faces. These continuation schools were now encouraged by the Government and by right thinking people becau;e they helped our young folks to go on with their education. He did not leave school until he was nineteen years and five Months old; then he had three years and a half at the University which brought him to 23 years and he really thought he had been getting educated ever since. Education was not finished until the end of their lives. These continuation clicirals were very useful, and helped brilliant and clever people to come to the top, which was a great encouragement to everybody who eten ed an evening elisol. Mr. altindella, was a member in toe present rzovernment, who had ezently been vice-president of the Council on Eancation gained alinest the whole of his education at a night school in SheffiAd. He therefore recommended those who felt they had the requisite force and energy to enter the night schools now opened. Go forward to the technical classes, or when they got the Technical Iretitute, which he hopsd they would push on there and then there was no telling what they might become. They might become chairmen of the School Board of this import int town; end that, was a position of which anybody might be proud. The real else of edncation was not to put people above their fel- lows hut t s open out all kinds ot avenues of interest 'which male life a much richer and fuller thing.—The Bev. Canon then distributed the prizes a; follows : FIRST CLASS PRIZMI for passing in five subjects:— John %V. Gleave, Nicholas Sheave Charles Burrows, John Gatley, George teed, Harold Smith, Thos. Leach, Thos. Corzons, Artllur Leath, James H. Littlemore, Harold Yates; Richard Pewee% Ernest Gleave, Frantt Burrows, Sam Blundell, Joseph Percival, Thos. Milling- ton. Thos. messes, Win. Hopwood,Fred Wilkinson,Chas. Littler, Win. Litt'er, Water Jones, James Jones, Syd- ney Watson, Alf. Parr:, Henry Holt. SECOND Cuss Paiz.es for passing in four subjects : Fred W. Smith, Wesley Coventry. Chas. Jones Matthias Weiser+, George Forre-ter, Wm. Cook,Albert ch, Her- hert Newton, Win: Allan, Daniel Catterall, G. W. Holt, Thomas Weston. CIigiTIFCATES fr passing in three stibjects :—Solin T. Briscoe, Redly Powell, Chas. Watson, El. Hewitt, Chas. Baker, 'Dina. Plant. The following receipeil certificates from the Lanca- shire and Cheshire Union of Institutes for successfully passing in Arithmetic :—Daniel Ca!t-rall, Thos. A. Millinger, Jehn W. Gleave, Chas. Epee, Frank Burrows, Chas. Burrows, Win. Littler, Thee. Briscoe, Thomas Curzens, H. Smith, W. IT. fropwssad, E. Gleave, Thos. Leach, Chas. Watson, Harold Yates, S. Blundell, Jas. H. Littlernore, F. W. Wil'ainson. Sydney Wharton, George Reed, Wm. Conk, Geo. 11. Marsh. I Received Certitica'es from Lancashire and Cheshire 'Union of Institutes for success in Enelish Grammar ' Joseph Percival, Wm. Littler, Win. ',Cook, Wesley Coventry.'" On the motion of Mr. Moulton, seconded by the Rev. Dr. Gastaldi, a vote of thanks to Canon Wood for his, services. In reply the rev. centicman said he had learned that e was under a. misapprehension when he stated that the money for the prizes. That was not so. The Government grant; and the fees had covered the cost of working the school, including payment for the prizes. The proceedings then terminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>NOTABLE CYCLIST.</head>
        <p>A NOTABLE CYCLIST.</p>
        <p>Mr. Lawrence Fletcher, who recently broke the 1,000 miles record, and thereby accomplished one of the moat remarkable performances ever authenticated in the his- tory of cyclinst,is not an," everyday youog man" by any means. Mr. Fletcher, who is on the shady Ride of 31, has traded in South Africa. In recent years he lived in. Liverpool, where he %cm the leading spirit in cycling matters till within the last few months, when he took up his residence in Cork. Next to Mills, he is peril:3p. the greatest long-distance rider of the day, and his recent failures to beat Land's E3d to John o'Groats record were largely doe to Rd verse weather and ill luck. In his leisure momenta Fletcher is a sicvelist—on the lines Of Rider Ilarvird—and Ills books, " Into the Unknown," and "Zero, the Slaver," have been favour- ably noticed.</p>
