        <head>flroftssfonal.</head>
        <p>flroftssfonal.</p>
        <p>ALB and FEMALE NURSES INSTITUTION, 70 ill Ilope-et., Liverpool. Betab. 1878.—For reliable' Attendants. Mental, Medical, and Monthly Nurses. Massage, 4447 QZ WleirsPh. Kusses." Liverpool. lelephone, 51‘.</p>
        <p>THE importance and urgency of the A Pressing unemployed question could not be Problem.</p>
        <p>much more forcibly stated than in the report which has been issued this week by a Committee of Inquiry in Liverpool. This com- mittee WAS appointed in May last by a conference held under the presidency of the LORD MA.von of the great seaport, and its inquiries have been extensive and eYhaustive. It has come to the very unwelcome conclusion that "at no time has the condition of the unemployed in Liverpool been more distressing than to-day." It is esti- mated that, in the different unskilled occupations from seven or eight per cent .up to a Fill one-third, of the persons engaged in them are either out of work altogether, or only partially employed; while in the skilled trades the unemployed average from nil up to ten per cent. We are further told that Liverpool is not exceptional in regard to the distress amongst its workers. To a greater or less extent, practically all the cities and larger towns of the United Kingdom are similarly afflicted, and in many centres of population on the Continent the treatment of the unemployed is found to be one of the most pressing problems. The causes of the want of employment are stated under six heads:— (1) Normal surplus labour; (2) the sending of work elsewhere which ought io be done here ; (3) immigration from other places is always proceed- ing; (4) the application of machinery and dis- placement of hand labour: (5) strikes and lockop ts here 11121 elsewhere; and (6) want of consideration</p>
        <p>on the part of employers in the hiring of labour. The more statement of these " causes "is sufficient to show how difficult it is to find adequate reme- dies ma ordinary lines. "Immigration from other places," for instance. What does that mean but that, bad as Liverpool is, there are other places in an even worse condition, or, at all events, that there are people who think they may improve their position by going there. Then, as to "the appli- cation of machinery and displacement of hand la- bour,''how suggestiveis such a statement as that when given as a cause of aistress. Machinery enor- mously increases the total national production of wealth, yet at the same time impoverishes largo masses of people ! It surely cannot be said that an industrial system which brings about such para- doxical and calamitous results has reached its most perfect development. The " remedies " which are suggested by the Committee will, it is to be feared, for the most part prove futile and ineffective. The City Council of Liverpool is re- commended to give its work as far as possible, to local men. That for a time might lessen the local distress, but from a national point of view there will be no benefits. What is given to Liver- pool men will be taken from those in other dis- tricts, so that the total sum of the' unemployed will not be reduced at all. And, then, if other cities and towns follow the example set them, as they probably will in time, Liverpool will be no gainer. In the vast system of "give and take "in the commercial and industrial world. Liverpool at the present time probably " takes " as much as she "gives." Another recommenda- tion is that a labour bureau should be formed—a good enough proposal in its way, but when the labour market is already over-stocked, the mere duplication and re-duplication of machinery in the shape of bureaus does not increase the demand for workmen. The suggestion that the unemployed should be engaged to reclaim waste land, and to cultivate it after it has been reclaimed, has a more hopeful look about it. In such a scheme as this they would at least be occupied in producing what may be termed the primary necessaries of life, which could be utilised for their own maintenance. 1 But whatever plan may be adopted in Liverpool it is getting quite apparent that the problem of the ! unemployed is one which will yet tax the wits of our most capable statesmen.</p>
        <p>IN his speech at Elinburgli on Parish Councils. Wednesday night Mr. GLADSTONE referred to the Parish Councils Bill as one of the measures which he hoped to pass through Parliament this session. That is a hope which will be re-echoed very loudly in many parts of England. The prospects of the Bill are at the present time, however, just a little doubtful. The Opposition have become, by continuous practice, so proficient in the arts of obstruction, that one cannot calculate upon the passage of any measure within a reasonable time, if they are bent upon opposing it. But will they oppose this Bill openly and determinedly? We all remember how at the time it was introduced several Tories and Unionists preferred, in language almost as cordial as that of the Liberal themselves, that they liked its provisions immensely, and they declared that it was so good that it should even take , precedence of the Home Rule Bill. But that was several months ago. The Tories knew that Home Rule was the first dish in the Parliamentary menu, and they openly expressed the expectation that while that was being discussed many things would happen—such a thing, for instance, as a collapse of the Government. In that event, they would be able to take full credit for all their fine democratic sentiments in regard to the Parish Councils Bill, while the Bill itself would be numbered with the "slaughtered innocents." But as—from the Tory point of view—ill luck would have it, the Government has not collapsed, and it is fully intended to push the Bill forward when Parliament meets again. This places many of the Tory members in a position of some little difficulty. Shall they persist in their commendation of the Bill, or shall they throw consistency to the winds and openly oppose it? The quality of consistency has, it should be remembered, been lately made into a kind of fetich by the Unionists. These bewildered politicians are at present left without guidance. Their leaders are either absolutely silent or enigmatical. We notice that LORD GROSS—who is not much of of a leader after all—advises the Tories not to oppose the" principle " of the Perish Councils Bill. But that is practically no advice at all. The Tories never oppose the "principle " of any measure of reform. They always profess to have the highest respect for the " principle "of such measures ; it is the concrete embodiment of the prisciple that they ure opposed to. It will be seen, therefore, that Lord CROSS'S advice is char- acteristic ;it lacks pith and substance. On the other hand, it is said that the country clergy, especially in the more rural counties of England, have conceived a dislike for the Bill ; and the nearer they get to it the less they like it. It is pretty certain that the other village magnate— the Squire—will like it no better than the parson, and we must not forget, that these are the two pillars of rural Conservatism. On the whole, therefore, taking all the elements of the situation into account, it i 3 not at all likely that the Parish Councils Bill will meet with perfectly plain sail- ing. While speaking in respectful terms of the " principle," the Tories will, we venture to predict, do their best to mutilate if not to destroy the provisions of the Bill.</p>
        <p>THE House of Lords will not feel Mr. Gladstnne g ene opposition o t s th d • •t.. and the Peers. strengthened Home Rule by the interesting histo- rical sketch which Mr.GraosroNE gave in his great speech at Edinburgh on Wednesday evening. Nor will the opponents of Home Rule in the country be encouraged in their reliance upon the resisting powers of the hereditary Chamber. As Mr.GLAD- STONE showed, the Peers have, since they rejected the great Reform Bill in 1831,been hostile to every groat scheme of public advancement. But'their hostility has been tempered with fear, and in the end they have always yielded to the will of the House of Commons—sometimes, indeed, with ignominious alacrity. As they have always done hitherto, it may, with some confidence, be antici- pated that they will do again. Mr. GLADSTONE held out no threat to the House of Lords. The most violent language contained in the speech was a iyissage quoted from an oration delivered by Mr. CHAMBERLAIN in 1884 :—" The House of Lords for a hundred years has," said the Mr. CHAMBERLAIN of nine years ago, "never contributed one iota to popular liberties or popular freedom, or done any- thing to advance the common weal. During that time it has protected every abuse and sheltered every privilege. It has denied justice and delayed reforms. It is irresponsible without independence, obstinate without courage, arbitrary without judgment, and arrogant without knowledge." j</p>
        <p>That sample of Birmingham eloquence was, it need hardly be said, received with much laughter, and Mr. GLADSTONE, with genial irony, disclaimed the ability to have "written or composed anything 00 forcible." While refraining from anything in the nature of nainatory language, the PRIME MISISTRIt very plainly hinted that there is no intention to allow the nation to be baffled by a phalanx of five hundred Peers. He has left open a line of retreat for the Lords. If they do not take advantage of it, the cry for "ending " in preference te "mending" will be raised with renewed em- phasis.</p>
        <p>WE publish this week the last ofl An Abattoir for Warrington. a series of articles on tint' slaughterhouses of Warringtour which have been contributed to our columns brill able and well-informed correspondent. That he has proved his case in favour of a central abatteit as opposed to a large number of small slaughter', houses situated in all kinds of unsuitable localities, will, we think, be fully and frankly admitted byall. who have read the articles. Indeed, the necessitY.j. for the abolition of the slaughterhouses has for a, long time been admitted by most people who take' i an interest in the sanitary affairs of the town. many cases reform means expense; but an abattoi9 may be confidently expected to pay its wer if not to hand over an annual surplee: in relief of the rates. In Edinburgh, we were informed last week, the public absttol7 pays over annually the handsome sum of en! thousand pounds to the City funds. Not 0r1174 is a central place for the slaughter of animals f immense advantage from a sanitary and a humane point of view, but it is found from experiencet ) that when once established its advantages over the old primitive methods are thoroughly aPPrZ ciated by the butchers and others who It is, therefore, satisfactory to learn that the Corporation are including in the new Bill intend to promote in Parliament next year,po"i, to close the existing slaughter houses vrhee.2 abattoir, under municipal management, has ben established.</p>
        <p>of thei ,woro Wife 40 1</p>
