        <p>A WARRINOTONIAN'S EXPERIENCE. „ A Warrington workingman seuds us the folloil contribution :—By this the hop picking season Woo be drawing to a close. It was my luck to get..„,Oliiis a farm at Five Oaks Green, near Tunbridge Iv e`i; There were over 34,000 acres of hops this Yeeroo Kent; the next county had only about 7,000., -a-II of the growers had over 2,000 pickers. NeariY,,;s„ the pickers come from London,being costerinong'" hawkers and nearly all grades of people. tbe It is a very nice eight to see the hop gardens; - hops grow up poles that are stuck in the grelisy the hops growing at the top of the bines. The„rtcp is so many bushels per shilling, from 4 to 8 sce-',fe ing to the size of the hops. 1 saw a man and vir'l.o and children earn 10s. in a day. Some of the plek,.B have worked in the gardens from childhood, r'oe novice would only make about 10s. a week. of the pickers bring neighbours' children from don to pick. The farmers have place; built for " hoppers" to sleep in, and some of them sleeP,be barns, tents, &amp;c. All the cooking is done 00„"its open fires of wood which the farmers suppl)' (Aq" the gipsy style). There is a poor supply of foThol the village; the principal make-shift is bread °ea cheese and beer and spotted dog (American tine . meat). ber This is the place for seeing life; on the whotet to " are a good hearted class of people ; it is a tre(oo3o hear them sing all the latest London songs ill gardens. 01' One couple were married in London on the 51113.0% and on the same day the man brought his hlustif,ir bride down to pick hops and they put up in r sty. OO I saw two of the Yorkshire colliers who at up strike down there; they thought hops gr",,fed apple trees. One of them an Irishman,was Preill;dolf to pole puller; for this purpose they use a "race —a pole with a piece of iron the shape of mouth fastened to it. One day he lost his "—IsID and swore the hop dog had eaten it. TheY,er through a dispute and went away with the iitP" tion of making their way to Paris. ging Another couple of pickers who were together had a dispute Lft-tr dinner, one tellatings other it was his turn to dein up the dinner t"Aciall which consisted of a black quart can, an Arae'" beef tin and an old black lid with a big hole iO.O On Sunday the wooden legged barber came - to do a bit of trade outside the" pub." Tile4ritig' window is the looking glass to do your 15t7,7, th° Then there is the sweets man who conies tb?, garden every day and does a good trade vv" 10' children ; also the fish hawker, 4' ole e man, &amp;c. "raeBll The pickers are allowed to " sub " three ti , the week, half what they earn, and are paid off end of the picking. Some will have as much a and £6 to draw, others as low as ss. „bane The work is very healthy and quite a •;„,ths: from London life My experience of liopPing's; aoa it is right enough if you have plenty of 01°11.`,'"8Loae can pick for nothing and for anyone who i 8 A tbel broke." It is the shop keepers' harvest, ari"„ecer seem to think a covering of wire netting is „sat sary ever their goods. The Sunday's are Viol washing clothes and nutting in the 'ivocsiga.'n° re visit to the Wells or Paddock Wood. the many an old score payed cif at the close °d tvo picking, one fight resulted in a mother, 80 daughters being well scratched.</p>
