ine of 10s. and costs and finding toot:. The Prisoner : It's a good job it's always hectl. a l it won't be bread and water like it has</p>
        <p>A RAINBOW BY MOONLIGHT. A somewhat curious sight was observed in War- rington on Sunday night. At five minutes to eight after a shower of rain, the mean shone out very clearly, and in the sky could be plainly observed a complete rainbow, extending from east to west. The tints were not discernable, but the streaks which formed the bow were of a bright silvery colour. It remained clear to the eye for about five minutes and then gradually disappeared. THE WATB IL POLO CHAMPIONSHIP. T - e Warrington Swimming Club are having another grand swimming gala on Thutsday next at the Warrington baths when there will be a two lengths youths' race, a two lengths members' race, and a two lengths maiden race. In addition to these events, the final for the Water Polo Championship for the " Athletic News "Challenge Shield will also be fought out. The two teams who will be matched against each other will be Osborne-street S.C. Man- chester, who are the finest combination in England, having in their team, J. H. Tyers, the famous amateur champion, and J. IL Hicks, the well- known international and Lancashire county, and Leaf-street S.C. Manchester, who took part in aie semi final a few weeks since at the baths. This team contains Alf Smith. the Mersey champion. There will also be a four lengths race (open). The Mayoress will present the prizes at the close. THE WARRINGTON CYCLING CLUB. On Thursday night the most successful "smoker," the above club has ever held, took place at the Nor- ton Arms Hotel. About 80 members and friends were present including representatives from the Widnes C.C., Wallasey Wheelers C.C., and other local clubs. The chair was occupied by the Presi- dent, T. Morris, Esq., who opened the proceedings by a brief speech. The programme for the evening was an excellent one. The sentimentalists were Mr. G. Parr and Mr. Voss, of Liverpool, and their songs were enthusiastically received. The humor- ous part of the evening was divided between Mr. J. CaMercott, who gave some grand comic songs in fine style, and Mr. J. Pal on, of Widnes,who received quite an ovation over some of his items. W. Whittle gave several songs in a pleasing manner, while Mr. W. H. Pedmore gave a grand selection on the flute from airs from the" Bohemian Girl." Mr. 11. Gallo- way ably accompanied. During th'b proceedings a humorous ventriloquial entertainment was given by Mr. A. Grounds, and the way he manipulated the dolls and his voice was a treat, and he received tre- mendous applause at. the finish of his exhibition. In an interval of the proceedings, Mr. T. Morris ex- pressed his pleasure at being present, and asked those present to give liberally to the collection which was in aid of the distress fund. Letters of apology were read by the hon. see. Mr. E. Pickavance, jun., from Messrs. P. Davies, 3. Lyon Whittle (Town Clerk), and H. Pierpoint (member for the borough). Mr. Pierpoint also sent 10s. to- wards the collection. The collection amounted to £42s, which is a very satisfactory sum. A vote of thanks to the artistes,was proposed by Mr. E. Picka- vance, jun., and seconded and supported by Mr. D. Davies and J. Critchle.y, and carried with acclama- tion. Mr. J. Caldecott and Mr. T. Morris suitably replied, and the proceedings closed at about eleven o'clock after a most enjoyable entertainment.</p>
        <p>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN AT RUNCORN.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>BY AN OCCASIONAL CORRESPONDENT.</head>
        <p>BY AN OCCASIONAL CORRESPONDENT.</p>
        <p>The fourth session of the Runcorn School Board Evening Continuation School was commenced on Tuesday evening, by the presentation of prizes to the successful students of last winter. Each year the results have been highly satisfactory, and it would be almost imilossible to wish for a better record than that read at the meeting referred to, for no fewer than 202 passes were obtained out of a possible 211. One particulary gratifying feature of the Continuation School from a ratepayer's point of view Is that the school has been self-supporting. A large number of students have joined for the pre- sent session. Up to the present the school has been confined to boys,now classes have been formed for girls, and there is reason to believe that the gentler sex will take full advantage of the op- portunity afforded them of widening their educa- tion.</p>
        <p>The Science and Art classes have also been in process of formation during the week and a very , considerable number of students have entered for ' the various subjects. Only classes will be formed where a sufficient number jointand as the real work of teaching practically does not commence until next week, those who desire to acquire knowledge, will do well to present themselves at once. The syllabus comprises the following subjects : Free- hand, and model drawing, geometr3, prospectus, ' and shading, machine construction, and drawing, ' building construction, applied mechanics, steam. physiography,chemistry, French, shorthand, sound light and heat, and dressmaking. One discourag- ing feature of the work hitherto has been the con- sidorable number of pupils who have not persevered with their studies to the close of the session and who have failed to present themselves for examin- ation. With a view to avoiding this condition of affairs in the future the committee have decided to offer prizes, which will be awarded on just and equitable principles, and it is to be hoped that the result desired will be achieved. It might be thought that the mere acquirement of knowledge for its own sake would be sufficient reward,but experience has taught that some further inducement is needed to act as a spur to the flagging energies of the students and it is to be hoped that the committee will have the satisfaction of finding that their generosity hag not been in vain.</p>
        <p>Still referring to education it is a cause for con- gratulation that Mr. Jol.d Holford has again com- menced his popular singing classes. The credential he has obtained himself from the Tonic Sol fa, and the large number of his pupils who succeed in passing the requisite examinations, is the best tes- timony to his ability as a teacher. About, nine hundred pupils have passed through his classes,and received a more or less complete knowledge of the sol fa system, and as music is the handmaid of art and of peace, great good cannot but have been effected in the majority of cases.</p>
        <p>The Relief Committee of Widnes is working harmoniously and energetically in the relief of the distressed, and as the Guardians of the Union admit have saved the rates from a somewhat severe drain. The work is divided and arranged as well as possi- ble and the duty of soup making, which is done in a large boiler at Messrs. Gossage's Soap Works, is entrusted to four gentlemen, of aldermanic propor- tions, and occasionally they mix the hard labour of preparing 800 gallons of soup with a little humour and jollity. A little joke was played upon them- selves on Thursday. A workman made four com- mon metal medals with copper centres, and incribed upon them were the words " Soup makers tosthe Borough Bench," together with the name of the recipient. A well known chemist in the district made the presentation in due and solemn form and the eloquent periods that fell from the lips of thv burley soup makers in acknowledgement would have astonished the Mayor had he chanced to have made his morning call at the time. Those soup makers now wear the medals on their watch chains and the rest of the Committee are green with envy</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 30. 1893:</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 30. 1893:</p>
        <p>. I would call evidence to ehtav that there was as much I carbonate of lime in the'river Wye, at the Isle of Man, or at Southport as there was in the Mersey in the neigh- bourhood of this brook. .Compared with the hundreds I of thousands of tone of solid matter sent into the river from Warrington, Garston, Liverpool, Birkenhead, and other places by sewerage schemes and from manufac- tories,the liquid mud sent in from these works was abso- lutely insignificant. The first witness called for the defence was Dr. F. Hurter, vice-president of the Society of Chemical Indus- try. He explained in detail the Chance process, and said that between 87 and 89 per cent, of the residue was carbonate of lime, 2 per cent, of it was coke, and 0.58 per cent, of it was sand. Thecartionate of lime was in very small particles, and, taking the very lowest figures, he had found that the flow ef the river at Widnes was 300,000,000 cubic feet per day. The amount of abso- lutely dry 'solid matter put into the river each day was eighty-one tons, and this was 1-I,Booth part of the quan- tity moved about by the tide during the same period. On the sth inst. witness took several surface samples of the solids in suspension found in the water above Run- corn Bridge. These solids dried at boiling heat con- tained 9i percent. of carbonate of lime. Witness then detailed the result of his analysis of sand at Hale Head, Grassendale, and other parts of the river, and showed that the percentages of carbonate of soda were very small. He also showed the bench how short a time it took for a quantity of carbonate of soda to dissolve in sea water obtained from New Brighton. The Court adjourned after sitting six hours. I The hearing of the case was resumed at 10-45 yester- day morning, when Dr. Hurter continued his evidence. He said carbonate of lime was found in all rivers. Thirty- five tons of the refuse from the Chance process hal been discharged into the river, and of this 87 per cent, was carbonate of lime, and if it were not' dissolved by the , river water,it must be very widely diffused. There was , not a larger quantity of carbonate of time found in the ' Mersey. Theortically, there was enough carbolic acid 1 gas in a single tide in the Mersey to dissolve 7,440 tons of carbonate of lime,if it were thoroughly diffused, but I practically, it was probable that only half of this quan- tity would be dissolved. Cross-examined: All the ingredients of "Chance mud" , were soluble except coke and sand. The percentage of these ingredients was about 4 percent.,and if 500 tens of the mud was put into the river,vritness admitted that 20 tons af it would not he dissolved. The Bench than retired and after an absence of 20 I minutes returned into court. The Chairman said The bench are of opinion (though not unanimous) that the I summonses worded " so as to tend to the injury or I obstruction of the navigation " should be dismissed. We I have done this with the idea of getting the case as short I as we can. The information wnen these words come in will be dismissed ; the others are not dismissed. 4 Mr.'Waltot : There will be no evidence offered on that point ; it hat been admitted already. He asked the bench to state a case. The Chairman said the C.unpany would be fined LIO and costs in each of the Informations not containing the above mentioned name. . . .</p>
        <p>'I he other summonses were dismissed with costs. Mr. Carver said he would appeal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>ALLEGED POLLUTION OF THE MEMEY</head>
        <p>ALLEGED POLLUTION OF THE MEMEY</p>
        <p>AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>--- On Thursday. at Widnes Petty Sessions, before Mr. W. Is M eChiee, Mr. P. A. Stapleton Bretherton and Mr. J. Birchen, the United Alkali Co. were sum- moned by the Upper Mersey Conservators for pol- luting the Mersey. Mr. B. Carey, secretary of the Company, appeared and the company were prolessioaalie-, represented by Mr. Walton, Q.C., and Mr. Stuart Deacon, and the I\l ereey Conservators I were represented by Mr. Carver. There were also present Mr. R. K. M uepratt, J.P.. and other chiefs of the Alkali Company. Mr. GirVer Said Ito appeared for the acting conserva- tor, Admiral Richards, who proceeded against the United Alkali Comp thy, in respect to their works at Widnes, which were formerly known as Alessr,. Golding Davies's works, and the other known as Messrs. Hall Bros., anti Sq:l'F'l4 works, The matter of whi-sit the conser- vators complained was, that trom thtee works a quant- ity of what was really alkali waste, or the refuse from alkali waste, was being sent into the Mersey to a very lu rye MO import int extent amounting from the two works to something like a thousand tons of stuff in a week perhaps not at the present moment bet this ea I hens going on during past months, and this WaS PO serious a ma. tor and so entirely contrary to what was allowed as regarded all other persons and so con- trary to the Act of parliament that it was absolutely necessary that notice should be taken of it, and if pos- sible be stopped. The summonses were somewhat differ- ent in form. In one case the words "so as to tend to the inquiry and obstruction of the Navigation." and in ; miter tlesll words wero ornitted.—Mr. Walt m contended that these words ought to be inserted in both cars.—Mr Carver seleuittecl that these words were net necessary in the seumil case. Continuing he said Messrs. Goldin, Davies's works had been employed for some three years vest in treating alkali waste, which contained sulphur, which could be recovered by a process known as " Chance's process." This was a process by whieh the alkali waste was mixed in water and with carbonic acid gas at a high pressure, with the result that part of the sulphur got detached from the waste and left a resiitial which was largely composed of carbonate of lime and some other substances. The iesidual was solid and would form a deposit. 'the practice of. these works had been to allow this solid matter to deposit in tanks, whence it was carried away and tipped on the marsh, but about spring of last year,he sm,posed for purposes of economy, the solid matter was kept suspended, by artificial means, long enough to be flushed through a shoot into the Marsh brook, into which the defendants had the right to draw. This brook had a considerable fall. and hundreds of tons, nay thousands of tons oh this stuff flowed into the estu- ary of the Mersey. It was of that the Conservatnre com- plained, and they said the defendants were casting into the Mersey the rubbish of their works, and that was an infringement of the statute 54 George 111., chap . 159, called an Act for the Better Regulation of Ports Harbours,roadsteads,basins,is channels, navigable rivers &amp;c:, in the United Kim:dom. Section 11 provided that if any earth rubbish rack or filth be emptied or ca-t in- to such ports, navigable rivers, dtc., "so as to tend to the injury or obstruction of the navigation thereof, or in any place or situation on shore, where tke same shall be liable to be waihed into the sea or into any such ports, roadsteads, havens, navigable rivers, either by ordinary or high tides by storms, or land floods shall pay for each such offence a sum not exceeding £10." The learned council argued that this clause constituted two direct offences—in the one case it was necessary that the rub- bish or refuse should tend to obstruct the navigation and in the other the offence was created by merely plac- ing it in such a situation where it was liable to be washed into the sea or tidal river. The United Alkali Co. hail offended in both respects. It was clear the stuff went into this stream, which had silted up very considerably, but he was informed that if it silted up al- together, that would not amount to a week's work at these works, so that it was quite clear there was only a small portion remained in the brook, and the rest went into the Mersey. Mr. Walton said the Company admitted as far as they could in a proceeding of this kind, that the refuse went into the river. • Mr. Carver proceeding said, he should he surprised if any professional witnesses came forward to show that the throwing of thousands of tons of heavy refuse into toe river had not a tendency to obstruct or injure the navigation of the river. It was not for the prosecution to show that it did injure the river, but that it had a tendency to do so. The matter had been thrashed out most elaborately by any num- ber of engineers in relation to the Manchester Ship Canal scheme, and it would be most astounding that anybody should conic there and say that it did not matter to the Navigation of the estuary of the Mersey that there should be adventitious matter thrown into it The emptying of one vessel load of gravel into the Mersey would tend to obstruct the navigation, and if the number were increased to a hundred vessels empty- ing, the obstruction would be real. The Mersey Docks and Harbour Board had most stringent regulations against any rubbish being sent into the estuary ; it must be taken right out to sea and no doubt had that body had jurisdiction up to Widnes, they would have found the same regulations in force. After reading a lengthy correspondence which had passed between Admiral Richards and the defendants, : Lieutenant Simpson, water bailiff to the Mersey Docks and Harbour Beard and to the Mersey. Conservators was called. He stated that on the 16th May last he visited the out-fall of Golding Davis works and there observed a thick fluid flowing into the brook. He took a sample. On the 28th July he again visited the spot with Mr. Francis 'rate, analytical chemist of Liverpool, and took another sample. He also took a sample of the water at the out fall of the brook into the river. The brook is channelled with timber and there is a wooden culvert into the river.—Cross-examined by Mr. Walton witness said he round deposits at the junction of the brook and the river. In the river itself or any part of its channel or banks he had not looked, but he saw the deposit being washed down the river. He had made ne investigation to ascertain what became of the deposit after it entered the river, but it would not, he thought. be washed into the sea. His view was that it would be found on the banks of the river. Be understood that the discharge had been going on for 18 months or two years, and so far as lie knew it had.had no appreciable effect in the channel. He could not give what the tidal flow above Runcorn Bridge was, nor could he give the total capacity of the estuary as he had not the figures with him. The tidal capacity of the Mersey was of very considerable importance to the navigation and that was one of the things the Conservators were afraid of in this case—a reduction of the total capacity of the river. So far as he knew the navigation between Widnes and Liverpool was as good as ever. Francis Henry Tate, of the firm of the Messrs. Norman Tate and Co., a nalyaticat chemists of Liverpool, spoke to the analysis of the liquids taken from the outfall at the works contained 25.15 per cent, of solid matter or 24,584 grains per gallon. In the second deposit from the same place there was 11.70 per cent, of solid matter in suspen- sion and 8.21 per cent, in solution. This sample was coming out rapidly and was warm. The sample taken from the outfall into the river showed solid matter in suspension 3.52, and in solution 4.40. At that point the deposit had become mixed with the water of the brook. The principal matter in suspension was carbonate of lime and the solid matter in the deposit at the works was the same substance. In the sample taken in the brook at the junction with the river, he found 19 per cent, of carbonate of lime and 11 per cent, of carbonic acid, besides a quautity of oxide of iron. In the solid matter taken at the output from the works he found 25 per cent, of carbonate of lime and 12 per cent, of carbonic acid.—Cross-examined witness said carbonate of lime was soluble in sea water to a small extent, and this would depend upon the amount of carbonic acid in the Sea to dissolve all the waste from the Widnes works. He would expect to find a quantity deposited on the banks of the river.—ln re-examination witness said carbonate of lime was frequently found in sand and he had himself examined sand from the Mersey and found carbonate cf lime in it. Thomas Bambury, chemical labourer, formerly employed at Golding Davis, said up to May last about 550 tons of alkali Waste per week was treated by the Chance process. The refuse was deposited into the sewer cennected with the brook and the refuse was equal in weight to the waste treated. George Junor,weigh clerk to Hutchinson's estate, gave evidence as to the amount of waete used by Hall Brothers and Shaw. In cress-examination by Mr. Walton he admitted that the dredgings from West Bank Dock were washed into the Marsh Brook. Mr. Walton admitted that the refuse from the Chance process at both Hall Bros. and Shaw and Golding Davies works, was discharged into the brook. This closed the case for the prosecution. In his address for the defence Mr. Walton said the deposits of alkali waste in Widnes had been a great source of r uisance through evolving sulphuretted hydrogen gas, through the sulphur it contained. Mr. Chance had invented a process by which the sulphur could be extracted with profit, and the process had been made compuleory by the authorities, and the manu- factures of alkali were no longer at liberty to deposit their waste in heaps round about the neielibourhood nor were they allowed to deposit the mud or residue from the process because when brought in contact with alkali waste it became accumulated and increased the generation of sulphuretted hydrogen. The refuse or mud from the Chance process was precipitated carbon- ate of lime, and being precipitated was in extremely fine particle and mixed with water it would flow away. It 0088 drained away into the brook and thence into the river without difficulty. He contended that this was no offence against the section of the Act of Parlia- ment relied upon. He contended that this waste was not rubbish within the meaning of the section, which was to prevent the deposit of solid things, whereas this mud was an inpalpable substance that would flow down the Mersey to the sea. It-was idle to argue that this substance would tend to the obstruction of the navigation of the river. 'the defend- ants had turned thousands of tons of this stuff into the Mersey, and they might expect it to have formed banks in the river somewhere, but the tidal capacity of the river was equal to its Car a:ity twenty years ago, and he</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>DISTRES IN WIDNES.</head>
        <p>THE DISTRES IN WIDNES.</p>
        <p>There is no diminution of the distress in Widnes. In fact, those who are actively working in the re- lief consider that the distress is rapidly increasing, at the same time the number of men unemployed is said not to be any greater, and the more numer- outs applications for relief may be taken as an indi- cation that the small sums set apart for a rainy day are failing. On Wednesday afternoon, a Meeting of the Catering Committee was held in the Council Chamber of the Town Hall, under the presidency of Mr. Councillor Christie. .Accounts amounting to £174 18.3 d. were passed for payment. A letter was , read from Mr. O. J. Moulton and Co.. Ltd., of Soho ,1 Mills, Macclesfleld,who forwarded 180 relief tickets I for distribution of the value of 3d. each, the goods ! to be obtained from the grocers named on the ticket It was decided that the grocers thus named should die. tribute the tickets. The Committee further decided to distribute on Sunday 1,040 hotpot's, being an increase of 140 over last Sunday. Attention was drawn to the heavy labour entaileo by the preparation and cooking of so large a number of hotpots, and time apathy shown by the unemployed in- Isn flog a helping hand. Mr. C. Heramingway stated that on Saturday evening there were many big able bodied men standing at the Market, and they would neither help to shift a tub or turn the potato machine. Some of them said they would sooner eat grass than do it. —Subsequently at a meeting of the General Relief Committee held under the presidency of Councillor F. Neil, the minutes of the Catering Com- mittee were confirmed.—The Finance Committee re- ported that' there was a credit balance at the bank of £3lB Is 2d. The donations received amounted to £2ll 55., and the weekly subscriptions promised were £l9B 9s. 11d. In the first week the subscriptions received totalled £l9B Os. 4d., second week £195 us. 3d., and the present week up to date £175 104. 3d. It was decided to allow the Ditton Relief Cominitte the weekly subscrip- tion of £335., given by the Lever works in considera- tion ot the people who worked in Widnes and lived in Ditton.—The Chairman said the cost of relief last week was £125 13s. 2d. ; this week the cost would be £l7O or an increase of £45. From this fact it was evident that the distress was rapidly increasing, and if it continued at the present rate, the balance in hand would soon be 1 absorbed.—Mr. G. Davies said he had been travelling about the coal districts, and lie had conversed with mas- ters, managers and men, and there was a concensus of opinion that thestrike would not come to a conclusion until the end of October.—The Committee unanimously decided to grant Supt. Keighley a further suni of £lO to be expended in providing lodgings for local single men out of work.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>MARRIAGE AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>MARRIAGE AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday afternoon, the marriage was cele- brated of Mr. W. E. Dudley and Theresa Oldham Sutton,of Lockfl.ld Cottage,Runcorn. The ceremony was performed by the Itev.Canon Wood. The bride was attired in a dress of grey cloth, trimmed with silk anl lace, and she wore a picture hat, trimmed with ostrich feathers. She carried a bouquet of choite white flowers. The bridesmaids were Miss Dudley (sister of the bridegroom) and Miss Rawlin- son. They carried pretty posies. Mr. Herbert Clare was the best man, and Mr. John Baden was the groosnsman. The service was fully choral. Mr. Johnson,the organist, played selections as the wedding patty arrived, and as they left the church he played with ability Mendelssohn's "wedding March." The ringers also gave their services. and from the steeple came forth merry peals. A large congregation witnessed the ceremony, and, the newly- married pair were the recipients of the hest wishes of many friends. The partfaftnwards partook of break- fast at the residence of the bride. Mr. and Mrs. W. E. Dudley have gone to Southport for the honeymoon. The following is a list of the presents ieceived : Mrs. It. P. Gill, Woodhey's Hall, timepiece ; Mrs. C. P. Worrall Longeroft, dress; Parish, Church choir, case of carvers; Parish Church Sunday School teachers, case of 1 dessert knives and prmgs ; Sunday school el se, egg service in velvet lined J3se ; Mr. Jai n Littler, silver sugar basin and jug in case ; Mr. and qrs. H, Rowlinson, copper kettle and h indsorne presmt ; Mr. B. imison, half dozen silver teaspoons ; Mr. Simister, cheese dish; Mr. W. A. Bailey, cruet; Messrs. Harrison, Connor. Heath and Co., brass dogs; foremen Bridgewater Dockyard. han some clock; Duke of Devonshire Lodge of Oddfellows, emblem ; Mr. W. Powell, reading lamp and are Irons; Mrs. Handley, glass cr-am jag and sugar butin, and butter dish ; Mr. W. Mack, two bronze ornaments, two small ornaments, one view ; Mr. and Mrs. Dudley. counterpane and blankets and celery glass; Mrs. Weedall, counterpane; Mr. John Soften, dinner service ; Mr. James Paden, be,room service ; Miss Watts, trinket set ; Miss Cooper. cheese dish ; Miss Payne, set of ornam.nts and tea box; Mr.Withlng on,copper kettle ; Misses Withhigton.dessert spoons ; Mr.F. Lewis,teapot ; Mr. W. Lewis, ornaments; Mrs. Rawlinson. two cake dklies and breakfast era t ; Miss Rawlinson coal vase ; Mr. Clarke, trass candlesticks ; Miss Robinson, bedroom slippers. pincushion, and nightdress nee ; Miss Tonis, one dozen tea spoons ; Mrs. Marson,set of jugs; Mr. (5. Dough ,set of jugs ; Mrs. J. Barker, tablecloth ; Mr. P. Deakin, knife, salt, and soap box ; Mr. R. Jeseank, plaque ; Mrs. Smith, plaque ; Mrs. Gresty. china candlestick ; Miss Bate, pair of ornaments ; Mrs. Whinyales, pair of bronze slippers ; Miss le-wis, glass dish ; MisA Dudley. wine glasses ; a Friend. plush table- cloth ; Miss Fla.sin Beckett, carvers ; Miss Reynolds, silts ; Miss Leach, inkstand ; Mis's It. Cleave, table-cloth ; Master A. Baden, water bottle and glass ; Mrs. Bate. two sauce ladles ; Mrs. Hardacre, bottle of wine ; Mrs. Brown, plate of fancy butter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>SHIP CANAL</head>
        <p>THE SHIP CANAL</p>
        <p>FATAL ACCIDENT AF PARTINGTON.</p>
        <p>-- An inquest was held on Wedsesclay evening, before Mr. F. Price, county coroner, touching the death of Win. Hampsm, aged 51 years of ago, who lives at 7, Jackson's-row, Cadishead. The deceased, with others, was employed in taking down some wooden buildings on the Ship Canal works at Partington. About noon on Monday deceased was sitting on some ceiling boards of the roof, " prizing" one of the joints, when the boards cave way and he fell to the ground, a distance of eight or nine feet. On being picked up he said he was killed. , jle was taken home and attended to by Dr. Jago and his assistant. On examination it was found that his spine was injured, and he died on Tuedly afternoon. A ver- diet of accidental death was returned. TUE ELECTRIC LIGHT ON THE CANAL. For a considerable period the Ship Canal Company have made rapid progress, owing to the brilliant illumi- nation of the electric light during the night time. This reflects great credit upon Mr.Morton,electrician,Sankey- st., Warrington, who has had the installations of the electric light at the fat-Ilford and Acton Grange sec- tions of the canal: At the former section, there are six 2,000 candle power arc lamps, and at the latter section there are twelve similar arc lamps. These cause the work to be pushed on at night almost as well as during the day time. All the dynamos, plant, &amp;c.,, belong , to Mr. Morton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>WARR I N GTON SLAIJG aTE It HOUSES.</head>
        <p>WARR I N GTON SLAIJG aTE It HOUSES.</p>
        <p>CORPORATION TO BUILD AN ABATTOIR,. "BY CLEO."</p>
        <p>There can, I think, be no room, for honest contro- versy on the subject of establishing an abattoir for Warrington. The preceding investigations have been as thorough as poesible, the language in which they have been submitted has been as moderate as possible, indeed much too moderate, but. the object of publication would be lost were it not followed by a result as practical as possible. Public opinion on this question having hitherto lain completely dor- mant it will taKe little less than a rude awakening to rouse it into immediate vitality. Such an awakening is all that is required and I am pleased to learn that this which but yesterday seemed cen- turies off is now at our doors. The Corporation have for years past realized the necessity for the reform advocated in these columns but in the numerous improvements in which they have been interested they allowed their opportunities-for carrying it out to slip away. This week, however, the General Purposes Committee have set themselves to tackle the question of several much needed improve- ments for which Parliementary powers are required, and have given instructions for the preparation of what is known as an "Omnibus" bill. This term does not apply to omnibuses as might be perionably assumed but applies to improvements of ill kinds which the Corporation may deem proper to under- take. Special reference will be made to the com- pulsory sale of the gas annuities; and street and sewerage improvements, but a clause will also be ' inserted for full power to close all the present slaughter house s when the erection' of an abat- toir ties been completed. It is intended to proceed with the bill in 'the next session of Parliament.. , Singularly enough, therefore, public attention has been drawn to the subject at a peculiarly opportune occasion, the first paper having appeared contemporary with prosecutions for diseased meat, unsound fish, and bad fruit, and the last appearing contemporary with the Corporation's initial steps towards a practical solu- tion of the evihwhile the interval has been marked by the sudden death of a Warrington man in the market,owing to his having partaken of unsound or decomposed meat. The question has, therefore, practically resolved itself into one of details,in the arrangement of which the Corporation will probably have the usual crop of difficulties. These can readily be overcome by their collective activity, and by the individual activity and interest of members of the community. For the same reason, the matter being no *sub judiee, it would savour ef dictating to the Council were! at this stage to advocate any length,a particular locality for the site of the abattoir or for any special feature to be made applicable to War- rington. I have sufficient confidence in their ability to successfully carry out in minute detail this much needed municipal reform, and shall, content myself with holding a watching brief. If undue delay or lethargy is manifested or if there be any truckling to party interest, my VOiCA may again be heard. Having, however, at the outset promised to say a wo la or two as to how and where an abattoir could t?. carried on in Warrington. I must very briefly, now that events have developed much more rapidly than i anticipated, fulfil that promise. In selecting the site several things must be considered, notably suitability for struc- tural and sanitary purposes, the convenience of the &amp;Am, and the nature of the surround- ,igs. Arpley fields come first and foremost mind. They might be found rather far re- moved to be convenient for those most interested, this drawback would be more than set off by Cie prospective advantage of being close to the docks of the Ship Canal, by means of which foreign cattle could be unshipped at the door of the abattoir, and might lead to Warrington developing into 4 centre for supplying the whole district with meat, live or L' d. This is a point of vantage that our city fa- thers should lose, no time in anticipating, as the Canadian cattle traffic will be specially catered for by the canal authorities, and possibly by the Lanca- shire, Derbyshire, and East Coat Railway. Many will be surprised at 'another of the places suggested tt me. It has advantages, buildings upon it could be mede o' much service,end the site could be obtained at a very reasonable price. It has one drawback fatal to its acquisition for the purposes, it is in close proximity to a fashionable neighbourhood. I refer :to the old barracks, Arpley-street. The residenters •a that neighbourhood, would be loud in their de- nanciation of any proposal to so transform these Premises and their denunciation Would have effect. Apart from this drawback, the site is not, in my opinion, suitable. For central purposes the choice for position rests betwe'en the old chemical works of Messrs. Roberts Dale and Co., or the stone yarn be- longing to the Corporation at Howley. For economic purposes the latter situation, it large enough,is the more preferable, as no expense would be required for purchase money. Their close proximity to the river bank is a great advantage as much of the manure, &amp;c., could be removed by barges. The neighbouring works would . also do much to detract from the drawbacks that are inseparable from an institution of this kind. But. for the matter of that wherever the abattoir may be erected or however large it may be. there woule be less of a nuisance then there is in the case of ably one of the slaughter-houses at present in use. , Gentlemen in the town who ardently desire this , reform to be brought about., and brought about speedily, have suggested to me that it would be necessary to erect two abattoirs. Ido not think so, one has proved ample for towns much larger than Warrington, and I do net see any necessity for more than one in Warrington. lam well aware of the many and costly improvements which the Cor- poration have to face and of the enormous sums of money which have already been expended and will have to be expended in the near future and of the caution necessary to be observed before incurring further expenditure. This caution is a plea which may well be argued in reference to some improve- ments in contemplation, but it cannot be applied in this case as for a certainty, not only would an abattoir be self-supporting, whether it would be a purely local one or one one larger scale, but it would actually bring ins handsome surplus revenge. For this very reason alone it should be one of the first improvements the Corporation should carry out. A wholesale meat depot would doubtless form one of the adjencts of the abattoir. This would confer a great advantage on our local farmers and cattle dealers, as they could send in their stock to he slaughtered and disposed of in the wholesale depot thereby bringing themselves into almost direct touch with the consumer. Warrington is a business town and for business astuteness it ranks high in the commercial commu- nity. Let this business astuteness be manifest in working out the details of the coming arrangement and its financial success is assured., It is rare that commercial enterprises go hand in hand with the reform ot abuses which damaged the pub- lic health, besmear youthful morals and invite or encourage needless CTuelty, but in this case it does, and Warrington would be unworthy of its repute- . tion were the opportunities thus thrust upon us not taken advantage of. The Corporation are now doing their share, let the public do theirs, and at the coming municipal election sound the candidates on the question, and send to the Council Chamber only those who can rid themselve4 of the rag- taggery of either political party and support and carry on this and other improvements for the greatest good of the greatest number.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>PROPERTY SALE AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>PROPERTY SALE AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>A DISPUTED MORT TO SELL BEER.</p>
        <p>A property sale was held at the Blue Bell Inn, norsemarket-street, on Wednesday evening. Mr. T. Tunstall, of the firm of Messrs. T. and J. L. Tuns- tall and Co., was the auctioneer. The property offered for sale consisted of thirteen lots and is situated in Bewsey-road and Iloyle-street. There was not a very good attendance and only one lot , was disposed of viz : Lot six. This comprised the dwel- ling house and shop, number 1, Hoyle-stre( t, and in the occupation of Mr. John Armstrong. The shop has a yearly rental of £5 10s. and has a grocer's off-license. Several questions were askwi at the sale about the off- license attached to the shop. It appears Messrs. Greenall, Whitley and Co., have a clause in their lease, made when the land was sold to them by Mr. John Piernoint, giving them power to close all licensed houses on the estate, and the shop in question is in close con- tiguity with the newly erected hotel of Messrs. Greenall and Company. They had given the present tenant of the shop notice that he was to cease selling beer. Mr. E. Cooper, solicitor, Blackburn, who has been the solicitor for the sale, read out the clause in the original lease, but, he said he should hold that Messrs Greenall's could not close these off-licensed premises as the license had been held for something like seventeen Years without any question being raised. The lot was ulti .mately sold for £305. to Mr. J. Spurr, traveller, Salford, for, we understand Messrs. 9. B. Walker and Co. Should Messrs. Greerall and Co.,persist in their claim, it is pro- bable that the matter will be decided in a court of law.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>DISTRESS IN WARRINGTON</head>
        <p>DISTRESS IN WARRINGTON</p>
        <p>The position in Warrington with regard. to the ,distres: in consequence of the coal strike, remains much the same as last, week. The wire depart- ment of VI hitecross Works, is still at work. Ity- lands Wire Works are also still going on at three days a week. Dallam and Bewsey Forges continue partially stopped. It is expected that a portion of ' Messrs. W. D. Houghton • and Sons, Sankey Wire Mills, will also be partially closed at the end of this week. At Monk.' Forge, one mill has been working a portion of this week. THE CHILDREN'S FREE BREAKFAST COMMITTEE. This Committee has issued the billowing report : " In presenting the following rmort to the public, we de so with feelings of gratitude and thankfulness for the many helps and encouragements we ,have received towards the object we have in view, namely, that of helping to feed the poor of our town, who are brought to want by the great strike. We feel that we hare helped to bring about the good arrangement now being put for- ward by the General Relief Committ3e,which is SO aptly grappling with the deep distress in our midst. We wisb as a Committee to thank the two bands who so ably as- sisted 114 in our Saturday's collections, namely, the fluckley-street Sunday School and the League of the Cro.s Bands. The sec. nd Saturday coils ction amounted to £6 4-s. 101. We also acknowledge a *elation of £1 from Mr. R. Piemonte, M.P. The total expenditure in feeding 600 children is £4 18s. Bd.; Handed over to the Relief Committee, £2 6. al.—Signed on behalf of com- mittee. T. MALKIN, chairman ; J. PRIM secretary." THE REV. E. T. REED, AND THE LOCAL DiS CRESS. The Rev. E. T. Reel, v.car ef Emmanuel Church, Bold-street, made special reference on Sunday evening, to the distress at present so general in the town. Be said that no doubt some wrre apt to forget the obliga- tions which wealth owed to the poor. Some calamity overtook a town, which plunged thou.ands of the in- habit ints into deititution and semi-statvation, and the feeling's of a common humanity, to say nothing of christianity, led them to see that when one member of a cotnmnnity suffered all the rest sufferel with it. Now came the opportunity of those who were possessed of the world's goods to help those who were not so fav- ourably situated. A calamity such as had fallen upon their town should tea-h the rich on the one hand to be considerate and kind towards the poor, and to be gener- ous in their gifts to their needy brethren. They should not be content with hearing about the state of affairs from the newspapers and other sources, but they should take a practical and earnest part in the alleviation of the distress, and, on the other hand, it should teach the poor the necessity of thrift, and of husbanding their In- comes in times of prosperity as much as possible. Re- ferring to the coal strike time preacher said that it was almost national in its range and its effects. ' Vast organ- izations' both of employers and employed, had come in- to existence within recent years, and the action of these bodies was proving that to set a gigantic piece of human machinery in motion might result in terrible suffering to another portion of the community, who had nothing to do with the dispute in question. To throw a nation into a state of confusion and dire distress—distress the weight of which fell chiefly upon the poor—was a responsibility which ought not to be lightly incurred. It was to be hoped that some means might be found for settling such disputes by other methods than those at pi esent adopted. THE SUNDAY DISTRIBUTION OF SOUP.</p>
        <p>On Sunday the distribution of soup took place for the first thne. The places of distribution were the Drill Hall, whure 2,400 pints of soup and 3,400 lbs. of bread were given away, and at Liverpool-road Cycling Works, where 720 pints of soup and 800 lbs. of bread were also distributed. AT THE SOUP KITCHENS.</p>
        <p>On Monday the following quantities of bread and soup were distributed .—Bank-st. soup kitchen, 560 pints of soups, 8001 b. of bread; Buckley-st., 1,000 pints of soup; 9601 b. ot bread; Liverpool-road, 800 pints of soup, 8001 b. of bread; Drill Hall, 800 pints of soup, 8001 b. of bread. On Tuesday the quantities were as follows :—Bank-st., 560 pints of soup, 5601 b. of bread; Bucklev-at., 1,200 pints of soup, 1,2851 b. of bread; Liverpool-road, 768 pints of soup, 8001 b. of bread; Drill Hall, 1,100 pints of soup, 1,000 lb. of bread. On Wednesday the quantities were Bank-street 560 pints of soup and 1400 lbs. of bread. Buckley-street, 1,200 pints of soup and 500 lbs. of bread, Liverpool-road 780 pints of soup and 700 Um. of bread, Drill Hall, 1,120 pints of soup and 1,000 lbs. of bread; Thursday, Bank. street 572 pints of soup and 800 lbs. of bread, Buckley- street 1,440 pints of soup and 1450 lbs. of bread, Liver. pool-road 800 pints of soup and 400 lbs. of bread, Drill Ha 111.040 pints of soup and 1,000 lbs of bread ; Friday, Bank-street 600 pints of soup and 552 lbs of bread, Buckley-street 1,440 pints of soup and 1,450 lbs. of bread, Liverpool-road, 800 pints of soup and 800 lbs of bread, Drill Hall, 1,200 pints of soup and 1,250 lbs of bread ; Saturday (estimated) Bank-street, 600 pints of soup and 500 Is of bread, Buckley-street, 1,440 pints of , soup and 1,550 lbs of bread, Liverpool-road, 800 pints of soup and 800 lbs of bread. Drill Hall, 1,100 pinta of soup and 1,200 pints\ of soup and 1,200 lbs of bread. This makes a grand total of Bank-street 3,392 nints of soup, and 3,372 lbs of bread ; Buckley-street, 7,720 pin's of soup and 8,145 lbs of bread, Liverplol-r isd, 5,448 pints of soup and 5,100 lbs of bread, Drill Hall 8,780 pints of soup and 9,650 Iris of bread. It will thus be seen that Buckley-street and the Drill Honore far ahead ns the other districts in the number who are served. The division of labour has, however, been rnlre equal since the new arrangement came between districts 2 and 3 which is annouced elsewhere. The bread is supplied by the local bakers in rotation. The soot) which is of an excellent qualitY is nuidts at the Drill Hall by Mr. Powys, the Town gall keeper. On Sundry a number of the clergymen te4eil its quality and pronounced it to be " very good indeed." THE LADIES AT WORK.</p>
        <p>A numher of ladies met at the Town Hall on illnndav,the Mayoress heing among those pr,sent. It V, aAel eided to form N COMlllitt..e to render assistance ;14 nurses for cases of sick TICAS amongst the poor women of the town, and also to provide food for them when necessary. AN INQUIRY OFFICER APPOINTED. To gmird ao.ainst imposition, n inyniry officer has been ',pointed by the relief committee, who will inquire into all suspicious eases. INCOME AND EXPENDITURE.—MORE SUBSCRIP.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>'MONS WANTED„</head>
        <p>'MONS WANTED„</p>
        <p>_ p. is cstima.ed that the cost of distributions per day rlonnts to £l9, and unless the subscriptions come in more freely, it will ho impoisible to go on with thfl work. At present there is only money in hand to last about eight days. THE WORK OF DISTRIBUTION.</p>
        <p>At tile meeting of the committees for districts 2 and 3 which was held to settle the question of more equally dividing the districts, it was finally decided to take oil Bewsey district on tha west side of the L. and N.-W. railway from district 2 and ad I it to district 3. This alteration has caused the work of distribution to be done with much less difficulty.</p>
        <p>WEEKLY MEETING OF THE COMMITTEE. SUSPECTED IMPOSITION. The weekly meeting of the Relief Committee was held yesterday afternoon in the Council Chamber of the, Town Hall. There was a good attendance of ladies and gentle- men. The Mayor presided during the former part of the the meeting but Mr. F. Monks subsequently occeplid that position. Mr. Monks, in making the weekly statement, said that the amount of food distributed durimt the week was greatly in excess of anything expect-d,an'd a search- ing, examinaton should be made in reference to the way in which the distribution was given. The amount had gone up to 28,2671b5. of bread and 25,320 pints of soup during the week, against about 18,000Ibs. of bread and pints of soup last week. This was a very large increase which made a difference in the cost of food alone, which for the present week had been £143 Ts 4d. This did not include other expenses such as wages and advertise- ments. The response to the appeal made last week had been £l2O 55.7 d. He was sorry to say that the Mayor had not met in one or two instances with that support which he had a right to expect. There were one or two works in the town in which they refused to subscribe because they were finding employment for their workpeople and yet upon examination they found that a large number of the workpeople from these works were being relievecl,at cost of the relief fund. It was disappointing that money could not be found, and the relief could not go on with- out it. It Would be as well to know that tino balance in hand was £l3B 19s. Bd. This was sufficient to carry them over antither week. Since the commencement, the total amount received had been £520 Ts. aud the expenditure £383 Ts. 4d. There was an )ther agency at work, viz, the ladies, which was not included in this. Unless the funds were larger they would have to meet next week to see what was desirable to do, whether to continue the relief or not. With reference to the districts, he thought that district 1 ought to make a closer exam- ination of those relieved, as some cases of imposition had been found in it. It would be extremely difficult to improve the way in which the work had been done in district 3. It was a question whether it would not be bet ter to work district 2 from the Drill Hall. The other day, the man who was in charge of the work in the dis- trict had pointedly refused to do anything, and a man had to he sent from the Drill Hall to do the work. The Rev. Owen Watkins said that he had been away from the town during a part of the week, and that was the first time he had heard of this. The workers gener- ally of that district had done good work, but he had had to do away with some of them. Personally he was willing for the work to be done at the Drill Hall, but he thought that it would be a hardship for those in the further parts of the district to come such a distance. Mr. Monks thought that as there was so much imposi- tion it was necessary to bring in the aid of the poor law officials. One of them whom he bad spoken to on the I subject said that if it were done £2O or £3O would be saved per week. At a former meeting a very strong feeling had been expressed against the idea,and he would not like to bring them to the work unless the meeting authorised it. Rev. Father Feeny thought it was very desirous to call them in as they would be a great help in distinguishing</p>
        <p>.</p>
        <p>rhronic poor from those thrown out of work by the strike.</p>
        <p>The Rev. J. Yonge did not wish the poor law officers ? to he fixtures and he did not like giving the poor people the discomfort of asking for relief off poor law officers. • Mr. Monks said that it was only suspicious cases that • need be visited by the officers. He moved that the executive be required to call in the aid of the poor law officers to give what assistance they could. The Rev. E. C. E. Carleton seconded the motion. The resolution was supported by Revs. F. Slater, W. H. Drummond and Father Hennelly, ties latter remark- ing that the secretary for district 1, hai ben away since SatOrday. (Mr. Monks : You ought to get a new secretary then.)—The motion was carried. The Rev. E. T. Reed moved the following resolution; That in view of the great distress in Warrington and elsewhere, caused by the existing coal strike, which distress in this town alone necesbitates the feeding of four thousand persons every day, who have been plunged into a state of starvation by reason of the stoppage of various works in the town from want of fuel ; this Committee formed for the purpose of relieving the dis- tress in Warrington calls upon the owners and colliers to speedily settle the present dispute, and at the same time enters its earnest protest against the barbarous method now employed of settling trade differences, whereby in the present instance, almost the whole nation has been brought into a state of embarrassment and acute suffering. And that copies of this resolution be forwarded to the Secretaries of the Coalowner's and I Miners' Federations to "The Times" newspaper and to the "Manchester Guardian." The Rev.W.H.Drummond seconded the resolution. He thought that the papers should take more notice than they did in bringing attention to the distress in districts where work was stopped owing to the coal strike. Mr. F. Monks in supporting the resolution said that he had looked forward to employing a large number of workpeople but owing to the strikes which had occurred so often of late, he was beginning to think that it would be better to leave that to younger men to grapple with. With all the strikes and lockouts, the employer could not carry on his works with that degree of noninterference as he did hitherto done, nor manage his own business in his own way. Capital could find employment in the colonies far more remunerative than here and with less anxiety. The motion was carried unanimously. This concluded all the business of public interest.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>SANKEY BRIDGF.S.</head>
        <p>SANKEY BRIDGF.S.</p>
        <p>The members of the Sankey Bridges Wesleyan Chapel continued their work of relief on Saturday when they provided a tea that about 180 men, after with a miscellaneous entertainment was ".idtni presided over by Mr. J. Summergill. Mr. T. Brough presided at the piano. There were also .n the platform Councillor Shaw Green, and Messrs.W.Brough and A.Birch. The schoolroom was well filled and the entertainment was eviderfily much enjoyed. The chairman in opening the meeting said that he had hoped at the beginning of the week, that there was a rift in the clouds, and that the colliers would have gone to work. but he was sorry to say that those hopes had not been realised. The men who were at that meeting were bearing patiently under the circumstaaces lin which they were placed. He hoped that very soon the strike would come to an end, and that full work would he found for those who were at present unemployed. He also mentioned that he had been past the scene of the unfortunate affair at Featherstone, and described the scene. He finish his speech by handing to Mr. Brough for the committee a half sovereign which Mr.R.Garnett had sent towards the funcl.—Mr.S. Hassell next gave a song "The Anchor's Weighed "for which he was encored and sang again in resp6nse. Mr. Birch in addressing the meeting said that he was very pleased to meet his fellow workmen that night. The work they were doing at Sankey Bridges showed what Christianity was. In former years there was a better feeling between employers and employed, and he was sorry to see that this had changed. He joined the Chairman in hoping that the present strike would end.—Mr. A. B. Bailey next sang "Johnny Sands,' and on being encored sang "Farmer's boy "—Councillor Shaw Green followed with a capital speech full of sympathy for the MEM who were out of work, and he was followed by Mrs. Wonlley,who gave a recitation "In the market," in a first-class man- ner, for which she was encored, and gave " The drunk- ard's wife."—Mr. C. Pritchard followed and gave some impromptu poetry which he had composed on the work in Sankey Bridges.—Mr. W. Brough, in moving a vote of thanks to the chairman, speakers, singers, and the ladies who bad devoted so much time in preparing the teas, said that he had hoped to have been able to have given the men a right down good " feed" that night, as he expected it would have been the last tea they would have needed to have given them. But that was not to be, as the strike was not over yet. Had they known at the beginning that it would have needed £4O to carry on the work, he thought that they would have hesitated before commencing, the teas, but they had al- ready spent over £3O, and had a good amount ti com- mence the next week with.—Mr. Bennett seconded the motion, and Mr. Thlmson in supporting it, expressed his sitisfactinn that it was proposed to continue the Satu- rday night's entertainments after the strike was nver,and- promised to assist in the arranging of them.—The vote was carried with acclamation. Mr.Fairclnugh,of Burton- wood, at the end of last week gave 20 hampers of potatoes. On Tuesday night again 420 children were provided for, and on Wed nesclaY 160 women were entertained. On ThuNday, Mr. and Mrs. GeorgelSavage provided a tea for 430 children at their own expense. This makes a total of 1,280 free meals which have been provided gratis to the distressed people about Sankey Bridges and Mill-lane. The. meals have been provided at an average cost of 2d pr head. The weekly free tea and entertainment will as usual be given to- night.</p>
        <p>• • ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>SUCCESSFUL OPERATIONS AT LONG FO RD.</head>
        <p>SUCCESSFUL OPERATIONS AT LONG FO RD.</p>
        <p>The long talked of nuisance at Longford is shortly to become a thing of the past. The present engi- neer Mr. James Deas, who has now filled that post for two years has effected awonderful improvement and has transformed it from the chaos in which he found it into an object of envy and admiration of other corporations. An influential deputation front Wolverhampton visited the works yesterday to examine the appliances which Mr. Deas completed on Saturday last and has been putting to practical test during the week. A representative of the press was present during the proceedings and ample opportunity was given of testing the success of the experiments. Without going into details an eyewitness's account of the principle upon. which the improve- ment is based will be a pleasing contrast to what has so often been previously written about Long- ford. First of all the long steep incline up which the town refuse is carted, will be dispensed with, as it will no longer be necessary. Mr. Deas has con- trived a lift bp which the refuse is conveyed from the ground floor to the hopper at the mouth of the different furnaces. This is a most ingenious i pll- - and eaves a great amount of labour to b Ali man and beast, which the previous metl od en- tailed. The most important part of the improve- ment is the furnace itself. In marked contrast to the old furnaces and destructors,this special furnace' which was only completed on Saturday is simple in design, economic in operation and effective in purpo=e. And further unlike other furnaces which emit the various gases from the burning refuse into the chimney flue this furnace completely consumes these gases and the vapour which ascends the chimney is perfectly innocuous. This was proved beyond doubt by testing the vapour as it ascended into the flue. This is not the time nor place te describe the de' ails of the furnace, for which Mr. Dens in cord t nctim with Mr. Coun- cillor Beaman has secured a protective crd-u• so that a brief summary must suffice. Toe elevator or lift drops the refuse in regulated quan- tities upon the hopper at the furnace mouth and the refuse is placed on the grating as . soon as the furnace has been sufficiently heated. The charge takes about 20 minutes to be consumed. While one charge is on the grating next charge is on a ledge within the framework of the furnace so that it is being dried before being put upon the grating. All the gases have to pass through the hottest part of the furnace and this effectually prevents any egress. A specially arranged chamber adjoins the furnace which serves as a further safeguard against any eicape of these gases. When one charge is withdrawn the furnace is ready to receive the next one so that no time is lost. In this way as much as 24 tons of refuse can be consumed per day and already it is found that the town cannot supply refuse in sufficient quan- tities to keep the works in steady operation. No fuel is necessary other than what is required to heat the furnace in the first instance as the refuse material in itself supplies all that is needed. Front the experiments male it was proved that the fur- nace burned about 20 per cent, more refuse per square foot of grate than any known destructor with about 10 per cent, less form of clinkerresiduum ' at less cost per ton and comparatively small cost for maintenance. The heat generated from the burn- ing of the material is used for generating steam with a 96 TIP. Tubular Boiler which may be used for any alterior purpose. The deputation expressed which . the difficulty of treating satisfied with the complete man n fner i viz.; the es- cape of noxious gases, thems.elves highly To Warrington works.b highly appreciated from a htri nbeerneu osyeercome. Other deputations are to visit the I itself the improvement muct \ n sanitary point timate adoption i n most fact every point of view i The arrangement is yet of view, from an economic. point of view, and in ' its infancy, but it ul- st corporations is only . matter of time.</p>
      </div>
