        <p>American Depa—Ma. R. D. WILLIAMS,</p>
        <p>Pharmacist, Plymouth, Pens.</p>
        <p>ALLEGED EXTENS'IVII THEFTS FROM THE L. AND N.-W.</p>
        <p>RAI LWLY.</p>
        <p>Before the Warringtori-m—a-g'i;-t-ra- tee. on Friday, Robert Dawson, 116, Longford-st., Thomas Asht3n, 5, Elms Terrace, Thelwall-lane, and Alfred Lamb, 43, Pinners Brow, were each charred with stealing early the same morning, from the goods warehouse of the London and North-Western Railway, a quantity of eggs. Dawson was further charged with stealing from the same place four overcoats,. aud Ashton was also charged with steal- ing two pairs of boots from the same place. Mr. A. Browne appeared for Ashton and Dawson and Mr. P. Davies for Lamb. The Chief Constable who was corm- borata d by railway detective Payne, said that Detectives Payne and Ede were on duty in the goods warehouse, Bank Quay, during Thursday night and Friday morning. At about 12-20, they saw Ashton and Dawson come into the warehouse and open a crate of eggs with an instru- ment of of some kind,and shortly afterwards Lamb came on the scene. The two detectives hid ttPmselves be- hind some waggons. and prisoners after some further walking about they went towards the gate at Crosfield- st., but. where there stopped by the detectives, who searched them. It was then discovered that there were , 39 eggs in Dawson's possession, Ashton had 58 eggs and also a pair of boots, and Lamb had 22 eggs in his posses- ' sion. The prisoners were then taken to the Police Station. On Dawson's house being searched four rail- way overcoats were found.—A remand until Friday was asked for as there was a large number of articles miss,: ing from the railway of late. The remand was granted. Mr. Davies, and Mr. Browne applied for bail for their clients, but the magistrates said that they regretted that they could not grant it. It was subsequently on the application of Mr. Browne decided to let them remain in Warrington during the week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATE.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATE.</p>
        <p>AGGRIEVED MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>At the close of the business at the Police Court, on Saturday morning, Mr. Burgess (the presiding magis- trate), said that he wished to make a few observations to the reporters. A paragraph had appeared in the papers with reference to the attendance of magistrates at the Court. It was quite true that on the previous Saturday there was pretty nearly half an hour past, be- fore there were two magistrates to dispose of the busi- ness, one being not sufficient. He would explain how that arose. There were two cases of felony, and the prisoners were brought in very late,somewhere between eleven and twelve o'clock, and that was after the time that the officer in the ccurt would have left his duty, otherwise the magistrates would have been advised, as they were every Saturday morning by post card. There was no such advice owing to the cases coming in at that late hour. Beyond that, Mr. Chandley, one of his colleagues, had been, and, was still unwell, and could not leave the house. Mr„ Voisey, who was now present, never had the notice, and not having been ad- vised by any constable at the usual time that he would be required he had come to the conclusion that there would be no business at the court. Mr.Geddes, his fourth colleague came now on Wednesday instead of Saturday, as he was now living out of town. He (Mr. Burgess) thought that it should be known that there had been no neglect on the part of any one of the magis- trates, as they would have been present had they been advised in time. As for himself, he had nothing to say as he was at the court in time. • STRANGE AFFAIR AT BRIDGE FOOT.</p>
        <p>Robert Whitfield was brought before the Warrington magistrates en Monday, on the charge of frequenting the river side at 115 on Sunday morning with some felonious intent. The Chief Constable said that he had a statement to make before the prisoner was dealt with. Constable Clough was on duty in Mersey-st. and Rose and Crown-st. during Sunday morning. When in the latter street he heard a whistle like a policeman's, ap- parently near Bridge Foot. He hurried in that direction and then heard a crash as though a window was broken, which he found belonged to Mr. Houghton's pawnbroking Shop. Two distinct blows were heard. When Constable -Clough got to the shop, he found that young Mr. Hough- ton had come downstairs in consequence of the crash. Mr. Houghton had seen a man with his hands in the window, and shouted to him through the window upon which he ran down Wharf-st., along the river side. Mr. Houghton and the officer then hastened along the river side, and crossed the fields, and when near the sheds at Mersey Mills they heard someone shouting "Stop thief! " towards the Corporation buildings. They went in that direction and near the Corporation wall, they found pri• soner in a crouching position. He was taken into cus- tody. Houghton also caught another man who was, however, rescued.—P.C. Clough corroborated the Chief .Constable's statement, and Mr. Houghton on being called said that he could dieinguisli the man who was at the window when the crash occurred, but did not think the prisoner was the man. Prisoner stated that he was 4,n his way home and when captured he was looking for his cap which he had lost. The magistrates dismissed the case, but thought that the ofer had done right in taking the man into custody when he was acting in such a suspicious manner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>GONE FOR ANOTHER HOLIDAY..</head>
        <p>GONE FOR ANOTHER HOLIDAY..</p>
        <p>A young woman named Julia Ann Shields appeared before the Warrington Bench on Tuesday. She has gained some notoriety among the police and the newspaper-read- ing public of Warrington, by the circumstances under which she has troubled the Bench to pass sentences upon her some 20 times during her brief but evidently active career. In giving evidence against her on a charge of drunkenness, a constable described how Julia Ann stag- gered about Market Gate on Monday evening, and dis- played evident signs of having been figuratively hit with what has been sapiently described as the " harm stick." Her actions in the main thoroughfare were rendered somewhat ludicrous owing to a delusion under which she seemed to labour, that the pavement was capable of a movement similar to that of a rolling billow. With some intention, perhaps, to add to the facetiousness of her position, the indomitattle Julia Ann had brought out her sister's baby, and, albeit this child like all others had been gifted with eyes wherewith to regard the sky, the nurse, by a simple act of "up ending," bad induced " baby" to regard the heavens with its sockless feet. Al- though this may be a beneficial position for viewing sky- ward things,the policeman thought it inefficacious to the child as a cure for bronchial tightness ; as he handed the child over to a more trustworthy nurse, and took the now irate Julia to the "Station." She had only com- pleted a short sojourn in the Strangeways prisomand she was now committed to go and serve another month.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>POACHERS_ IN TROUBLE.</head>
        <p>POACHERS_ IN TROUBLE.</p>
        <p>Lt Daresbury Petty Sessions on Tuesday,Wm. Taylor, a ganger on the Snip Canal was charged with having on the 3rd September, committed a trespass upon certain land in the occupation of Sir Gilbert Greenall, in search of game, and Arthur Turner, a navvr,was charged with aiding and abetting. Mr. W. H. J. Jenkins prosecuted, and said that on the day in question, two of Sir Gilbert Greenell's keepers Lockett and Irlam, saw the defen- dants with two dogs—a greyhound and a lurcher—pro- ceeding towards Hatton. Taylor was seen to go into a field and leave Turner with tha two dogs at the gate. Taylor took a gun out of his peckets and fixed it together. On seeing the keepers, Taylor ran away and one of the keepers followed him, and Taylor seeing that the keeper was gaining ground him turned back. The keeper de- manded to see what he had in his pockets, and Taylor admitted having a double barrel gun. The other man seeing that Taylor was caught came through the gate and set the two dogs on the keepers. After evidenco by Messrs. Wm. Lockett and John Irlatn, keepers, bearing out Mr. Jenkin's statement, each defendant was fined 10s. and coats.—The same defendants were charged with a similar offence on the land of Mr. Thomas Henry Lyon, on the same date, in search of conies. Mr. White prose- •cuted. The defendant Taylor was seen pointing his gun to the wood, and a shot was heard. After evidence by Messrs. W. and T. Broady and Joseph Cook, each de- fendant was fined 10s. and costs.—Robert Stevens, Boa- tcck-st., Stockton Heath, was charged with having in possession, two hares and a gun found upon him by Ser- geant Booth on the 3rd September. Mr. P. Davies ap- peared on behalf of the defendant. Thomas Broady, keeper, said he saw the defendant running with the two men Taylor and Turner. He afterwards went by him- self to London Bridge. He (witness) followed after him and shouted to two men to stop the defendant which they did. He took him to the police station where Ser- geant Booth searched him,and found two hares in a bag, and a gun. Tito defendant said he had shot them in the lane. Sergeant Booth deposed to finding the hares and gun npon the defendant. Mr. Davies said it was simply a case of giving an explanation how his client had that gun in his possession and the two hares in his bag. If the defendant had given the officer a proper accnunt,the Sergeani; would have inquired and the man would not have been charged at all. In August last, Mr. Stock, farmer, Antrobus, gave the defendant permission to ?hoot grouad game on his farm, and he could call three wititeases to prove that the defendant was seen there ' -with ;.is gun on the day in question. Edward Stock said lie saw defendant at hi a farm on the morning in -question and heard one shot fired, but could not say v.lmether the two hares found upon defendant had been shot on his farm. J Imes Cooper also deposed to seeing the defendant at Antrobus on the morning in question. Alter evi:ence by Wm. Broady, the defendant was fined 10s. and costs and the gun was forfeited.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>IiIN olt CASES. WARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</head>
        <p>IiIN olt CASES. WARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY.—Before Messrs. J. Burgess (chairman) and</p>
        <p>L. Voisey.</p>
        <p>SUSPICIOUS CuartacT—Eits..:-Peter Bailey, alias Elijah Rustidge, and Samuel Rustidge, were each charged on remand with offering a lady's watch and chain without giving a satisfactory account of the same, being sus- pected of stealing it, on Tuesday evening week.—The two prisoners had been brought before the magistrates and remanded in order to find an owner for the watch. The police had,however.been unsuccessful. The conduct of the men was,however,so open to suspicion,Bailey be- ing under an assumed name, that the magistrates fined each prisoner 10s. and costs, or in default 14 days' hard labour. AN OBS'TINATE SOLDINR.—Edward Bulwer, a soldier, was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Win- wick-st.on the previons night,also with refusing to quit the" Lord Rodney,"and assaulting the landlord,Mr.Mars- hall, at the same time.—From the evidence of Marshall and P.C. Sankey,it appeared that prisoner went into the "Lord Rodney" the worse for drink. The landlord refused to serve him with any, but he said he would have some drink at any cost. The landlord then proceeded to eject him with the assistance of P.C. Sankey, and in doing so the landlord was kicked by the prisoner.—The magjs- crates inflicted a penalty of 10s.and costs,or 14 days' hard labour, and said that they were going to protect land- lords. It was sometimes difficult to tell whether a man was too drunk to be served, but it was the duty of the Bench to protect publicans when they were assaulted by persons whom they refused to serve owing to them hav- ing too much liquor. A NIGHT DEPREDATOR.—CharIes Cotterall was charged with stealing from the garden of J. Lander, 29, Hoyle- St., early the same morning,* quantity of celery, valued ed., also from George Allen of thesame street,a red cab- bage, valued 3d.—P.C. Meacock said he met defendant early that morning in Boyle-at., with something bulky under his coat, which upon examination proved to be the red cabbage and five sticks of celery. Defendant's hands were covered with soil, and he was wet through with the rain which had fallen during the night. Witness took him to the Police Station, and when there he con- fessed to stealing them.—The two complainants, who 'ap- peared in court,said they did not wish to press the case, as they knew defendant's family to be respectable people, and defendant was discharged on payment of costs.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—Before Mr. F. Monks (chairman), alderman Harrison, and Francomb, Messrs. W. Pierpoint, J. W. Hayward, and S. Wright. Dames AND DISORDERLY.—MichaeI Walsh, Winwick- st., Daniel Stevens, Queen-st., Charles Ayres, Hill-st., Thomas Howard, Dial-st., Patrick Lyder, Buttermarki t- at., Frank Quinn, 5 Welch's-yard, were dealt with fcr this offence, and John Carroll and Wm. Lythgoe, were fined for being drunk. ASSAULT or; • SHOPKEEPER.—Frederick Rickton, a navvy, was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Hanklst., on Saturday night, and also with assaulting Daniel Lyons at the same time and place.—Trisoner had gone into the shop of complainant's mother-in-law and commenced to create a disturbance. Lyons went into the shop to put him out when defendant tore his shirt sleeves. P.C. Clough had to be called in and locked him up.—The magistrates fined prisoner ss. a.nl costs or seven days. AN INCORRIGIBLE THIEF,—ThOHISS Foy was charged with being a convicted thief frequenting Bridge-st.,with intent to commit a felony on Saturday night.—P.C. Turner said that at 10 30 on Saturday night he was in company with P.C. Bell in Bridge-st., in plain clothes. They saw prisoner very busy about a man who was end -r the influence of drink. The officers warned him to go home, but soon afterwards near Market Gate, they saw prisoner with one hand in the coat pocket, and the other in the waistcoat pocket of a drunken man. They rushed across and found 3d. in the prisoner's band. The man was too drunk to know whether the 3d. was his or not, and the prisoner when brought to the police station was charged with the above offence. This made over twenty times that prisoLer had been brought before the magistrates on similar charges.—The magistrates sent prisoner to gaol for a month. ROGUE AND VAGABOND—Win. McGee was dismissed with a caution on the charge of being a rogue and va.;a- bond, found sleeping in a shed in Mersey Mills. A Sate APPOINTED FOREMAN —Walter Whitley was charged with stealing 4d. from .I,,tin Connolly in Old-rd. on Sunday night.—Connolly it appeared had gone to Cobley's lodging-house in Gici-road, for a night's ledeing when prisoner came to him saying he was the foreman and taking the money off him showed him to his bed. Shortly afterwards, the real foreman came, and when he saw Connolly in the bed, he threatened to throw him downstairs. It was then found out that prisoner had no right to have received any money at all, and the police being called in he was locked up.—The magis- trates sent him to gaol for two months. Ax OLN WARRANT.—Margaret Fleming, was appre- hended on a warrant with using disorderly language in Bridge-at., in 1889. The magistrates discharged the prisoner. CAB DRIVERS SUMMONED.—Martin Manion, a cab • driver vele charged with being drunk while in charge of a cab in Marsh-house-lane, on the 20th inst.—P.C. San- key proved the case, and prisoner was fined ss. and cests —Robert Grace was charged with a similar offence committed in Crewn-st., on two occasionsoen the 18th inst. P.C. Barratt and P.S. Cheney proved the cases and prisoner was fined 2s. 6d. for each offence. BREACH OF THE Psece.—John Whittaker and William Heaton were tined in the costs and hound over for six months on the charge of fighting in Museum-st., on the 18th inst. FURIOUS DRlVlNG.—Charles Brierley was fined ss. for committing this offence in NVilderspool-road, on the 16th ins'. CARD PLAYING.—Three youths, Patrick Murree', Patrick Dooney, and Harry Marratt, were each charged with playing a game of cards called " twenty-fives " for money in Springfield-st., on the 19th inst.—P.S. Taylor and P.C. Rylatt caught the defendants each playing cards and took their names and addresses.—Each pris- oner was fined 2s. 6d. and costs or in default one day's detention in the bridewell. TURSDAY.—Before Messrs. R. Garnett, E. J. Guest, and Dr. Starkey Smith. DRUNKENNESS.—For this offence in Bridge-st. the pre- vious morning John Williams and Win. Hughes were each fined 53. and COOS. FALSE ATTESTATION.—Joseph Jones, a private in the 42nd South Lancashire Regiment was charged with mak- ing a false attestation when enlisting on the 19th July. It was proved that although prisoner said he had not been in the army before, he had been dismissed with ignominy from the Welsh Fusiliers a year before. He was sent to jail for two months with hard labour. FOR PLAYING A GAME OF CHANCE.—John Joseph Riley, a respectably dressed youth, was fined 13s. including costs for playing pitch and toss in Birchall-st. on Mon- day.—The defendant's father, a respectable mechanic, when asked to account for his son's conduct said he was a newsagent and owing to having too much time on his hands had fallen into had company. He had always been brought up respectably and "sent to Sunday school and chapel regularly." The Chairman (to P.C. Clough who proved the case) "What kind of companions does he keep."—l hardly know what kind they are; but none of them ever go to Sunday school or chapel I know." THURSDAT.—Before Alderman Sutton (chairman) and Councillor James Faircloagh. tr THAT STACK STILL INVADED.—George BrOWEI, a navvy, was charged with being a rogue and vagabond, and found sleeping on the straw stack of Mr. Fleming, at Arpley. at 1.20 that morning. He was fined 103. and Costs, or 14 d ys'imprisonment. SCHOOL ATTENDANCE Cases.—Mr. G. Riley, school at- tendance officer, prosecuL^d in the following-cases for irregular attendance at school :—Martha Ellen Grady, Latchford, 23.15 I. : Joseph Cloughefilecutter,Warburton- ' two,cases, 7e. 6d, ; Martha Ann Meddlicat,Brackley- et., 54. ; Patrick Dolly, labourer, Turner-at., 5. ; Win. Farringt on, forgeman, Bewsey-road, sa. ; John Mannion, t inner, Latchford, child ordered to attend school until 14 years of ege ; Frederick Roberts, clockmaker, Kean- at. two cases,lo3. : James Rohinivin,Ship Caryl labourer, Admit al-st., 23. 6d. ; Thomas Sloane, joiner,Crossley-st., FRIDAY.—Befm.e Alderman Harrison (chairman), Alder- man Wright, Dr. Gomel!, Councillor Roberts and Mr. J. W. Hayward. EXPENSIVE TO THE RATEPATERS.—AIfred Morris was charged with running away from the borough of War-</p>
        <p>rington leaving his wife chargeable upon the Warrington I Union in August 18, 1890.—Mr. Shearer who represented the Guardians said that this was a very bad case. In 1887, prisoner's wife died and he deserted his three chil- dren leaving them chargeable on the union. They were maintained by the Union until July 1889 when prisoner was arrested in Birmingham and sent to gaol for three months. On coming out of gaol he took his children but deserted them again in August 1890, and had been missing ever since. The total cost to the Guardians had been £22o.—Prisoner was sent to gaol for three months. —John Frain was charged with running away from War- rington on April 24, 1890, and leaving a child chargeable on the Union. This child had cost the Guardians £3B.— Prisoner was also sent to gaol for three months. DRUNK AND DISORDERLY. —James Cunliffe was fined for this offence.</p>
        <p>CainLlT vo A HORSE.—J6bn Bradbury, 98 Hoyle-A..' was charged with illtreating a horse by working it while in an unfit state on September Bth.—P.S. Smith said that he saw defendant with the horse attached to a cart with some meat in it. The horse was lame on the fore- legs. Witness ordered defendant to take it home.— P.S. Lawrence corroborated, and Inventor Dowty, R.S.P.C.A., said that it was suffering from ring. bone. It was the third time that defendant had been summoned for the same horse.—The Bench inflicted a fine of 20s. and costs. —Samuel Ellis, Whitehouse, Newton Common, was charged with committing a similar offence on September 13th In- spector Dowty stated that he saw the horse standing on three legs in the fair ground, and on examining it, he found that one hind leg was very lame and suffering from spavin. Defendant had brought it from Earlestown that day. Be had not worked since the coal strike, and wit- ness did not wish to press for a heavy penalty. Defendant , was fined in the costs.</p>
        <p>ASSAULT ON A TENANT:SA muel Barlow, Folly-lane.was charged with assaulting and beating Jane Tickle, 3, Tur- ner-st., on September •25th. Complainant saki that she was a tenant in one of Mr. Barlow's houses,and had given notice to leave. He came to the house on Monday in a drunken state,ai.d presented a bill for some broken win- dows. She refused to pay for them,as she had not broken them, whereupon Barlow lifted up his stick and struck her on the arm. Two witnesses alsn gave evidence. Bar- low denied that he had struck complainant, and said he was attacked by Mrs. Tickle. Defendant was ordered to pay the costs. PRETENTED FRom HITTING MM.—Caroline Lyttigoe, Portland-st.,was charged with assaulting CatherineFlan- nigan of the same street, on the 25th inst. Complainant said that defendant was drunk on the above date, and struck her. She (complainant) would have struck back but was prevented by bystanders. If she had hit defen- dant she would not have summoned her. The case was dismissed.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS. MONDAT.—Before Mr. James Handley and Major Hazlehurst.</p>
        <p>DRUNKEN/TESL—Nicholas Comborford, Jno. Trelfa, Witham Jones, Mary Jackson, and Joseph Gayter, were each fined for this offence. Tms JURY LISTS—The several assistant overseers in the division presented the jury lists for their respective townships. ALLEM) ASSAMMTS.—Arthur BeeslOy, a rope runner, was summoned for assaulting an elderly man named Joseph Hunt; Elizabeth Warren and Eliza Beesley were summoned for assaulting Elizabeth Hunt. Theevidence was very contradictory and the summonses were dis- missed. _ . _ _</p>
        <p>PITCH AND Toss. —Charles Butterworth and Peter Coyne. boys, were each ordered to pay Is. ed. for play- ing pitch and toss./ A JIISTIFIABLB IssAtlvr.—Joseph Rowlands alias Por- ter a well-known character of Alcock-st. was summoned for assaulting John *efton Ball, on the 16th inst. The complainant alleged that he wss in the Commercial Hotel (Glass Barrel) when the defendant without any provo- cation knocked him down to the floor. He had a serious wound on the back of the head and on the forehead.— Evidence was given showing that the complainant was the aggressor, twice striking Rowlands, and the Bench dismissed the summons.</p>
        <p>ASSAULT ON THE Pottcy.—A young man named John Critcbley, was charged with being drunk and disorderly and assaulting P.C. Richardson by striking him on the stomach. The prisoner was fined Ss. and costs on each charge.</p>
        <p>DARESBURY PETTY SESSIONE.</p>
        <p>TOESDAY.—Before Mr. H. Nield (in thiTtiraii) and Mr.</p>
        <p>H. Talboi.</p>
        <p>TELE Jim LlSTS.—Gentlemen from the following town- ships were appointed on the Cheshire Jury Lists for 1893 without any objections being raised .I—Latchford, Dares- bury, Hartington, Whitley Superior, Dutton, Keekwick, Moore, Acton Grange, Walton Superior, Preston-o'th-El ill, Gra ppenhall, Newton, Whitley Inferior, Batton Seven Oaks. Walton Inferior, Antrubus, Appleton-with-Hull, Stretton, and Little Leigh. DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.—Frechrick Pinder was fined ss. including costs for being drunk and disorderly on the highway at Moore. A SLEEPS Dnivsn.—George James was charged with being asleep whilst in charge of a dray and horses at 8.15 p.m., at Moore, on the lath inst. Defendant denied being asleep and said he was smoking. Ile was fined 10s. including costa.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>THE</head>
        <p>THE</p>
        <p>TERRIBLE ACCIDENT IN A FOUNDRY.</p>
        <p>At St. Helens on Thursday, Mr. Brighouse held an inquest on the body of Harold George Harris, aged nineteen, an apprentice fitter at Messrs. Dag Hall's engineering works, and son of Mr. George Harris, coach proprietor, Denton's Green-lane. Mr. Jackson, Government inspector of factories, was present, and Mr. R. W. H. Thomas, solicitor, watched the proceedings on behalf of the relatives of the deceased.</p>
        <p>Mr. Harris stated that his son had been at Messrs. Daglish's works between four and five years. On the 14th inst. he went to work in good health, but durine; the day he (witness) received a telephone message to tAke a cab to the surgery of Drs. Twyford, Reid, and Mason, and there found his own son, who had been badly burned. He was taken to the Cottage Hospital, and there he made a statement that he was sent to the smithy to get some tools sharpened, and while passing through the fitting shop whore some men where taking a pin from a crosshead, a flame shot out at him and set him on fire. He explained that a man named Mason was pouring some paraffin oil into a heated hole, and as he suddenly stepped back some of the oil was thrown over his (deceased's) clothing. Almost immediately the flame shot out and he was enveloped in flames, although be was three yards away. Deceased said he did not speak to any of the men, nor did he meddle with tbem. He was a stead', well-behaved young man.—A Jury- man : It is a very dangerous thing to use paraffin on anything that is warm.</p>
        <p>Bold said he was an erector in the works,and on the day named he and a labourer were getting two rods out cf a crosshead that were tight in and had been broken off. They had been trying tvo days to get the rods out with hammers, but could not do so, and the foreman (Johnson) told witness to pour paraffin into the cotter hole. The bottom of the hole was wedged with clay, and Sam Mason poured the best part of a gill of paraffin into it from a stone bottle. There were heaters underneath. The oil boiled, exploded, and flew out.— The Coroner: It was like muring paraffin into a hot pot, wasn't it P—Yes, .it was warm.—Wha,t happened then P—Dneased was standing behind me, and a little ball of fire flew at him and set him on fire.—Did be be- come deluged with the paraffin before that by Sam Mason stepping back P—No. Deceased was sudden ly enveloped in flame. Witness ran to throw his slop on hitn,but he ran away. Others ran attar him and he fell. Then they got the flames out, and he was taken to Dr. Twyford's.—Was Mason doing something he ought not to have done when he poured the paraffin in ?—lt was the usual thing to do. Johnson, the foreman, had or- dered it. Witness said be was the leading hand, and Mason was assisting. He thought it was safe consider- ing the tow temperture. They did not expect it to boil. Samuel Mason was called, and said he poured about a gill of oil in the hole. He understood Johnson had given the order for oil to be used.</p>
        <p>The Coroner at this stage said the points were resolv- ing themselves into a small compass. The facts were admitted. The only question was whether the men had a right to do this. ,k William Akers nson, the foreman said he ordered a pipe to be used for thefoil ,to be poured tl rough. If he had been there he would not have allowed them to pour the oil from a bottle. If is had been red hot he would not have ordered oil at all. He thought the oil had been thrown in. and that it got on the heaters and flew off. That was his theory. The Government Inspector considered- the use of paraffin on iron, even when it was only at black beat, very foolish. There was nothing to show what kind of paraffin was used, and a great deal would depend upon that...as to how soon it would explode. He considered the use of paraffin, when the heaters were there, very reprehensible . _</p>
        <p>'The Coroner said the eircumsttnees were rendered the more sorrowful because deceased was only a passer-by, and not engaged on the work. The jury had to consider Whether it was a wise thing for the menu use paraffin if even they had carried out the foreman's orders to the letter, which they did not appear to have done. The jury returned a verdict of " Aceideiat4 death," and expressed the opinion that paraffin should not have been used on hot iron. The Coroner hoped the foreman would Caii-Y into effect both the expression of opinien and the verdict of the jury. Some surprise was expressed that Mesers.Dag- fish were tilt officially represented at the inquiry, and that their foreman had to be sent for during the in- quiry.</p>
        <p>YOUR ATTENTION, PLEASE t Sufferers from Gravel, Lumbago. Piles, Pains in the 13,,ck, Dropsy, Wind and Water Complaints. Diseases of Kidneys, Bladder,. Urinary Organs, Stone, meet, Stria. titre. i,dattcu. Itheutriat lam, and Gout, will find a positive mire lii Iloldroyd's Gravel Pills. Try a email box, and if not satisAed your money will 7 ere! urned. Price 1s 11d.. Of all nPrrilStK, or pOit fre f, r 12 stamps from HOLD- Medical HAM C tech heat, n, Yorke. Don't be put oft yoo natinct get thdrrt write to the PrOprietor, (ma a box will be sent seat pat.—Advt. ,</p>
