      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>"„* Secretaries of Footb,dl Clubs are requested to note Mai reports of matches played on Saturday must be sent to the head office, Bewsey-street. Warrington, not later than ten o'clock on Wednesday morning.</p>
        <p>GEORGB G. BUS3EY and Co. are leading makers of Footballs, Hockey, Golf, and requisites for Sports Games, and Gymnasia. Catalogue may be had on application to the Sport. Manufactory, Peckham, London, S.E. BEST 46694 t BRAND.</p>
        <p>RUGBY. WARRINGTON v. BROUGHTON RANGERS.</p>
        <p>It was an important day on Saturday for Warrington when they met the Broughton Rangers Club, and, when for the first time during a period of seven years they were beaten by the Rangers. There was a very large number of spectators on the Rangers ground including Mr. W. H. Holland, M.P., and Mr. Platt Higgins, M.P., both of whom acted as starters during the game. The visitors were without C. Potts, and W. Nevins, the vacancies being filled by J.T.Masscy and H.Teare. It was no small advantage to Broughton to win the toss seeing that they had the benefit of the elements for forty minutes. Mr. Holland started the game on behalf of Warrington, and his kick off having no reply the early phases of the game saw Warrington aggressive. Berry, I Jolley, and Turner were conspicuous in some dashing Iforward play, but the invasion was only of brief dura- tion, and Dalton, Oman, and Medley took ;the oval beyond the centre line. It was early seen that cutting down tactics were the order of the day, and the speed the game was started at was palpably too hot to last. A temporary lull in the struggle left the ball close to the , Warrington goal, and whilst in this very desirable spot I —from a Banger's point of view—Thompson succeeded in whipping back to ihhitehead,and the latter succeeded lin dropping a goal. Nothing daunted by such a trivial proceeding as this, the " wire-pullers" carried war into the enemy's country, the two Bates playing a beautiful game, whilst Jolley and Berry were making the Rangers hum forward. Favoured by a still breeze the Rangers cleared, and following certain acrobatic evolu- tions on the part of the Warrington hacks, the game was stayed once more under their own goal. Despite the fact that the Rangers had established such a commendable lead, the Warringtonians were still hopeful but on the other hand the Rangers were determined. 'The latter early gave evidence of their intentions, and capital forward play terminated in their attacking. Jas. Bate tricked his opponents splendidly and passed to Massey judiciously, but the latter preferred to punt, in- stead of accepting the opening. offered. The game was hotly contested here, and from A pass by Evans, Berry ran too near the line, and Hakim got over, but a late ap- peal of the Rangers' toush judge was allowed. Follow- ing this let off, the hanesters forced the pace, and the wind having dropped considerably the Rangers' backs were seen to advantage with several kicks. Boscow checked one determined essay, but Jolley at three-quar- ter was forced behind his lines. The succeeding scrim- mage saw James Bate clearing beautifully, and another free kick to turner was also unsuccessful. The Ran- gers' forwards showed tha better combination, and, as a result, the visitors backs had but few chances, though Bate gate Massey another pass which might have been turned to good advantage. Jolley saved a certain try in kicking dead, whilst Gresty the next minute was tackled on the line. The visitors again relieved, this time Thornily doing the trick..a run, kick and tackle, eliciting cheers. Bandies next , made his mark near touch and on the centre line, and Turner had another shot at goal, and alsplendid kick registered Warrington's only tangible point, this being the last interesting item of a capital game. Final score: Broughton Rangers, two dropped goals and a try (eleven points) ; Warrington, one goal (four points). Teams :—Br.mghton Rangers : J. Hacking, back; E. Gresty, H. Chapman, R. White- head, and Sam Todd, three-quarter backs; C.Thompson, son and R. Harwood, half-hacks ; Skidmore Squires, Medley,Oman, Dalton, Nowell, Campbell and Skidmore, forwards. Warrington : J. Boscow, back ; F. Heesorn,J. W. Evans, and J. T. Massey, three-quarter backs; Jas. Bate and John Bate (captain), half-backs ; Fair Turner, J. Jolley, H. Tears, J. Berry, W. Bandies, T. Donohue.J. Thorniley„ J.' Holmes and J. Dakin, forwards. Re- feree,Mr. J. Mills, Swinton F.C.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON v. STOCKPORT.—The Warringtonianr paid their annual visit to Stockport on Monday, this day be- ing the opening of the annual holiday in the Cheshire borough. There was a gate considerably over 6,000 present, and when the teams lined up it was seen that the visitors were but moderately represented,John Bats, Evans and Nevins, all being absentees. Potts started for Stockport, ad the game opened fast. The teams were evenly matched in the opening stages, but having the aid of the elements Warrington eventually bore down on the Stockport goal, mainly, through the in- strumentality of Bate, Jolley and Dakin. Bate got cleverly away from a scrimmage, and this effort was. succeeded by some capital passing by the Warrington backs, Beesom thking, the oval too near the line. Stock- port touched down, and in turn Boscow was compelled to adopt the saving clause. The visitors renewed the attack, and Ilaslam was once more forced to save, and although Bailey WWI to the fore with a capital run. Bos- cow averted disaster with a fair catch, and piloted back to the Stockport 25. A too strict attention towards Lally and Bate forced Stockport en their goal line, but Saville relieved with a judicious kick. Dakin ably led a rush from a line-out, but the visitors experienced hard lines, when Lally and Bate pissed well, and Jolley was held outside. Scrimmages were the order, but eventually Warrington gained a sub- stantial point,James Bats dodging over, but Fair Turner failed to augment. Bate was almost in again a minute later,and Boscow with useful kicks helped the leaders to maintain their aggressive tactics. A combined rush of the home team was Well saved by, Potts, and then Mas- sey forced Stockport beyond their quarter flag, and half time arrived with the score as Watrington, 0-1-3 ; Stock- port, 0-0.2. Restarting Saville responded to Turner's kick, and although Warrington tfeld the Stockport men at bay for a considerable period, the combination of the home forwards had its effect, and Warrington were forced to adopt a defensive attitude. Smart kicking by both sides ended in Pickard scoring a try,which Saville improved upon,while the dose was repeated a few minu- tes later, both exponents being responsible for the try and goal. Turner experienced hard luck on a shot at goal from a fair catch,and although Lally got clear from a scrimmage, and looked likely' to score, he was pulled up. In the end Warrington had to retire defeated for the second time this season. the final score being—Stockport, 2-0-3; Warrington, 044. 'reams :—Warrington : Bos- cow, back • Ileesom. Potts. and Massey, three-quarter hacks ; Bate and Lally, half backs-; Fair; Turuer,Jolley. Dakin, Handles, Berry, Holmes, Teare, Donohue, and Thorniley,forwards. Stockport : Flaslam,back ; Saville, Ashton, and Bailey,three-quarter backs ; Chorlton, Pick- ard, and Bellamy, half hacks; Potts. Bailey, Speet,Mor- ton, Mottershead, Beard, Smith, and Aldred, forward.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON A v. I.SROUORTON nANORRS A.—These two teams met at Wilderspool-road on Saturday to play off their first fixture this season. Considering the weather and the distress in Warrington there was a very good " gate " for an A team . match. The Rangers came attired in their new war paint, hr jersey if that suits better, and a very nest jersey it is with its narrow rine</p>
        <p>ot dark blue and white. Warrington won the toss and set their opponents to defend the railway end. At the commencement of the game the Rangers went away with a rush and Warrington had some difficulty in keep- ing their lines intact. After a short time, however, Warrington beat them back, and Barnes intercepting an opponent's pass, dodged the wnole of the Rangers but the full back. He, however, neatly passed to Lawless who ran in, and Ashurst failed to kick a goal. After some desultory play, Barnes again got to the line, and he and an opponent dropped on the ball together,the referee only awarding a minor. The Warringtonians would not be denied and Lilly got over, Barnes this time doing the needful. The game after this to the interval was of a very curious nature, each side appearing to be quite disorganised, and the half time arrived with the result—Warrington 1-1-2, Broughton Rangers nil. After the interval the Rangers pressed for sometime appearing several times to be on the point of scoring, but failed to put the finishing touches on. At length from a break away of Warrington,Lawless passed toLally who scored his second try and Barnes kicked ,his second goah. The Rangers turn after so much bad luck came at last when Howe scored a splendid try which was, however, unim- proved. From the kick out Warrington broke away and but for Cummings, the visiting full back, they would have scored. Cummings, who appeared to have awakened up near the end of the game, immediately afterwards secured the ball and got through the whole of the Warrington pack, and even the backs, but Barnes proved to be the quicker runner, and grassed him wneu he had a clear field before him. Soon afterwards the whistle blew with the result—Warrington 2-1-2, Rangers 0-1-0.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN T. Sr. HELENS.—Runcorn, keeping up their reputation, found a team capable of coping with the St. Helens club at Runcorn on Saturday. Following up the defeat of the Wigan team St. Helens were beaten at all points by lw goals to nothing, and had to struggle hard to keep the score down. Faulkner played centre, and scored a very creditable try. Williams then scored after a mull by Bell, Warder kicking goals from both tries. Another try was scored, which. the referee disallowed, eventually owning that he had made an error, and ex- pressed his regret. This, however, did not get Runcorn the other three 'points. Forward was Runcorn's forte, the pushers working like horses', their brilliant following up giving Cross, Sudlow, and Dogherty no possible chance. Houghton never played finer defensive play than he did in this match. With more speed Faulkner will fill Crompton's position with credit. Abrams and Warder gave fine expositions of the game.</p>
        <p>WIDNIIB V. tarmarrza.—Widnes played their first match in the second division of the Club Championship op Saturday, Lancaster being the visitors. Both teams were represented by their full strength. Owing to the high wind which prevailed the referee ordered four quer- ters to be played. The game was a very rough one,both claim being offenders in this respect, and consequently play was not at all interesting from a spectator's point of view. After the first quarter Lancaster were left with 14 men, but Widnes failed to score before the interval, although they did a tot of pressing. Early in the second half Johnson gave Widnes the lead by scoring a grand try behind the pasts, and Drummond kicked a goal. This proved to bo the only score, and Widnes registered two points in the competition. Brennan, at back, was a tower of strength to the home team, and although suffering from a severe kick (received early in the game) he played grandly throughout. Barber was the best of the three-quarters, while the pick of the for- wards were Johnson, Foster and Fraser.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON RANGERS IST. V. BEDFORD CHURCE.—• Played on the Rangers ground on Saturday afternoon before a good number of spectators. The visitors kicked off, but owing to offside the ball was scrimmaged at centre. It was early evident that the game was to be keenly contested, as both sides displayed good football ; indeed the passin on both sides was considerably above the average. The Rangers who had the wind In their favour, slightly had the best of matters, as, after several futile efforts to score, a pretty exhibition by Deakin, G. Savage and J. Savage allowed the latter to secure a meritorious try, which was not improved upon. The Churchmen strove hard to equalise but the home team set up a strong defence which repelled any serious inva- sion, and finally Bedford who have the reputation of going through last season's fixtures without a lass were defeated by 1 try to nil.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON 2ND V. PORT SIINLIGHT..—PIayed at Beb. ington on Saturday. In the absence of Mr. Lever, Mr. J. Faulkner kicked off for Warrington, and no return being made the visitors at once took up the attack, and after a series of scrimmages Rowe scored, but the referee did not allow the point. However,Warrington were not to be denied, and a pretty bit of passing by Boyle, Mason, Ed'eaten, and Raybold ended in the last-named scoring. The kick at goal proved futile. From the drop-out Ed- lesten got hold, and a nice run and kick by this player nearly ended in a major point being scored, but Spencer cleared his lines splendidly. Sunlight now had a turn at pressing, and by the aid of Cousins and Peacock took the ball dangerously near the visitors' lines, where play raged for a few minutes. From a loose scrimmage Sun- light looked like scoring, but Edlesten was again to the front with a huge punt to the home full back, and the Warrington forwards following up Spencer was tackled with the ball in his possession, and from the succeeding scrimmage Boyle got over, Mason kicking a neat goal. Half-time score :—Warrington, 7. goal 1 try to nil. The play in the second half was interesting, and when time was called Warrington were the winners by lgoal 2 tries to Port Sunlight nothing.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON ST. MARY'S V. WARRINGTON BRIIWERY. This match was played on the ground of the latter on Saturday. The Saints were short of Hammon and Ray- bold, Edmonds and W. linughley being called upon to play, and efficaciously filled the vacancies. After a rough game the result read, St. Mary's two tries fivs minors to 3 minors. WARRINGTON BANGERS 2ND V. BEDFORD LEIGH 2ND.— The second team, of the Rangers journeyed by bus to Bedford Leigh on Saturday. The two team,' were very even, for at the call of time neither side had scored any major point, the match ending in a draw.</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH V. BEDFORD Sr. TOSEPR'S.—This _match took place at Leigh, on Saturday. Stockton Heath ran out victors by one goal one try and good form was displayed by Barber and Glover.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN v. Sr. HELENS.--A TRAMS.—At St. Helens on Saturday. Runcorn sustained their reputation by winning, the score reading Runcorn one goal, three tries, St. Helens nil. Woodward dropped a goal and Hollings- worth (2) and Evans scored tries.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN RECRE&amp;TION V. WIDNILS A TRAM.—At Run- corn. • Widnes won by one goal, one try to Recreation one try. HALTON v. APPLETON.— At Halton; the home team won by one goal, one try to one goal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>FRIENDS' SCHOCIt, PENEETN, V. FAIINVFORTH.—These teams met at Penketh the ground being In splendid con- dition. The school, losing the toss, had to play with the wind at their hacks. The start was rather sensational. At the kick-off, Smith passed the ball to Charnock, who passed back, then from Smith the ball went to Reynolds, who thus, in the first minute, scored with an easy shot the Farnsworth men never having touched the ball. The. second goal was scored by Smith rushing the goal- keeper, who had mulled a shot from Pollard. Score at half-time : School 2 • Farnworth, O. Upon resuming, the School hacks deffmded well, but at last Farnworth scored from a corner. Final result Friends' School, 2; Farnworth, 1.</p>
        <p>PENICICTII VILLAGE V. WARRINGTON Sr. ELVIIIN'S.— This match which was played at Penketh on Saturday was witnessed by 500 spectators. Hatton kicked off for the Saints, and socn a stiff game was anticipated, and about five minutes after the start the visitors scored the first goal. This aroused the Penkethites, who Up to this time had made a capital defence. Re-starting the home team pressed hard, but without effect until near half- time, when Dumbill with a long allot passed it on tO Bowyer. who was near the goal, and the latter sent the ball spinning through the goal amidst acclamation, making the score equal at half-time. In the second half Penketh succeeded in scorin/ three more goals. St. Elphin's one goal,the mate); ending in favour of Penketh by four goals tl two. PADGATE V. WARRINGTON UNITED.—Played at Padgate on Saturday. After twenty minutes play E. Howard scored from centre half with a grand shot. The home team had all the best of the game. ci n a Is afterwards being scored by W. Allen (2), Weston (1), S. Howard (2), and W. Weston. (1). The final score being Padgate seven goals. United nil</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>CHARGE AGAINST A JEWISH RABBI.</head>
        <p>CHARGE AGAINST A JEWISH RABBI.</p>
        <p>The Rev. Mr. Littman, pastor of the Jewish Syna- gogue in Aberdeen, has been summoned to appear at the police court on the charge of cruelty to animals, the charge being that the reverend gentlemanl. while slaughtering a bullock for ' the use of Hebrews in that city, cruelly ill-treated and tortured the animal by cut- ting its threat in an unskilled manner, causing great and unnecessary pain before it died. Dr. Adler, the Chief Rabbi, has written saying that he feels confident that in Scotland, where the Hebrew Scriptures are so deeply venerated, nothing will be done to prevent his co-religionists from obeying the dictates of their reli- gion, and preparing their food in accordance with Mosaic law. He adds that the system is common in in London, and has not heel objected to. The sum - Mons will be tried next week:</p>
        <p>The wife of Mr. W. O'Brien, M.P., has received, under the will of her father, a handsome addition to her fortune.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>MEDICAL.</head>
        <p>MEDICAL.</p>
        <p>A CAlto._</p>
        <p>pEVEREND JOSEPH HOLMES' improvAtl 'LW Mexican Prescriptian, as discovered in the hot table- Jul. of Old Mexico, is a guaranteed cure to all those whs ?miler from the errors and indiscretions of youth, neiv,us weakness, physical exhaustion, and early decay.. To intro- duce it into lingland, the Mexican Prescription will be a-nt freo of charge. upon receipt of a self addressed stamped envelope. Only address: JOSEPH HoLBIES' Remedy Co., Plomnsbury Mansions, Bloomsbury-square, London, W.O. I.:..ention this taper.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>'hest</head>
        <p>'hest</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS LICENSING SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>ALTERATIONS OF BACK DOORS. THE BLACK LIST.</p>
        <p>The adjourned licensing session for the borough of St. ifelens was held on Monday, the magistrates present being the Mayor (Mr. Sinclair), Aldermen Ilarrison, Cook, and Bishop, Councillors Martin, Forster, and Burchall. and Messrs. R. A. Gaskell, T. Glover, and J. Cotton. The first business was the consideration of a list of licensed victuallers and beerhouse keepers to whom objection had been taken by the Chief Constable on account of the back door arrange- ment.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. 0. Swift said he appeared for Messrs. Greenall, Whitley, and Co., who were the owners of a number of houses included in the list. He (Mr. Swift) had advised his clients that as a matter of law the Bench was not entitled to insist (with regard to beerhouses) upon any alteration being made. But Messrs. iireenall had in- structed him to say that the objections which had been stated by the Chief Constable were objections well founded on the ground of public interest, it not of valid objection in law. In every case in which a satisfactory alteration could be made without considerable structural alteration, that alteration had already been made,andin the other cases where building would be required for which plans would have to be submitted to the Corpora- tion the firm had had plans made, and in every one of them they were prepared to cirry out alterations which would be satisfactory ta the Bench and to the Corpora- tion, and they would improve the premises in the way the Bench required. Mr. .1. Massey, Mr. R. W. H. Thomas, and Mr. W. Webster appeared fer other persons in the list, and on their behalf promised the necessary alterations should be made. _ . .</p>
        <p>The magistrates retired, and on returning the Mayor expressed the sense of satisfaction felt by the Bench that in almost every instance their wishes had been satisfactorily complied with. In two cases were wire- guards had been put over windows they had to ask that such windows should be barred with iron a quarter of an inch thick, so that bottles could not be passed through In one case, that of the Travellers Rest, Liverpool-et., no satisfactory answer had been given, and that case ; would be adjourned until Wednesday to give the owners an opportunity of coming to court and satisfying the requirements of the Bench. The magistrates wished the Chief Constable to take it as an instruction to report ,at the first transfer sessions in the new year how many of these undertakings had been carried out. subject to those remarks, the Bench were prepared to renew all : the licenses with the exception of the Travellers' Rest. , The' blacklist" was then taken. It consisted of four , names—William Peet, Union Hotel, convicted of being drunk ; William Whittamore, Bird-P-th'-Hand Hotel, drunk in charge of a horse and tap; Thomas Clunb (present tenant James Johnson), Railway Inn Beerhouse , Parr, opening his licensed premises during prohibited hours ; and James Gerratty, Farmer's Arms Beerhouse, similar offence. Mr. J. 0. Swift appeared for the whole of the applicants. Chief Constable Wood objected to the renewal of the off spirit license of Mrs. Burchall, King-st., on the ground that although it had been t 'ken out regularly it had not been used. Mr. Swift submitttd that the bench had no power to refuse, and Mrs. Bur- chall now intended that the license should be put into effect.</p>
        <p>The magistrates said they were guided by leniency in renewing the licenses of William Peet and William Whit- tamore, who had been convicted of one solitary instance of being drunk ;but they had decided to refuse to renew the licenses of Thomas Clubb and William cierratty, who had been convicted of opening their licensed premises during prohibited hours. They would also refuse to renew the spirit license of Mrs. Burchall, on the ground that such premises had not been used for some years past for the sale of spirits.</p>
        <p>The business of the sessions was concluded on Wed- nesday. The members of the Licensing Committee pre- sent were the Mayor (Ms. Sinclair), Aldermen Harrison and Cook, Councinsr Forster, and Mr. Sharpies. Two applications for new licenses ' from residents in the Denton's-green district, which has just been included in the borough extension, were on the list but they were withdrawn.—Mr. J. 0 Swift said the only application was by Alfred Hardy, of the Boilermakers' Arms beer- house, Norman's-road, Sutton, for a wine licence, to sell either on or off the premires. The magistrates refused the application.—The only other point 4hich occupied the attention of the court was in regard to the Travel- ler's Rest Inn, Liverpool-st., belonging to Messrs. For- shaw, brewers, of Ormskirk. The magistrates on Mon- day were not satisfied with the backyard arrangements. Mr. Swift now appeared for the owners, and said the best arrangements would be made,even if it necessitated the taking down of a cottage adjoining. On this under- taking ths license was renewed.—Mr. Swift gave notice of appeal in respect of the Railway Ina, Fleet-lane, and the Farmers' Arms, Market-place, the magistrates hav- ing refused to renew the licences on account of the ten- ants having been convicted during the past year of keep- ing their houSes open during prohibited hours. Mr. Swift said that instead of entering into recognisances. the sum of £4O in each case would be deposited with the clerk of the court.</p>
        <p>MR. GLADSTONE IN EDINBURGII.</p>
        <p>NATIONAL LIFE SAVING COMPETITfv' kt In order to make known more fully the sill,- toe I objects of the Life Saving Society, and to encoutsV,tiO • teaching of practical rescue work in schools, swill/-vest clubs and similar institutions the Society last organized a National Lite Saving Competition, oeecco teams (of four persons in each team) in England. land, and Ireland, and the Channel Islands ; the Viol entering are divided into districts and subdivide9lo, divisions, not fewer than tour teams forming a div',s;o- - The names of each team in each division arelhen P`"too - in one lot, and drawn in couples at the time. The 05 • teams thus drawn compete against each other theo drawn has choice of venue, As soon as the di°,i,s,llott" competitions are finished, the winners are drawn in their respective districts, and the district ";;:ft compete against each other until two teams ars, '",.0 01) for the final struggle, which this year will take Piaiord' the 30th September, at the Excelsior Baths, INtl°s„tatO street, Bethnal Green-road. The prizes in this inallo,'„os, event consist of a Challenge Shield, value 50 presented to the Society by Messrs. Lever Bros. provide low as a perpetual trophy, and in addition they gold, four silver, and eight bronze menals for the 'loyal ing teams in the penultimate and final ties. T,lr 1,00 value of prizes given for this event amount to Ds"-",.ered, year 24 teams from various parts of the country and as each win numbered four persons ; ninS in individuals competed, which was a best on renorstili team competitions; hut this year the result has be!rl, in more satisfactory, as 32 teams have been coroPet'lllkt the divisional and district rounds, which have been - in England and Scotland with great snccess.</p>
