        <p>'quits ' ??, Sap IMPORTANT SPEECH. TIM HOUSE OF LORDS AND HOME 1117"' Mr. Gladstone left Blackcraig at two o'clock on Virbo74 nesday afternoon and drove to Cargill, a station ten miles north of Perth, where a special train waiting for the conveyance of the right hon. gentletev7_, and his friends to Edinburgh. The Prime Minister ;2, accompanied by Mrs. Gladstone, Mr. Armitstead,was a ro Algernon West, and Mr. Henry Gladstone. 'filete: ser- no stoppage or demonstration on the route. The 0104 est taken in the visit was intense. A large awaited the arrival of the Premier at the station, ISt gave him an enthusiastic reception. The arrival Pt of form is on the west side of the station, and this pat the building was completely cut off from the other stet by the erection of a large barricade. Opposite the If where the right hon. gentleman was to alight a tits, platform had been erected, covered with crimson °viho Admission to the station was by ticket, and thosere had been fortunate enough to secure these Pilaes:geke ranged on the two platforms on each side of the oar-,„,a• way. Although timed to arrive at 4.30, the train ^to. signalled ten minutes before that time, and at three minutes past four it came slowly ibt the ststiod, The first of the party to alight was Mr. Henry tiliaod stone, and a cheer was raised as be. ,a' with Sir Algernon West, stepped out of ,se train. In a short while the well known .a of the Premier was seen, and this was the signal for 510 e lo ud cheer. As he alighted, along with Mrs. Glaqshl9,o, Mr. Cowan at once stepped forward and welcome° "-„s, Then Mr. Miller shook bands with Mr. Gladstone a„",„. the Lord Provost warmly welcomed him. Mr. and Gladstone were at once conducted to Mr. Cowan's t" 0 rd cov riage, and, preceded by two mounted policemen, e. slowly out of the station, the people cheering lustllf,a following the carriage out to the street. In other ca riages were Mr. Armitsteati and Mr. Honry GladstOne; Outside the Etation the crowd was a very largo antig thusiastic one. A 4 vehicular traffic going or o.o6Thle from the west had been suspended. Loud cheeva I hale announced the arrival of Mr. Gladstone, and a 1:441 later he emerged from the station. The weather. sv";bis- afterwards became rather unsettled, was fair at ".„r, time, enabling the crowd to get a view of tho Fvertii-ed and they cheered him most enthusiastically ; the 01,1 statesman responded by raising his hat. On the ar,rlg;io, the hall at Queensferry.lane, Mr., Gladstone seeir.nets the best of spirits, nodding to all and sundry. of admission to the meeting had been ate prettio The Albert Hall, where the a isemblv was held, totally inadequate to meet a thud of the deniliall",ds seats, the doors of the hall had been beseiged by of ticket holders from three o'clock. Half-an-hour wo, fore the time agreed on they were open, and soon the hall was packed to the The audience for a long time WAS pre-eMinernice a Committee one—quiet, earnest, and undem.inst,?),"sol during the time of waiting. Sir Thomas H. u-sse Carmichael was the first to receive a recognition bY audience ; and, following him, Mr. T. Shaw, also warmly received. Mr. and Mrs. ChilderS loudly cheered on taking their places on the platfor,"cx About ten minutes to five r'clock someone in the e of the hall struck up "Scots wha hae," and tbe body of the audience heartily joined in the reirne Immediately afterwards Mr. John Cowan, who vv,a'',„tie preside, appe wed on the platform, leading Mrs. of stone. This was the occasion for a great outbutet,rbe cheering,t he whole audience springing to their feet* ssr. enthusiasm was redoubled when a moment late of Gladstone was perceived making his way up the 5te9.5,0 the platform. Elie cheering was vociferous ;IS the rig„s hon. gentleman passed to his seat. Mr. Gladstone u'ses a stick in walking, and though fresh and ruddy, ileeTast to be not quite so robust as when in Mid-Lothian year. „re: After the Chairman's brief speech, in which he sented an address of welcome to the Premier, the,3„-sd ence again received Mr. Gladstone with prolonge° -0 of enthusiastic cheering, followed by the lusty singitne r "He's a jolly good fellow." As soon as Mr. Gla commenced his address, it was observable that the 8„,1104. erica were following his remarks with great eaget"stit They had come to hear about the House of Lords, 110 they listened with the closest attention to all that „"0; Premier had to say regarding measures in which 'st, only Mid-Lothian but Scotland, had a deep interej, sr, The reference to the Disestablishment Bill, in partle',oo, evoked a most encouraging cheer, and must have his vinced the Liberal Leader that he had the support °:.(43, Mid-Lothian Committee in dealing with this rieeeojeet, When Mr. Gladstone went on to touch questions a"ttsi ing the United Kingdom, the interest evinced LI eOI audience in no way abated, but it obviously isereati7o when he approached the suleject of the till of Lards. The quotation from Mr. Chamber fot speech at Bingley Hall was a sweet morsel the audience, who laughed heartily av die Gladstone read out slowly and deliberatel% rr opinion of the mernher for West Birminghani in 1138'bir garding the Upper Chamber. The retrospect of the os tory of the House of Lords received due attention, ate led up step by step to the question of what Was t„°,oli. dose with that House at the ' present crisis. The lir; re cation of the retrospect was exactly what the ao"ted • had been led to expect would be the reined)' Begg-the by Mr. Gladstone, and when at last he declared thated nation would not be baffled by a phalanx of rive ho' 4 peers, the cheering was loud and prolonged. Thet„-,hll no mistaking the fact that the audience were VlOlOlOO earnest. They seemed inclined even to go farther td the right hon. gentleman, and to be quite preParc„oSe abolish the House of Peers right off. 'The Premier s,etse in excellent voice, and was well heard through°°"„-IV hall. On leaving, Mr. Gladstone was again vocifev°7:od cheered, the crowd extending along Princes-sta "of greeting the right hon. gentleman most enthusiast° as he drove along to the hotel.</p>
