      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>CHURCH AFFAIRS AT ORFORD.</head>
        <p>CHURCH AFFAIRS AT ORFORD.</p>
        <p>THE RETIRING CHAPLAIN.</p>
        <p>On Sunday, the annual harvest festival services were held at Orford- Church, the preacher in the morning being the Rev. El. Gibson-Smith, rector of Halewood, and in the evening the Rev. Isaac Phillips, M.A., chaplain. In the afternoon a chil- dren's service was held. The, proceedings in the morning were rendered additionally intiresting owing to the large number of communicants. We understand that there are only 350 parishioners, but no fewer than 53 attended Holy Communion, which was held at the close of the service. In the evening, when the church was crowded, the Chaplain, who is leaving the parish owing to circumstances already reported, preached his last sermon, the proceedings being of a very affecting character. In concluding his sermon the rev. gentleman said that God's work there had begun and was going on. During his nine years he had observed progress. He knew that God having begun his work there would con- tinue it to the end. He was much interested in the spiritual welfare of the people there. How could it be otherwise? As Christ's minister he had baptized, in that small place of 350 people, more than 70 of their children. He had thus taken part in starting them on the heavenly march. He had always been most earne in hisennver- sation with candidates for confirmation, and in this he felt sure the parents of the children would-bear him out Sixty at least had been confirmed during his ministry amongst them, and these were often to be seen at the Lord's table from time to time. When he spoke to them he always had their highest interest in view. Would that he could have done more, but God forgive him for what he had left undene. Might it be that none in Orford should lose by what he had done or said. His desire had been to help them on the heavenly way. Ho _trusted that his efforts had not been in vain. The church had, as usual, been very effectively decorated for the occasion by the following :—Mrs. Beamont, Mrs. C. Grounds, the Misses Pollitt, Phillips, Grounds, Smith, Woolmer, Woods, Richardson, Melbourne, Fisher, Massey, M. Richardson, E. Woods, and Messrs. Henshull and Richardson. Appropriate harvest hymns were sung, as well as anthems, Mr. Cann being at the harmonium.</p>
        <p>On Sunday afternoon,at the children's harvest festival services,a very interesting presentation was made to the Rev .Isaac and Mrs. Phillips by the teachers and scholars. It took the form of a handsonae tea tray of polished cak, with Aver mountings. Some cutlery was also given. In the centre ef‘the tray,on a silver plate, was the fol- lowing inscriptionei--" Presented. as a token of esteem. to the Rev. I. and ra. Phillips, by the teachers and scholars of Orford Sunda?, School. September, 1893." Accompanying the tray was a small hook containing the names of the subscribers,who numbered about 130. The Presentation was made by two young ladies, the Misses Esther Woods and Jessie Monks.on behalf of the teachers and scholars. Both Mr. and Mrs. Phillips, who were deeply affected, returned thanks,assuring the donors that the gifts would be highly prized by them.</p>
        <p>" Mr. Stanley," says Rr. Carl Peters, "is as much underrated now as he was formerly overrated. He is a clever man. He knows how to get money, Some ex- plorers travelled like tramps. Mr. Stanley had big tents, plenty to eat and drink, and he travelled slowly. Mr. Stanley was a journalist. He knew how to get himself into public notice. Other exAorers before him had done as much aid more. They are saarcel3F known." The Royal Laundresses say Critchley's " STARCH ()toss Is unequalled for imparting a beautiful finish to sten:lll6i 1, nen „ &amp;e., either cold or boiling water starch. Mr. Corn- wall, hays :—.•O My wife will not finish our linen without your " Starch Gloss." From Mrs. —, Bradford : "Dear eli.— lan pleased to intorm you that I have tried many so-called Starch Glazes and Polishes, but toad none equal to yours." It should be used in every house. Sold at id., 3d., and ed., by .00st starch sellets. If not in stock will procure It. T. CSITCHLEY. Blackburn. r.morietor.—Adst.</p>
