        <head>LITERARY -POLICEMAN.</head>
        <p>A LITERARY -POLICEMAN.</p>
        <p>-- A literary policeman is at present spending ID' TO earned holiday in the Reading Room of the 13:1,rbe Museum engaged upon a work to be eptitled Bibliography of Wales." Mr. Charles Ashton, w" ip born in 1848, entered the Merionethshire Nice Force of 1869, and is still a constable in a village at the f.)O it, to Ceder Idris. In his leisure boors he devotes hiMgeL,A.. • literature, and after winning many prizes At the EIT:e, dfodau, has recently gained ono at the Eisteddfod • cently held at Chicago. • c• 13 The G.O.M. of Keighley, Sir Isaac Bolden, the" f)elcbrity at Home" in the "World," and cription is given of the far-famed gardens and conser!„ tories Oakworth that might make a marquis envir tTA:i. • Sir Isaac told his interviewer once again the secret od long life—care in his diet, with plenty of fruit, plenty of open-air exercise. It is a far cry from 9`,' humble position of a teacher in a Leeds school to 1 splendour and luxury of Oakworth ; tut Sir Isaac Om' ' achieved the transition. lean Richard Hodgson, LL.D., secretary of the mner Society of Psychical Research, tells the follow ing.instariu. of iemarkable duality ; Ansel Bourne, an itineri?,., preacher, disappeared from his home in Greetie,. Two months afterwards he was discovered in NO% rr 2 own, Pa., where for six weeks he had been keePing small variety store under the name of A. J. Brown. appeared as a normal person, but was, in fae, " somnambulistic condition all the time. "TOBACCONISTS eommenclnogililibu. st jsrautsitdgnuroaldeci(:S. ' TOBAOCAINISTS' OUTFITTING c Est. 1356.-4dvt.</p>
