        <p>HIS HONOUR. JUDGE WINNE4TOULKES.</p>
        <p>"A TERROR TO EVIL-DOERS." Tne present number of " Toe Law Gazette " has an excellent portrait of his Honour, who is judge of the Warrington and other County Courts. It thus speaks of him :—His Honour William Wynne-Ffeulkes 'stile fourth son of the late John Powell Ffoulkes, of Eriviat Hall, co, Denbigh, his mother being the second daughter and co- heiress of Captain Robert Jocelyn, of Ste nstead Bury. Herta, and was born on the 14th July, 1821. His early education was imparted him at Shrewsbury School, where he gained his earliest laurtls, and from which he won a scholarship at Jesus College, Oxford, to which he proceeded in due course, taking his B.A. in 1841,and hie master's degree in 1847. From Oxford he entered at Lincoln's Inn, by which inn he was called to the bar in November, 1847, going thereafter the North Wales and Chester circuit. His father's connection and his own University reputation helped to win him briefs, which his untiring energy,his courteous manner,combined with his sound knowledge of law,gradually increased until Mr. Wynne-Ffoulkes had quite a good practice. Feeling him- self firmly placed the subject of our sketch felt it incum- bent on him to justify the old Biblical aphorism that it is not goad for man t,) be alone, and accordingly, on the 7th September, 1854, led to the altar Miss Elizabeth Benedicta, the sixth daughter ol the Rev. Dr. Howard, canon of Bangor. After a brief span of well-nigh unal- loyed happiness this lady died on the 28th March, 1855. Frmai this terrible blow it took Mr.Wynne-Ffoulkes long to recover, and to this day it has left traces of the cruel wound which it inflicted. Time, the great healer, how- ever, gently drew its mantle over the paq, and on the 13th September, 1859,he married Miss Hester Mary,third daughter of the Rev. George Heywood,rector of Ideford, Devon. In the meanwhile his practice was growing, leading to his being nominated a revising barrister in 1888, and a commissioner of escheat, Chester and North Wales,the duties of which office he admirably performed from 1872 to 1874. In the following year he received a well merited promotion as County Court judge of circuit No, 7. The appointment was universally recognised as a fitting and proper one. Ever since then his Honour has held the scales of justice evenly in the various courts constituting his circuit. His keen knowledge of commer- cial law has rendered his court of very great service to suitors.whilst his unsparing wrath on bogus and fraudu- lent actions of all sorts has render( d him a terror to evil doers. In manner he is, perhaps, a trifle too judicial, but this manner only masks as warm and sympathic a heart as ever beat. His kindnessess are many, his gem - rosity unostentatious, but wide. To his own profession he is ever ready to ext-nd the right band of fellowship, and no man has fewer enemies. Is any better commen- tary needed than that? A good man, and upright and wise, judge, a true friend, and,ahove all, a keen sympa- thiser with real distress and misfortune.</p>
      </div>
