      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>LONG LIFE.</head>
        <p>LONG LIFE.</p>
        <p>HOW TO LIVE A HUNDRED YEARS. The theories are various, but not so diverse as the practice. For on the one hand we have the famous example of Parr and Henry Jenkins, laborious, patient toilers, eating the coarest fare, and enjoying few plea- sures but those of fresh air and sound repose, and yet stretching the span of life well up to the third half- century. And on the other hand we have reminders enough but a little enjoyment does no great harm, and that the most Bohemian and light hearted of individuals may laugh and dance and banter the grim enemy out of his account, even for a century. Macklin, the actor is reported to have been a magnificent Shylock for about 30 years after the period allotted to mortal man by the Psalmist. And recently Chevreuil, the great French chemist, ended a wondeiful career of over five score years, being, according to his own description, simply a long continuous stretch of student life. In long lite, as in everything else, theory is all very well, but it's practice we want. OLDER THAN THE CENTURY. Yes, it is a stiff steeple-chase that hundred, when you look at it closely, and many a jaunty record breaker comes to grief ere half the course is run, and even when the last brush fence or stone wall is cleared, it is a hard and weary piece of work, the flogging up the straight. In 1792, and very early in the year,:he was born—James Smith, of Basingstoke. In 1792 ! Reflect on it for a moment. Louis XVI. was then on the throne of Prance. James Smith was able to walk and tale when Louis perished en the scaffold these things of ancient history. Robespierre was just rising into supreme power. And wandering about the streets of Paris eager, unsettled, ambitious, but often, despairing, wayward, and uncer- tain, was a young Corsican officer, sallow of complex- ion, lean of figure, " like a slip of parchment," with two dark wild-looking eyes, that had looked upon even Starvation. That was Napoleon Bonaparte, a man whom James Smith was destined afterwards to guard at St. Helena only a few short years after the " little Corsican officer" had made his doings the history of Europe and filled the world with pictures the most mar- vellous and fascinating ever known in the records of imitang. In 1792 George Stephenson was but a hard-tasked ti ehoY, and railroads, and locomotives, and steam- ships, and telegraphs were dreams of the future, Byron, jest four, had just begun to " feel his feet," and eight years had to run before Macaulay was born. • PORTRAIT OF A CENTENARIAN. And yet here I am speaking to James Smith, a 'man who guarded Napoleon Bonaparte at St. Helena, who was often as close to him, he says, as he is now to me. I loAk at him with a wonderful interest, quite pathetic it seems; but Smith sitting at home in his cottage, eating bread and jam and drinking his tea in cheerful and con- cerned only with the things of the hour. His counten- ance is less wrinkled than in many a man of 40,years younger. A fine wide forehead he has,a little knit over the eyebrows with all the thoughts of his The white locks ' are scanty round the temples and up towards the crown but elsewhere soft and abundant. The face is clean shaven, except for the fringe of white side whiskers,' land the white eyebrows project over eyes that still beam , with intelligence, although the light from outside is growing dimmer and dimmer. The features are good, plain, well marked, honest features, and one can easily imagine how some 80 years ago the description given of the English soldiers by Joseph in Erckmann—now Chat- rian's Waterloo would apply to Jas. Sinith—" fair,fresh, clean-shaven, decent-looking folk, like our bourgeoise." His figure is now a little bent and contorted with age, but in the prime of life he stood about sft. 51in. high, well made and taut and trim ; hut not remarkably , muscular, and never distinguished in athletics, he tells me. " Always a little weak about the loins," he says, and that troubles me now." Indeed, it appears that once doing some work in carrying sacks of grain for a farmer his want of lifting power made him suspected of ' laziness. He was always fairly active, however and prides himself on having been a good swimmer. THE SECRET. "And what is the wonderful secret of long life ?" at length I ventured to ask, when he has finished his tea. He looks puzzled, but his daughter, who is present-78 years of age and not a white hair !—explains that he is now getting deaf, and repeats my question in more de- liberate tones. He laughs. " None, none." He has had smallpox and the plague at Cawn- pore, he tells me, but not much besides. That was enough. "Were you always a temperate man." " Aye, aye. In India they gave us three.gills of spirit a day, I like the rum best. And in Sant•Elena (so he called it) we had a pint of Cape wine, fine, ricb, strong wine. It made some of them drunk," and he crooned over his reminiscences, " but I was never much given to that." "And do you like rum now No, he prefers now a nice drop of light mellow wine. Rum and spirits are too strong for his taste. HE SMOKED. There is no need to ask about smoking. He has drawn out of his pockets couple of long pipes, and takes one already charged with strong smoking tobacco, lights it himself, and as he puffs relapses into an air of being at peace with the world, his features blooming, and seem- ing 20 years younger. A kindly old soul he looked in his clouds of smoke, comfortable, but not fat, and bent on enjoying his whiff. As he pressed the tobacco with his finger, the hand, hard, dried, gnarled, and tanned looked like a carved and smoked piece of aid oak. "He didn't think much of the Ceylon tea," he said, in a tone of contempt. "Weak ! We used to get coffee in Ceylon, btit for tea—the good old China tea—the taste , and the aroma of that !" And then under the genial in- fluence of the tobacco be expands, and relates his ad- ventures and the store of his adventures and the store of his quaint recollections. He expresses himself remark- ably well, for he was educated at a " charity school, over the church porch," at a time when education was not greatly thought of for the people. Enlisted before 17, a soldier for a great many years, a labourer at 14s. a week, a rural postman for 3.45. a week, during over 20 long years, and then at length this patient waiting for the summons, the steady soldier to the last, never court- ing danger, dreading nothing.—The " Suu."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>PARACFIUTIS'FS ADVENTURE.</head>
        <p>A PARACFIUTIS'FS ADVENTURE.</p>
        <p>Charles Baldwin, " the prince of parachutists," is not yet 24. years old, good-looking, clean-shaven, and an exact counterpart of Lord Charles Beresford as he used to be. The greatest adventure he has yet had was at Stockport. There was a tearing wind, and the bal- loon, travelling at the rate of 40 miles an hour, made straight for a high chimney. The youthful navigator of the air saw that to go on was to be smashed, so he let go his hold and fell to the ground a distance of 601 t. He was not killed, but they picked him up fractured in several places. The latest from America is that one of the new towns of Montana is so extremely healthy that the inhabitants have had to kill a man to start a cemetery. It was useless waiting for •ne to die, as people never do die there.</p>
        <p>VITALITY REGAINED. Men and women suffering from loss of vitality, can 0-,tain relief by wearing HARNESS'S ELECTROPATHIC BELT, which conveys mild currents of electricity to the affected organs,and has relieved thousands. The cost is within reach of all. It is comfortable to wear ani (bee not in any way interfere With the ordinary occupation or attire. Send for consultation form and pamphlet free by post.—Address s Airs C. B. JlAxeaas, a, Oxford-street, Loudon, W (c).</p>
