      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>CONSUMED IN GREAT BRITAIN IS SOLD B 1</head>
        <p>CONSUMED IN GREAT BRITAIN IS SOLD B 1</p>
        <p>MARRIAGE AT ItI7NCORN.</p>
        <p>The marriage of Mr. George Alexander ()rice, of Waste Farm, Kingswood, Frodsham, second son of Mr. Alexander Once, of the Lodge, Halton, and Miss Eliza Hampson, eldest daughter of Mr. Richard Hampson, of Waterloo-road, Runcorn, was solemnized at the Runcorn Parish Church, on Saturday afternoon. The ceremony which was performed by the Rev. A. J. Jameson, was witnessed by a large number of friends. The bride looked very pretty attired in a cream coloured empire cloth dress, trimmed with velvet to match. She wore a large picture hat, trimmed with ostrich feathers. Her boquet was composed of yellow marguerites. Miss Clara Hampson (sister to the bride), Miss Once (sister to the bridegioom),and Miss Sallie Perkin acted as bridesmaids. They were attired in pale blue cashmere, with black lace hats, trimmed with forget-me-nots. They each carried a posy of scarlet gladioli, the gifts of the bride- groom. Afterwards breakfast was partaken of at the residence of Mr. Hampson. The wedding'guests included besides the bridal party Mr. and Mrs. Clare, Mr. Arthur Once, and Miss Handley, Miss Florrie. Clare, Mr. John Clare, Mrs. Orme' the .Misses Stringer, Mr. and Mrs. Williams, Mr. John Pennington, Miss Shield, and Mr. John Hampson. Later in the afternoon Mr. and Mrs. Grim departed for Blackpool where the honeymoon was to be spent. The following is a list of the presents received ; Mr. and Mrs. Hampson, dinner service, travelling trunk, bedroom lamp, &amp;c. ; Miss Hampson and Mr. Pe- nnington tea service; Mr. J. flampson, reading lamp ; Mr. and' Mrs.' Clare, pair of handsome vases; Mr. J. Clare, set of brass fireirons ; Miss F. B. Clare, handsome vase ; Mr. A. Once and Miss Handley, handsome marble timepiece ; Mr. P. Once, grey plush ladies' companion; Mr. A. Pennington,bread board and knife; Mrs.Acker- ley, coloured counterpane ; Misses Stringer,two damask tablecloths ; Miss C. Hamblett, damask tablecloth ; Mrs. Mangan, set of silver serviette rings •, Miss S. Perkin, pair of pictures, "La Cigale," and " Cupid ;" Miss Per- kin, set of d'oyleys ; Mrs. Shield, view and d'oyley ; Miss Shield, two cake stands; Mrs. Moulton, pair of band screens ; Miss C. Withington, china teapot; Mrs. and Miss F. Temple, teapot and coffeepot ; Miss 0. Riley, cream jug, sugar basin, teapot stand and,paste roller ; Mrs. and Misses Carter, set of toilet trinkets, pair orna- ments, and egg boiler ; Mrs. R. Abel,case with fish knife and fork ; Mrs. A. Abel, set of ornaments ; Mrs. Hender- son, handsome china cheese dish • Mr. Flenderson,plush handkerchief box with handkerchiefs ; Miss Henderson, pincushion ; Miss I. Henderson, silver salt ; Mrs. Hayes, pair of pin trays ; Mrs. Gorst and Miss Sergeant, hand- some white counterpane ; Miss Gorst, set of toilet mats ; Mrs. Coward, damask tablecloth,half dozen damask ser- viettes with ivory rings; Mrs.Jones,wicker flower stand; Mrs. One, coloured tablecloth ; Mr. Leach, plush table centre ; Miss Torkington, woollen antimacassar ; Mr. Williams, floral album.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>CLOTHES -REFORMER.</head>
        <p>A CLOTHES -REFORMER.</p>
        <p>M. Dilfenbach, the eccentric Bavarian painter, stolidly refuses to wear modern clothes which are an aboicina- tion to the artist's eye. Instead he parades the street.; garbed in the picturesque and flowing toga of yore. Just now he intends Visiting America to convert the Yankees to his way of thinking, then to establish a re- form colony over which he will reign supreme. Not only in matters of dress does he aim at the regeneration of mankind; he is a vegetarian and believes over-indul- gence in meat and strong drink to be at the bottom of all evil.</p>
        <p>-Lmeno••••••••••••••</p>
      </div>
