      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>EMPLOYERS' LIA.BILITY BILL.</head>
        <p>THE EMPLOYERS' LIA.BILITY BILL.</p>
        <p>Sir,—We are soon to have a new Em ployers' Liability Bill—an "all round" Bill. There has been much time and paper used with it, but they have lost sight of one point in the BM. They have not considered the question of the employer insuring out of it. The employer pays a few pounds, and he is sold out of all responsibility under the bill. Be can kill and poison young people until further orders, and if there is any litigarion it is with the insurance company under his name. It almost takes one's breath away to think of it—that we working men are sold again—from Burke the apostate down to the present time. But it strikes me they have been look- ing at the L &amp; N.-W.Bailway in.the ointment ; and,what is more, the labour memb-rs in Parliament are 80 dull in the matter and have not seen it. The Bill will go limp- ing all the days of its life if the employers are allowed to insure out of it, and sell their liability.—Yours, &amp;c., A WORKINGMAN. Earlestown, September, 1893.</p>
        <p>SOUTII LANCASHIRE NEEDLEWORK GUILD. Sir,—As your paper is w'dely read in all the districts where the guild is in operation, I shall be so much obliged if you will allow me to be my vice-Presidents not to take any of their associates names off their lists, who from the exceptional distress this year, consequent, directivor indirectly,froni the coal strike,are unable to con tribute their usual two garments to the Guild wardrobe. To make up fir this deficiency I shall be most grateful if others more blessed with means will send the warm clothing, or join the Guild if they wish. Either I orany of the following vice-Presidents will be only too pleas( d to receive garments or fresh members :—Miss C.Brierley, Mrs. F. I.hnstone, Mrs. Jordan, Mrs. Whittenbury Kave, Mrs. A. D. Seton, MISS M. Winstanley, or Miss Watkins. The exhibition or distribution will takeplace at the Town Hall, Newton-le-willows, on November 4th, 3 to 6. Admission free. All work should be sent in by October 20th, to the vice-Presidents.—Yours faithfully, JOSEPHINE F. ROYSE, President S.L.N.G. Newton-le-Willows, Sept. 27.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>TERM "INCARNATION."</head>
        <p>THE TERM "INCARNATION."</p>
        <p>Sir.—Very recently in reading the Manchester " Guar- dian" I leave twice found the word "Incarnation." The first was a lecture by Lord Dunmore describing his journeyings in Central Asia" before a London audi- ence he said :—" And at the latter place. Hemis, the travellers were accorded the rare privileee of seeing and conversing with the incarnation who is a boy of eight years of age." In the other case it (incarnation) occurred in an address by Liberal Churchmen to Mr. Gladstone on Rome Rule in which the following passage occurs— " While the arguments on which that refusal is based w a want of faith in human nature which we hold to be deroganry to the doctrine Qf the incarnalon," As so few (comparatively) myself inclucltd dn not know the full metViing of tht Word, MI heing anxious not to re- main ignorant of it, it occurred to me that probably some- one of your readers would give a definition of it. I know there is mystery inseparable from the word ; hence the desire for enlightenment.—Yours, Scc, Lytnm, Sept. 28th, 1893.</p>
        <p>EX. 'FARMER.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>TIIE COAL CRISIS.</head>
        <p>TIIE COAL CRISIS.</p>
        <p>Sir,—The war has now been raging two months—how or when will it end ? It is a consummation devoutly wished for in many a home besides the collier's home. It has been an awful blunder,begunby the masters and con- tinned to a great extent by the men. Whether the out- rages committed at various collieries was the act of col- liers on strike or by some one else is scarcely an open question. The wrecking and burning of railway wagons and the destruction of coal, the burning of colliery en- gine houses and other buildings,stopping a pernping and other repairs have been done by same people said to be colliers, and it has not been contradicted. Featherstone was a blunder throughout—the unruly and mischievous assemblage. There was no riot it. is said.yet a bridge was burnt,the colliery offices were burnt and wrecked.the safe was smashed and the money taken, books and papers burnt, and yet there was no riot. Was it spontaneous combustion that caused this fire? It is said no stones were throwo,and yet policemen and soldiers were struck , with stone* and severely injured. Were they Eerolites ? No,sir, all this was done by mischievous hands. There is a certain percentage--a small one it may be—of mis- chievous men in every crowd, ready for mischief, reek- lees, careless of consequences, and destructive. The crowd at Featherstone numbered thousands, fifty, or at most a hundred such men could have done all the mis- chief done there. Part of the blunder was, that orderly and well-disposed men were present at all ; they would have done better for themselves and their cause to have kept away, but when atithnrities.maeistrates, chief con- stables, and the like, lose their heads, workingmen can scarcely he blamed for losing theirs. Here is where the chief part of the blunder lies—there was no magistrate present as there should have been, before the soldiers came, to speak to the men and warn them of the conse- quences of their unlawful proceedings. The magistrate told off for that clutv,it seems, backed out, and for hours there was tumult and unrestrained disorder and no re- cognised authority to direct the civil and military force present, to quell the lawlessness and disorder prevailing, until exasperation,provonatien,and assault had done its work and men were shot down to death and others maimed for life through official negligence, and want of common sense arrangement. An inquiry searching and thorough must be held, and the blame for the death and maiming of these men he placed on the right shoulders. Lancashire and Cheshire can be congratulated on the absence or outrage and disorder as far as the miners on strike are concerned, and it is earnestly to be hoped that other districts will follow their example. In the mean- time poverty and destitution stalks through the land. Thousands who have no part in the strike are thrown Over two thousand men are out of work in Open.haw alone, Whitworth's; werks nd Ashlnrry's railway nlart and ironworks,eontritaitieg at least fifteen hundred of the quota. The M.S. and L. Railway Works with Beyer and Peacock's engineering, establishment are all in tl same Stagnant condition,while a pall of poverty and d' l'ress; hangs ever the district which the sufferers; can- not remove. Surely it can he removed if those who are resnonsibie for it would try P—l am, sir, yours respect.</p>
        <p>CYMRO.</p>
        <p>re t deal bag la...en said and written upon the alsove subject durin the last eight or nine weeks. Yet I hope the few thoughts passing through my mind may</p>
        <p>not he out of place if I put them in writing at this I period oi the tzreat strike.</p>
        <p>I may say in the first place that I, like many more, am an outside observer of what is going on. This is a strike which affects the whole nation and the whole nation more or less is suffering through it. Now I would ask this question : Did the colliery proprietors really believe for a moment that the men would entertain the idea of submitting to such a reduction as 25 per cent in their wages ? I do not believe that they did, nor have I yet seen anything to justify such a sweeping reduction. The coal for liwusehold purposes has been the same for several years --Best coal 15s. 10d. per tow delivered in Warrington. I may say that I am not alone in my belief that the colliery masters wanted—or at least many of them—an opportunity for disposing, of the enormous quantity of coal and slack that has been accumulating• for some time at their respective collieries and which has been disposed of at famine prices against the purchaser. I have no doubt that many colliery proprietors have reaped a rich harvest in this way. I would not say all, for there are some of them that have not had large stocks by them, so that they have not been so fortunate as others. . _</p>
        <p>And then, again, there are what are called the middle- men, or what are calltd coal merchants. And what I wish to say about them does not refer to this class of persons in our town only, but in other towns as well. Now most of these people hav€ been storing up thousands of tons of coal, preparing for this sort of thing we are now passing through—for we are getting these strikes periodically—and as soon as the colliers cease work up goes the price of coal, though the middlemen have not paid any t•xtia for it At this time it is being sold for almost double its worth. The class of persons who suffer most are the very poor. These have not been able to lay in a quantity of coal to tide over the crisis, and there are thousands of thousands of this class that do not know how to raise the money to purchase coal. In our own town thousands are living on clarity. Were it not for the benevolent amongst us they would be actually starving, while the middlemen are growing rich. They call this speculation, enterprise—forethought. I call it greed—selfishness—grinding the face of the poor. I for one don't envy them, but leave them to a higher person. _ _ _</p>
        <p>- ' And now, to come to a close, I sincerely hope that as the masters were the first to commence this struggle by giving the notice of such a sweeping reduction I hope they will he first to approach the men in a conciliatory spirit, and I hope wise counsels will prevail and a speedy settlement of this unhappy struggle will be brought about. In the meantime I hope that the con- sideration for the poor that has been so Ii berally shown will be continued, and that those who have it in their power will remember the words : " It is more blessed to give than to receive."—Thanking you in anticipation for inserting this—l remain yours, &amp;c., Warrington, Sept. 28, 1893.</p>
        <p>OBSERVER.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>VACCINATED SMALL-PDX--OUR MEDICAL WITNES6ES.</head>
        <p>VACCINATED SMALL-PDX--OUR MEDICAL WITNES6ES.</p>
        <p>Sir,—The medical journals frequently, but uninten- tionally, furnish evidence of the uselessness of vaccina- tion in warding off small-pox, and it is only the blind preju,lice of the medical profession that prevents them seeing what a serious blunder they are making by con- tinuing the Jennerian prescription. The " Laneet "of August 28h, (P.P. 485488, contains a communication from Dr. J. Guest Gornall. " On some points in the diagnosis of small-pox as illustrated in the Warrington epidenic," which presents an object lesson of this kind. Dr. Gornall mentions four eases of small-pox with one vaeeina'ion mark,five with two marks, three with three m; r zs, and two with four marks. He also cites the cases of four nurses (probably all re-vaccinated) as hav- ing been infected in the Warrington hlspital and removed to the local smatl-pox hospital. One case was that of a boy, aged four, with four foveated cicatrices of a little more than one square inch, (included in the two with four marks), concerning which Dr. Gornall writes :—" The failure of so excellent and recent vaccination to prevent small-pox entirely may conceiv- ably be ascribed to the special exposure to the poison of the disease. The child was in the same ward with confluent cases in all stages." This statement confirms what anti-vaccinators have all along maintained, that vaccination only protects when there is no small- pox, and fails when brought in contact with that disease Another case was that of "a strong healthy man with vaccination marks of good quality on both arms ;" this unfortunate patient was vaccinated twice unsucces,fully whilst in the hospital,the doctor not appearing to know exactly whether he had small-pox or no. He was dis- charged in a fortnight but returned in a few days with an attack of small-pox about which there was no mis- take. Two unvaccinated cases are mentioned, one a youth of 17 with discrete small-pox, the other a child of five with a severe attack of confluent small-pox who recovered.</p>
        <p>Dr. Gornall says :—" The greater number of cases of mistaken diagnosis with which I have had to deal have been in patients suffering from varicella.' With respect to thisstatement may I quote the weighty words of Dr. Josef Hermann, Chief of the Imperial Wide Hospital, Vienna. from 1852 to 186%, who sacs: —" When a man has treated hundreds of cases of smallp:,x both under sporadic and epidemic conditi4ns, through many years and at all seasms, he comes to the deci•led con- clusion that vaccination has not the remotest effect on the outbreak, course, or issue of the disease. Vaccinated persons, bearing unmistakable marks of the process on their arms, frequently have confluent smallpox ; while, at the same time, unvaccinated people have it in the mildest form. These observations, resting upon the most indubitable truth, appear to have given occasion to the distinction between a genuine and a modified small- pox (varicella)—a distinction evidently made with the view of maintaining at all hazards the dogma to which vaccinators have committed themselves, as to the influence of vaccination in modifying the outbreaks of the epidemic. These clinical subtleties, belonging to the category of chimeras, have long since proved to be illusory ; there is no such distinction, for, even in a mild epidemic of varicella a careful observer will be able to discover amongst the sufferers fully developed pustules of smallpox, possessing every attribute of the true and genuine pustule." . .</p>
        <p>The case against vaccination is amply demonstrated by the reluctapt, but damaging, admissions of its medical supporters ; and the widespread mistrust of the profession, by a large and increasing portion of the public, is due to their advocscy of those cm.; monster;, vaccination arid vivisection.—Yours faithfully, _</p>
        <p>. TAMES R. WILLIAMSON. 21, denrqe-street, Euston Square, London, S.W. 28ch September, 1893.</p>
        <p>TIIE BREWSTER SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>Sir,—lt cs--i'n-oid-t.ru-i-srn-ti;a-t-laiesie-av is he that. expecteth nothing, for he shall not be disappointed. This saying fitly represents the faith I had as to what would happen at the Adjourned Licensing Court. I eXpected that the Warrington magistrat.s would not move a finger to lessen the drunkenness of the town,and my expectation has been verified. So saturated with Greeneßiser and Walkerism are the bulk of these magnates that we might as well expect the Ethiopian to change his skin, or tha leopard his spots, as to hope for any reform of the liquor traffic at their hands. It is ! true, indeed, that they made a great fiodrish of trumpets about dosing all the back doors of the public-houses under their jurisdiction, and went so far in this direction as to Causes great deal cf labour and annoyance to our worthy Chief Constable and his excellent men in getting up a report upon these doors. But the labouring moun- tain only brought, forth a mouse, and a very small one ton. All the back doers remain oven as bafore with one solitary exception on the premises of the Lion Hotel. And the shutting up of that one was owing mainly, if net altogether, to the persistent efforts of a private citizen to save himself and his family from an abomin- Able nuisance. The magistrates and the publicans, however, came to an understanding that the latter were to he good lads for the future and do no trade through ! the forbidden doors. The farcical nature of this-arrange- r /pent will only be too obvious ti all who will take the trouble to observe the condition of the public-houses aral the streets on the Saturday and Sunday nighta. The question of the "tied houses "does not appear to have entered into the magisterial mind when they were performing their functions, and in this they were guilty of a gross neglect of duty. The law requires that the keeper of a public-house should be the bona fide owner or tenant of the premises. reside therein, and be responsi- ble for the proper conduct of his house. If this dictum be correct how came it to pass that the licensee mana- gers of the tied houses of Greenall's and Walker's were not called before the bench and required to produce genuine proof of their tenancy, and if they had no such profit to offer to have their licenses cancelled. The fear and dread of the big brewers was upon their worships, and so they lent themselves once more to the scandalous evasion of the law which enables monopolist drink makers to hold any number of bogus licenses through their barmen, to the great detriment of the legal publican and the general public. This neglect of a grave and important duty affords another proof that our " great unpaid" are unfit for the position they hold. I did not expect that the Bench would make any re- - duct:on in the number of the drink shops in the town, and I have not been disappointed. With a full know- ledge of the deplorable condition of multitudes of the in- dustrial classes ; anA with an equally full knowledge of the fact that most of the want and wretchedness Of these unfortunate people is caused by their drinking habits, the magistrates sat in solemn conclave and renewed every on and off license in the borough. Before taking this important step one weuld have supposed that they would have carefully examined and considered whether the requirements and wishes of the people demanded it. But they did nrthing of the sort. It was enough for them to know that the pnblicans want-d fresh leaeee to debauch and impoverish the public, and so renewals had only to be asked for in order to be granted. And, will it he believed, some of the maeistrates who fie.ered on the bench are to be found making sneeches and otherwise takieg a very prominent, part in the Soup Kitchen movement? Let nae impress a little wholesome trutO on the minds of these good men. They know, or ought to know, that public soup kitchens and other kin- dred devices are a scaedal to our boasted civilisation, and a sure way to degrade and pauperise the people who frequent them. The working classes of this country</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>EXAMINER, SA TIT 1) A Y SEPTEM BER 30, 1893. LA.BOR DAY IN AMERICA.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SA TIT 1) A Y SEPTEM BER 30, 1893. LA.BOR DAY IN AMERICA.</p>
        <p>have created all its wealth ; and if they had only their just share of that wealth, and were delivered from the bondage of the public house there would be no need for the cheap and ostentttious philanthropy of grasping ' manufacturers and others in times of trade dislocations. Even as things are, it is not the sober and industrious workers who are to he found receiving doles at the ,giup kitchens ; most of these people would prefer death ti such a degradation of their mantireod. It is only the men who spend a large portion of their earnings in the abomina bit. liquids served up to them by brewers, that clamour for bread and soup in such times as these. Having impoverished themselves and their families tri) enrich the publicans they must either starve or become pauperised by their benevolent employers and other good people.—Yours truly, lONA.</p>
        <p>reduce the cost of production. The solidarity of !labour skilled, a d unskilled must be the one thing aimed at. A worker who is a trade unionist from principle, and not from selfish motives will not be a bargain hunter He will not seek a ch ap shoe store, for the :sake of saving a shilling, without knowing leontething about the conditions udder which the ' shoes were produced. He will not go to a cheap slop shop for his clothing, without knowing some- thing about the place where the clothes were made and the price paid for the work done. He will take care to instil into hip wife's mind, the facttitat cheap jackets, underlirsen, and shirts, &amp;c., are two often but the work of one who has sewn with a double thread a shroud as well as a shirt. It is the demand for cheap goods that is responsible, to:a r large extent, for starvation wages. The victims of the sweater, are really the victims of the consumer. A word as to labour bureaus. From my experi- ence of them in this country I am somewhat atraid that their establishment in a small town like War- rington will be somewhat of a difficulty. They are open to great abuse, unless under the control of a Federated Trades Council, or Sec. In each town a man is required whose whole time is given up to organizing labour in all its phases. A man who thoroughly understands the labour question, and knows the difference between stirring up discord, and keeping peace. Such a man will have a central office recognized by labour and capital alike, not merely recognized, but in friendly and harmonious touch with all the live interests of the town. Ile will keep a list of all applications made by em- ployers and workmen. He will use some judgment so as to avoid putting square men into round holes. He will see that applications by employers for men are not mere subterfuges for getting hold of cheap labour, or tofu' up vacancies where disputes are on. He will avoid favouritism, and by keeping himself above reproach gain the confidence of all around him. The political leanings of such a man would be the greatest stumbling block in nis appointment. I would suggest the futility of trying to prevent cream from rising to- the stop. The Conservative working-man has only to study the labour question ,of the past fifty years, and he will surely leave the region of skimmeri milk. The lalnur leaders of England are a body of men to be proud of, and it is nothing to be surprised at, that almost tb a man they are Radicals and tempetance men. But a representative labour leader need not, necessarily, be selected on account of his politics. The best all round man is wanted, but he should be a temper- ance MEM. Worcester, Mass., U.S.A. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>No, I cannot say that I have been exhilarated by the last two matches played by Warrington, and I have yet discovered none of that sort of brilliance in Saturday's game which is calculated from certain physiological in- fluences to permeate the brainal labyrinths of a man's head, and induce him to prance about, singing a hymn of praise to the team and emphasizing it by clashing together a salmon tin and a " spanner." The first competition match with Bratughton Rangers lost, and then another loss with Stockport on Monday—it doesn't look well. Now, I earnestly hope that this season is not going to be quite so discouraging as the last. You know I should like to point out to the players that when they lose serious things happen to their admirers—besides losing bets. Fr instance last year a friend of mine was attacked like a well-known author once was. His mind got so affected that he fell to swindling himself and was often known to steal things from one pocket with one hand, and hide them in the other pocket with the other hand. He was almost locked up for robbing himself. This should be a warning.</p>
        <p>For seven long years says the "Athletic News" Broughton Rangers have been filled with the burning desire to do for Warrington, but the fates have been un- propitious. They have been well beaten (not the fates) by Warrington, and they have been permitted to make draws, but victory has never been their lot. however, it is a long lane which has no turning, and the much sought for victory came at list, and now songs of glad- ness are permeating the balmy air of Vl'heater's Field for this, the first victory—and in a League game, teo—over the sturdy lads of Warringou. Both clubs decided to do away with appeals, the sole power of stopping the game being left to the discretion of the referee, with the result that the game was far more enjoyable, and pos- sessed greater fascination to the spectators.</p>
        <p>Songs of gladness !--Ali, where are these which were to have been sung here? Unsung, unwritten, and not needed! But never mind, I have faith in the "old dog yet" so to speak. We cannot always enjoy victory, and we must only expect our fair share. Still lam inclined to think that perhaps the game on Saturday might not have been lost quite so completely as it was. In the first place I agree with a popular opinion that the game ought to have been fought in four quarters. It was rather in- discreet on the part of the Warrington captain to have allowed his men to play 40 minutes against the stiff breeze that was blowing. In the first place it was enough to simply guard, even indifferently, the visitors stronghold against the borne men helped by such a wind. But in addition to that it meant that Warrington afte r their exertions during this 40 were completely "blown " and unable to take advantage of favourable position in the second half.</p>
        <p>There was a distressing absence of " go " and com- bination in the visitors ranks during this second half ; the forwards were " playe I out," and the backs were accordingly of little worth. And it is said that to see how the forwards were rushed by the Rangers' nine, was a sight to make the gods weep. But I cannot tell how the truth may le; I say the tale as't was said to me.</p>
        <p>James Bate's performance, however, was watched with admiration'. His defence was brilliant, and throughout the whole garne he was incessantly at work repelling advances. Massey and Boscow played excellent games whilst Turner's goal was balm to the soul ; it was like the result of an inspiration.</p>
        <p>The results of the competition up to Saturday are here appended :</p>
        <p>rld. Won. Lost. GT. G.T. Pta. For. Ag,t. Oldham 1 . 1 . 0 0 9 1 ... 0 1 ... 2 Swinton 1 . 1 . 0 0 . 3 2 ... 0 0 ... 2 Broughton Rangers 1 ... 1 ... 0 ... 0 ... 2 1 ... 1 0 ... 2 Tvldesley Wigan Salford ,Bochdale. Hornets "Barrow Warrington Broughton 1 . 0 1 . 0 . 0 1 9 1 ... 0</p>
        <p>Then comes another defeat to acknowledge. Warring. ton lost in the match with Stockport on Monday evening. The game was Hayed at Stockport before a large crowd. The half-time scose was Warrington one try ; Stockport nothing. The full time score was Stockport two goals, Warrington one try ! Oh: the blessul uncertainty of things. James Bate,Boscow, and Potts played an excel- lent game, but of course their efforts did not prevent the defeat.</p>
        <p>The Warrington A team achieved a capital victory on Saturday, over the second string of the Broughton Ran- gers. When it was known that there were no less than six substitutes in the team, and among the absentees were the captain, H Teare, and Westall, a repetition of the previous week's match was expected, but fortu- nately these fears were agreeably disappointed. The Warrington team had the best of play, though per- haps not to the extent that the score of two goals and a try to a try would indicate. At the °nisei it was feared that Warrington would only play 13 men, but just after the game began, the necessary number was made up, the full back Hamblett, however, appearing in his ordin- ary pants, and he cut a rather peculiar figure in conse- quence.</p>
        <p>Among the players. Barnes was the most prominent. He gave abundant evidence that he has plenty of foot- ball in him yet, and I rather fancy he would have been an acquisition to the first team which was receiving a "little surprise" at Broughton. Barnes was by far the best player on the field, and also exhibited his skill in kicking goals. Lally who captained the team else main- tained his reput-ition as a scorer as two out of the three tries were obtained by him, the third try being obtained by Lawless, who also played extremely well all through the game. The Rangers were somewhat handicapped by their full back's play. During the former part of the game he was a downright failure,and continually mulled the ball. Towards the close of the game however, his play was of the opposite character and he made some daring saves, and had he been a good runner would undout tedly have scored. The Rangers had a capital pair of half backs, and taking their team all round, it is rather surprising that they were so well beaten.</p>
        <p>The Warribgton Rangers fresh from their victory at Chester the previous week played their first match on their own ground this season in Lovely-lane on Saturday afternoon. The field is an excellent one for football and the Rangers, plume themselves upon having the best junior football field in the town., Bedford Leigh were their opponents on Saturday, and the fact that they completed their fixture card last season without once tasting defeat; and also that they had already beaten the Rangers rivals—St. Mary's Ist—a good game was anticipated. The match came up to expectations, as the teams proved to be very evenly matched. In the first half with the wind in their favour, the Raagers managed to get over the line once, and this proved to be the winning point, as in the second half nothing tangible was scored by either team. The game throughout was evenly contested and the Bangers were gratified at hav- ing lowered the Redford colours.</p>
        <p>From what I can hear the match between St. Mary's and Walker's Brewery Football Clubs developed into a " free fight "due to the interference of the spectoOrs. This is to be regretted as it takes away all the pleasant- ness of the game and cannot be called football. At least I think it cannot be called so. At the return en- Raftement on the Saint's ground a few weeks hence it is to be hoped that they will let bygones be bygones and that a repetition of Saturday's broil will not occur. From the score it appears thaf the Brewery team " weren't in it" as the Saint's came out victors by two tries five minors to three minors.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>"DOGS OF WAR."</head>
        <p>THE "DOGS OF WAR."</p>
        <p>The " Dogs of War " have really been slipt. During the late German Army Manceuvres, live trained dogs— two Scotch rind theee German sheepdogs.—were put on trial and acquitted themselves satisfactorily. They carried despatches from outpost to head quarters and vice versa, with blth soeed and certainty, distinguish- ing themselves especially by night. As searchers for wounded men their keen scent and instinct were particu- larly valuable. The Scotch dngs. as was anticipated. display the greater fidelity, while the German trio excel in speed, one of the latter batting covered nearly two miles ig seven minutes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>EXT RAO R DINAItY AFFAIR AT • " – HEY WOOD.</head>
        <p>EXT RAO R DINAItY AFFAIR AT • " - HEY WOOD.</p>
        <p>BOYS DECOYED AND MALTREATED.</p>
        <p>Two boys named CoHinge and Crabtree on Wednes- day mysteriously disappeared from their hotnes at Hey- wood. and at throe o'clock this morning Crabtree was found in a wood on the outskirts of the town in a most piti ible condition. He could give no intaligible account ot what had happened, hot subsequently the dead body .of Collinge was found in the river. It is believed the boys were decoyed into the wood and maltreated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>TRADES UNIONISM.</head>
        <p>TRADES UNIONISM.</p>
        <p>(BY MR. H. WHITHAM.)</p>
        <p>It may interest readers of the " Examiner" to know that the American wotkinan has at least one day in the year that he may call his own. It is not exactly a legal holiday, but it is fast becoming a well recognized institution, and workmen lobk for- ward and prepare for the first Monday in Sept. with confidence that no impediments will be placed in the way of their holding It as a national holiday. There is quite a difference between the Trades Unionism of America, and that of England. Until recent years English unionists have given little thought to display and parade. There has been a sort of feeling that trade unionism was something to be kept in the back ground, as if it was a shame- ful thing to act in conjunction with one's fellows in making the wage bargain. There is something in hereditary tradition that, is .hard to throw off. Truly, the English workman, in being the heir of all the ages, inherits a deal that is bad and cum- b”rsome. He inherits a State Church system that compels him to support a' parson with whom he has nothing in common. Be inherits a drinking system that invites him to skulk in and out of a back door, and so leaves the front door clean for respectables. He inherits a heap of -written and unwritten land laws, the outcome of which is as peasantry driven off the land to help to swell great cities so that as Dr. Jeffrey says: • He who bends the ear low enough to listen may hear the ground swell of humanity which at no distant state may burst into a storm." The English workman also in- h'erits the right to get coal at Is. 6d. a ton so that the colliery owner may be enabled to pay a royalty exceeding his wage to the land owner. Ile also in- herits the right to fight for the British Empire so that landlords in Ireland may drive Irish workmen into English towns to add to the horrid struggle now going on for the right to live. We have a few glorious privileges ht.re in the States, which smack not a little of the above, and go to prove we of the Saxon race find great difficulty in wriggling out of the skin our fore- father's kindly provided for us. We recognize individuals and alien ownership of land to the extent of allowing the Marquis of Sweedale to own nearly two million acres, of land; the Duke of Sutherland 428,000 acres, and several English. and Scotch companies own quite a number of millions of acres besides. Thus it will be seen that we shall soon have quite a respectable number of absentee landlords. I am not much disturbed about this. I think I see signs that the reign ot the land- owners, as we know it is nearer its end. In Eng- land and in America the landowner seems to be doing his utmost to goad the people to desperation. This cannot be done much longer, Germany with all its turbulent socialism, seems to be in a fair 1, way of escaping some of the troubles England and America are doomed to meet.</p>
        <p>It is not much of a jump from the landowner to the trade unionist. The former may think be has not much in common with the latter, but the intelligent trade unionist keeps his eye right on the land. He knows that it is no use telling him he has the right to live, without also giving him the right to acquire, and possess that which is necessary to support life. When labor day comes round the American Unionist calls to hie aid the local congressman, such business men as he can get, members of religious bodies, municipal organization, and members of beneficial orders. This he does because of his well-founded belief that his prosperity is se indissoluably" connected with that of the community that he being well-to- do, everybody else is likely to be as orell. I sometimes wonder what sort-of a'place America would have been had there been no foreign element introduced amongst its labourers. The native American workman is rarely a trade Unionist. Thoroughly saturated with selfishness himself, he allows the same as a right to his employer. There- fore be will not join the Union, for, says he, I expect to be an employer myself some day, and I shall allow no body of men to interfere with thy business. In all my experience in California as a member of the Federated Trades Council I found but two of the delegates, out of about three hundred, who were American born. Since I came to New England I have not come across one native who will even I listen to a trade union argument. The trade union- ist is a free trader, the non-unionist is a protec- tionist. I think there is a lesson in the above well I worth finding out. We often hear it said that "A man has a perfect right to sell his labour when, where, and bow he pleases." Trade unionism dis- tinctly challenges this position, and it does so on the highest ground of religion, and ethics. "Thou shalt love thy neighbour as thyself." If these were not vain and empty words, if. as Paul said," we are children of God, and if children then heirs," then no man has a right to make present self-preservation the first law of his nature. As Profesror Sidgwick says :—"The free pursuit by individuals of their own exclusive interest will not lead to universal wellfare," of course the old economists, and employerf of the dying Manchester school, hold up "Freedom of Contract" as something to swear by. We are learning better; we know there can be no freedom of Contract between a wealthy and power- ful employer, and a starving man, With a wife and family behind him. We know there can be no i freedom of contract between the drunken non' unionist, and his employer. Freedom of contract there never has been. True it is, as Professor Clark has said, that competition wholly without moral restraint has never existed. If competition were supreme it would be supremely immoral • if it existed otherwise than by sufferance, it would be a demon." Of course lam no advocate for physical interference with individual action, simply urging that as unionists we have a perfect right to insist that our unions be recognized, and that our action, short of physical force, shall not be looked upon as inimical to the nation's welfare. Can anyone imagine what the condition of the English workingman would have been to-day, had he not have had the union behind him P Thorold Rogers in " Work and wages" tells us that while the " Arkwrights and Peels and a multitude more were building up solossal fortunes on the misery of, labor, men were working sixteen and eighteen hours a day, and earning in those desperate times from 59. to 7s. a week. There were no laws to re- strict freedom of contract; there were no trades- unions, or if any timidly ventured into being, they were ruthlessly stamped out by law, supply and demand were the only regulative forces. To the action of Trades Unions we owe all the meliorating influences which have worked, and are working out the redemption of the workers. Two of M ease- chussetts largest employers have recently spoken out strongly in favour of unions., One, Mr. Means, the largest employer in the shoe trade, says, " no matter how liberal-minded an employer may be, he nannot raise wages, because he has to sell his goods in competition with other employers who are crowding wages down. The only thing that work- men can do is to combine. If they suffer without resistance the op••ration of the economic forces. they will be degraded and destroyed-r if they stand together for mutual protection they tatty be saved Mr. Lindenberg another large employer, says : "To place all upon the same level and to Overcome the general cupidity, the employer must be aided in his desire to do justice to the laborer by a more potent force than public opinion. 'The laborers themselves must create it bk well directed mutual effort, by combination within the law." "You see," he says that the remedy cannot come from the employers, therefore, I say, it must come from those employed. therefore,l declare that the remedy must come from the organization of labor in trades unions, for labor must be its own champion and right its own wrongs, labor must combine and see to it that its power to purchase its own products is not taken away from it." It is from the deep degradation into which unhin- dered competition plunged the English labouring classes that combination has lifted them, Thorohl Rogers goes so far As to say that he " would limit the privileges of citizenship, the franchise, parlia- mentary and local, to those, and those only, who entered into these three guilds,—the guild of labour the guild of production. and trade cc-operation, and the guild of mutual help." The Duke of Argyle in his " Reign of Law" says—" When the working classes combine tor the protection of their own labour against the effects of unrestrained competi- tion, they are simply taking that course which is recommended alike by reason and experience." Thus we pee that combination must gradually sup- plant competitiOn. It must always be remembered that capital will not arbitrate with a foe. that it can crush. Strong combinations on the side of labour are the necessary conditions of arbitrations. Arbi- tration will he the final term of the wage system, determining what h. man should get not what he can get. We must never forget that it is not the honest and straightforward employer who fixes the price of labour, it. i,invarialY,the dishonest and unscru- pulous employer who seeking to push his goods in the market, resorts to all manner of methods to</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>WARRINGTON -COUNTY COURT.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON -COUNTY COURT.</p>
        <p>TEURSDAY.—Before Deputy-Judge Crawford. A Perigwru.Buir.mia's bUlT.—flenry Cragg, joiner and builder, Penketh, sued Thomas Jones,chemical labourer, Barrows Green, Widnes, for £5 Bs. for work done.—Mr. Forshaw represented plaintiff, and Mr. Arthur Browne appeared for defendant.—Mr.Forshaw stated that Cragg was introduced to the defendant in May last; and the latter told him he was going to build two houses at Pen- keth,and asked him to send in an 'estimate for doing the joiner's work. Au estimate for £29 14s. was sent in,and defendant told plaintiff to get out the foundations; and put a bricklayer to work at once. He also told him to take his instructions from his (plaintiff's) brother-in-law —Mr. Owen. Mr. Owen afterwards accompanied plain- tiff to the site selected, and plaintiff marked out the foundations. Plaintiff and several witnesses having corroborated the above statement, Mr. Browne (for the defence) deniad that defendant accepted the tender. He contended that the prices were exorbitant, and that the work should not have taken so long. All that the defen- dant was justified in paying was Is. 81. for the making of a templet for a bay window and 24.8 d. for marking out foundations, and these sums had been paid into court.— Judgmeet was given for plaintiff fur three guineas. A DRAPER'S Crant.—John S. Turnbull, draper, Man- chester, sued James Bennett of Warrington, for £2, the balance owing on a debt for a suit of clothes supplied by the plaintiff six years ago.—Mr. A. Browne who ap- peared for the plaintiff intimated that the case was ad- journed from July in consequence of the defendant denying any knowledge of the plaintiff, and denying_ having had any business transactions with him, and also because the plaintiff was not then in court.—Plaintiff was now present and said that he supplied the suit of clothes to the defendant, and that previously he had done business with him for twenty years. Lie also produced a book showing the debit of the debt and also payments' which had been made until 1889. The defendant's wife in that year died and the instalments ceased to be paid' and the bill was handed over to Mr. John Snoddin to be collected. Mr. J. Snoddin gave evidence and proved. that in 1889 he received twelve instalments of one shif- ting each. The defendant denied having made these payments and also denied the debt. After some coesi- deration his Honour said there was not a sufficiency of proof against the defendant and accordingly gave judg- ment for him, but refused to grant an order for costs. DISPUTE ABOUT COMPENSATION.—George Ilankinson, 3, off Factory-yard, Bank Qaay, sued John Newton, John-st., confectioner, for £2 15s. balance owing upon an agreement in which defendant had signed for the payment of £5 from injuries sustained by the plaintiff by baing run over by the defendant's cart. Mr. Browne for the plaintiff said that his client was proceeding along Liverpool-road, upon New Year's Eve, 1890. He was overtaken by the defendant's cart and was knocked down. He was taken home and remained in bed seven or eight weeks: being indisposed from work bythe inju- ries he had received. Upon his recovery he, accompanied by his wife and daughter v isited the defendant and asked him what he intended to do in relation to the acci- dent. After some consideration the defendant under- took to sign an agreement paying plaintiff £5, which sum the plaintiff agreed to accept as some compensation for the wages he had lost and the medical treatment he had incurred. Mr. Longton who appeared for the defendant contended that the defendant simply agreed, to pay the £5 in case the doctor's bill amounted to £lO, and as this was not the case he was justified in not ful- filling the agreement. He bad paid half of the £5, however, and as it had been agreed that his broith4 was to pay the remaining half he maintained that his client was not liable for the amount claimed. The Judge gave judgment for the plaiptiff, to be paid at ten shillings per month.</p>
        <p>A CHEMIST'S ASSISTANT'S tIABILITY.—John Plinston, tailor, Warrington, sued Charles John Chamberlain, manager to Mr. Alfred Ashton, chemist, Sankey-st., for qhe cost of a suit of clothes. Plaintiff •tated that the defendant came to him and asked him if he would make a suit of clothes for a youth named Clarke, who was then assisting him, and if he would let him have them on credit. Plaintiff agreed to, but only on the under- standing that the defendtnt would accept the responsi- bility of the deht.—Mr. A. Browne for the defence denied thaaThis client had accepted any responsibility, and said he had simply recommended Clarke to the plaintiff.— Judgment was given for the plaintiff. CLAIM AGAINST • A SHOPKEEPER.—Jahn Bateman, Steckport, assignee of the book debts of Messrs. Sexton, Brothers, of Warrington and Widnes, sued John Shake- shaft, Warrington for £6 41. lid. balances, owing for groceries supplied and delivered. Mr. Bateman. who cOnducted his own case was unable to prove the assign- ment., and his Honour ultimately allowed that to stand over and hear the case on its own merits. Mr. James Sexton swore to delivering and supplying the goods, but Mrs. Shakeshaft and her husband both denied having received them: Mr. Browne who appeared for the de- fence said that the defendant was a very respectable shopkeeper and was in the habit of paying cash down in all his dealings with Messrs. Sexton Bros: He produced receipts for goods tieceived at various times to bear this oat. His Honour gave judg,embnt for the defendant.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES-WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES-WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>THE P.S.#:_AT WYCLIFF:E CFIURCH.</p>
        <p>There are now 890 members of the Wycliffe P.S.A., and the attendance of r. embers on Sunday afternoon was thelargest since the inauguration of the movement in Warrington. The chair was occupied by Mr. Roff, and in course of a short address he referred to the evils of intemperance. Mr. Forrest, J.P., of Manchester was the speaker for the afternoon and he delivered an excellent address on 'Character, and the building up of the same." Mr. W. H. Wallington was the soloist and he sang two solos in his usual excellent manner. The meeting was also taken part in by the Rev. John Yonge, Messrs. A. Birch and T. Brown, Mr. W. H. Payton ãa the accompanist. A movement is on foot to commence an ambulance. class for the eoming winter, and the benevolent fund has been the means of relieving some of the members in distress caused by the coal strike. HILL CLIFFE HAPTIT CHAPEL.</p>
        <p>The autumnal .;erviccs in connectinn with Hill Cliffe Baptist Chapel were held on Sunday afternoon and even- ing.. The preacher was. the Rev. D. Davies of Tarporley. Special hymns were sung for the occasion. There were large congregations at both services. The collections taken were in aid of tlie church funds and realised the satisfactory sum of £lB.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>ITALY AND THE,' VATICAN.</head>
        <p>ITALY AND THE,' VATICAN.</p>
        <p>SERIOUS DISAGREEMENT.</p>
        <p>ROMS, THURSDAY.—A dispute, which may have grave consequences, has arisen between the Pope and the Italian Government. The Pope having refused to recognise the right of the King of Italy to nominate the Patriarch of Venice.the It than GAvetntnent has resolved upon a step of the gravest importance, it being to-day announced that the Ministry has decided to refuse its execpiator to all of the bishops nominated at the last Papal Consistory, and to all those who may be nominated i at future Consiatories. This vigorous action on the part ot the Ministry has caused considerable excitement here, and crested a feeling bordering upon consternation in Catholic circler:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <p>THE FORTHCOMING TOWN COUNCIL MEETING.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Warrington Town Council will be held in the Council Clamber on Tuesday evening next at six o'clock, when the fol- lowing business will be considered. THE WATCH COMMITTEE'S" DIGNITY."</p>
        <p>The following resolution appears on the minutes of the Watch Committee That this Committee regret that the magistrates appe t to have acted with a want of courtesy by issuing hlstructions to and employing the Police without in any way consulting the Watch Com- mittee and that such instructions be not acted upon with- out first consulting this Committee.'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>NEW litmus, &amp;c.</head>
        <p>NEW litmus, &amp;c.</p>
        <p>The Street Improvement Committee has approved of the plans of the following buildipgs, :—Alterations at Gymnasium, Palmyra Square; house and shop in Orford-lane, for Mr. William Brookes ; alterations to property in Dial-street, for Mr. William Bostock ; addi- tton to houses in Longlord-street, for Mr. Hudson; new shed. High-street, for Messrs. Monks, Hall and Co. Ltd ; 9 houses in Knutsfurd-road, for Mr. John Lunt ' • 4 houses in Hanover-street, for Messrs. J. Toft and F. .Pendlebury.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>'PURCHASING LAND.-A STREET SIGN.</head>
        <p>'PURCHASING LAND.-A STREET SIGN.</p>
        <p>The Street Improvement Committee recommend that a strip of land in Liverpool-road be purchased from Mr. Joseph Tichett at 103. per square yard. The same COm- mittee has resolved that permission be given to Themes Allen and Sons to erect 3 sign in front of premises in Mersey-street not to exceed 4 feet from face of building, and not to be less than 16 feet above level of ground.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>HEALTH_ COMMITTEE_ MATTERS.</head>
        <p>HEALTH_ COMMITTEE_ MATTERS.</p>
        <p>The Health Committee has adopted a report of the Medical Officer in which a re-arrangement ef the duties of the inspectors of apisance is recommended. The Committee recommends that the sum of ten pounds be voted to Mr. Bloomfield for his special services during the recent epidemic r,f small-pox.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>PAVING AND SEWERAGE COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>PAVING AND SEWERAGE COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>This Committee has passed the following resolutions: —That about 50 trees be planted within the fence on the south or river side of the Stone Yard, Howley ; that an agreement be made with Mr. John White for the imme- diate possession of the land required for the new sewer for the Padgate district; and, that the sum of £7 10s. be paid 0 Mr. Norcott as compensation for interference with land in his occupation in Padgate-lane, and adjoin- ing the cemetery.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>SCHOOL COMMITTEE IMISINESS.</head>
        <p>SCHOOL COMMITTEE IMISINESS.</p>
        <p>From the report of the Inspector to the School Commit- tee it appeared that 25 rases had been taken before the magistrates, and fines inflicted in 22 cases, two boys sent to the Ship "Clio," and one boy to Father Nugent's Industrial School. 38 cases were brought before the Committee and dealt with.</p>
        <p>OIFTS TO THE MUSEUM. The Museum etc. Committee report the follow- ing gifts ;—Earth-balls, from Loch Eildonan, Hebrides, from Councillor Hepherd ; pocket ink horn and pounce box from Mr. John Peers; three copper and pewter tokens, found used as packing in the atone-work of War- rington Bridge from Mr. Douglas ; ancient dug-out canoe found in the bed of the Mersey at Lower Walton from the Manchester Ship Canal Company ; engraved portrait of Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., after Millais from Lady Greenall ; "Journal of the Iron and Stealnstitute," vol. 43 from the Institute; "Report on Small-pox," by J. G.,•Gornall from the Author; " Echoes " by Hogarth from the Monti • " Proceedings of the Literary and Philosophical Society" for 1882-3 (3 copies) from the Society ; Parliamentary rapers (22) from Mr. B. Pier- point, M.P. -------</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>CRMBIIIRT.</head>
        <p>THE CRMBIIIRT.</p>
        <p>From the report of the Registrar to the Cemetery Com- mittee it appeared that the interments for the OA four weeks were—Adults 40 ; minors 91; stillborn and pre- mature, 13. Corresponding period last rear—Adult:4 29 ; minors, 70; stillborn and premature, 11.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>BATHS.</head>
        <p>THE BATHS.</p>
        <p>From the report of the.Bathkeeper to the Baths Corn- mittee it appeared that the income from the baths for the last four weeks was £B4 2s. lOd., and for the corres- ponding period last year £29 14s. 2d. The Committee recommends that the well pumps at the Baths be sold to the Sanitary Works Committee for the sum of £5O.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>-PROPOSED NEW MUNICIPAL BUILDINGS.</head>
        <p>THE -PROPOSED NEW MUNICIPAL BUILDINGS.</p>
        <p>The lown Ball, etc. Committee ha resolved that the instructions to architects, and conditions for competition for the New Municipal Buildings be approved, and forwarded to the General Purposes Com- mittee for consideration.</p>
        <p>THE MARKETS.</p>
        <p>The Markets Committee report that the total income front all sources for the past four weeks was £342 18s. Bd. ; corresponding period last year, £334 3.55. 7d. ; increase, £8 3. id.</p>
        <p>Tin OAS COMMUTER.</p>
        <p>This Committee reports that the average illuminating power of the gas for the past four weeks was equal to 1826 candles at both works. The sub-committee report that on the 30th August they inspected the new gas hol- der recently completed at Longford works, and consi- dered it in every way satisfactory. The Committee recommend that the cottages it. Chrimes' Yard beoletno- 'Med.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>SHIP CANAL AND THE RIVER,</head>
        <p>THE SHIP CANAL AND THE RIVER,</p>
        <p>The Borough Surveyor reports t the Rivers Commit- tee that MikE. Leader Williams had written to him as foßows I have yours of the 7th instant, with copy of your report al to the dredging at Arplep.• This dredging has been somewhat delayed owing to its being -necessary to make some alterations to the dredger to enable it to work more satisfactorily under the special circumstances of the case. Two grabs are being put to work, and in addition to these, a new dredger has been purchased •at Belfast, which is also expected to be at work in about a week's time. I think the two dredgers, coupled with the grabs, will greatly expedite the work."—The Borough Surveyor adds :—" I have since visited the works, and find the dreciAing in the new river diversion is six feet below L. W. level and in other respects the dredging is not progressing satisfactorily. Good progress has been made with the Lock, the excavation being taseit out to the depth of the river bed. I have made 17 cross sec- tions of the river within the borough on the same cross section, lines taken by J. Wolfe Barry, Esq. C.E., in April, 1883, and find the channel has altered, and the river bed considerably silted up in various parts since last taken."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>TENDERS.</head>
        <p>TENDERS.</p>
        <p>Acceptance of the following tenders is recommended by the Committee named :—Sanitary Works Coml.- tee,—of Mr. J. Westgarth to supply two tons of hoot.) iron at £6 103. per ton, and two tons of hoop iron at £6 7s. 6d. per ton ; Paving and Sewerage Comrn!ttee—of Mr. W. Coates to supply 40 Scavengers' Slops at 7s. 10d. each ; of Mr. Frank Booth to supply 20 hats at 2s. 4d. each ; and the tender of Messrs. Hall and Son to supply 20 hats at 2s. 4d. each ; and of Mr. F. T. Bennie, for the forming and paving of Clara-st., at £l9B Bs. Bd., and Friars' Green at £B3 ai ld. ; Paving and Sewerage Com- mittee—of Messrs. Clare and Ridgway to supply 25 tons of cement at 34R. 6 I. per ton ; and the tender of Messrs. W. Chandley and Co. to supply 2b tons at 345. 6d. per ton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>NOTICE OF MOTION.</head>
        <p>A NOTICE OF MOTION.</p>
        <p>Councillor Shaw has given notice that he will move the following resolution : That the tenders for the paint- ing of the Market Ball and Shed be produced for examin- ation, and that an explanation be given as to why the lewest tender was not accepted..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>HARVEST FESTIVALS.</head>
        <p>HARVEST FESTIVALS.</p>
        <p>(Continued from page 3). WARRINGTON PARISH CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The harvest festival in connection with this church was held on Thursday, the preacher being the Rev. W. R. Wynter, vicar of Wellingborough. There was a good congregation, and the collection which amounted to £ll Os. 4d. will be devoted to the clergy fund. The de- corations are not of such aMextensive nature as in pre- vious years' but still they reflected great credit upon those who had the work of decorating in hand. The font was decorated by the Mipes White, the pulpit by Mrs. Willis and Mrs. Stevenson, the clergy stall by Mrs. Wood, Miss Cash, Miss White, and Miss Buckton, the chancel rails were decorated by Mrs. Percival and daugh- ters, and the communion rails by Mrs. Pairclough and daughters. The lectern' was beautified, by the Misses Burgess.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>ST. BASNABAS CHURbff.</head>
        <p>ST. BASNABAS CHURbff.</p>
        <p>The Harvest thanksgiving service in connection with the above church was held on Thursday night, when the preacher was the Rev. Dr. 01liver of Liverpool. The rev.gent leman took as the subject of his discourse,S.Luke 8, 15. The church was splendidly decorated, the pulpit being Aec)rated by Miss Short, the font by Mrs. Allton, and Miss Stewart, the large window by Mrs. Hassell, and the lectern by, Miss Langsden. There were also other lady helpers inclutling Mrs. McClew, Mrs. Roscoe, Mrs. Hight-, and Mrs. Looker. rue collections were in aid of the choir fund.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>PENKET.</head>
        <p>PENKET.</p>
        <p>Tun, TO SOUTHPOOT.—Saturday, although not an ideal day fora pleasure excursion wa the occasion of a trip to Southport, which was organized and paid for by Mr. Robert Garnett for the members of the Wesleyan Chapel Choir. Despite the somewhat obdurate character of the elements, the party bravely pressed their quest for en- joyment and were very successful., The party had tea at the residence of Mr. David Garnett, who had offered a , pressing invitation, and now gave a most cordial wel- come. When the party returned all could speak to hav- ing spent a go.ai time. TECHNICAL CLASSES.—Thesq classes have now cm- ' menced the first gathering being on Monday night. The subjects under con.irieration are chemistry, advanced and elementary, arithrm tic and mensuration. The in- structor is Mr. J. Woolrnan who so successfully taught the classes last session. Already a large number of 1 students joined, and more are intending to do so. Chemistry is held on Monday and Friday night, and arithmetic onMonday also.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-09-30_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>TEET•IC</head>
        <p>TEET•IC</p>
        <p>THE COAL-OWNERS' "PROPERTY."</p>
        <p>- OPINION OF THE LORD CHIEF JUSTICK. ens. f l It was reported a few days ago that a St.- i .--°- fil coal-owner had threatened "to have his Pi led t 8 '— : up rather than allow the men to go to work with. out the reduction." Such a declaration as 018 ; once, of course, raises the question as to the (Ate of the power of the coal-owners over the minerals node rI neath English soil. A correspondent of the Manche4et "Guardian" writes --In face of this truculent ded!all. tion it seems opportune to recall the words of the 1..0 Chief Justice cf England with reference to 'lsimilar _ claration made by a mineowner under similar circ.l?ar, stances. Lecturing before the Juridical Society in Lila' gow a few years ago, Lord Coleridge said : ~..,rpreil ' " A very large coalowner some years ago in"'''-, ill t with a high hand in one of his coal centres. 'He . for the workmen, he declined to argue, but he sal .1 stamping with his foot upon the ground,' All the oal: within so many square miles is mine, and if you ' instantly come to terms not a hundredweight of be brought to the surface, and it shall remain unworke": This utterance of his was much criticised at the dine_ By acme it was held up as a subject for panegyrical° ' model for imitation—the manly utterance of one css would stand no nonsense, determined to assert his rilgi _. of property and to tolerate no inference with thel!,., (Laughter.) By orders it was denounced as insolerel" and brutal, and it was suggested that if a few a°1,..., acted on them it would very probably result in the 05, witu. , owners having net much property left to interfere To me it seemed then, and does seem row, an inattc, of that density of perception and inability to see dig"; . %ions between things essentially distinct. I should self deny that the mineral treasures der the soil °I un - cue' country belong to a handful of surface proprietors In , „ sense in which this gentleman appeared to think Oat tc7 3 did. That fift) or a hundred gentlemen, cr a tnousria,, should have a right, by agreeing to shut the coal ma' w to stop the manufactures of Great Britain and paralyse her commerce seems to me, I must trankti unspeakably absurd."</p>
        <p>Oa' The smallest pmfessional child pianist in the recently made his debut in sp. Petersburg. The musician's name is Raoul Boezalski ; he is only live SorriZ old, and his legs are so short that his father lias vr°43. the pedals for him. Master Koezakki plat's onl v and his execution is said to be equai to that of the gre pianist himself. re, Mr. W. J. Peall, the champion "spat player," be'le 'Kt.,. that to play billiards well it is necessary to colmentf,4oo) attention on the game. He never looks about, he r' tol while playing an important match, or allows his esl' wander among the audience. When he moves rolloo the table to play his bail he keeps his eyes fixed 00 boots.</p>
